window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['poelitz/guyer'] = {"poelitz-s01-001":["Logic.",[]],"poelitz-s01-002":["Everything happens according to rules, in the world of bodies as well as in humanity. The exercise of our powers also happens according to certain rules and we proceed according to them although we are not conscious of them, but we arrive at cognition of them through attempts and through use. In this way we can learn much about ourselves by trying out. Gradually, through the longer use of our powers, we find this rule more familiar and finally to such a degree that it costs effort to abstract it — e.g. grammar is the form of a language in general. But one also speaks without learning grammar, i.e. one speaks according to rules of which one is not conscious. The understanding also acts according to rules, which we can investigate. It is the faculty to think, we can therefore not think except according to certain rules — the rules themselves we can again ourselves think without their investigation, i.e. *in abstracto* — to think properly means only to bring the representations of the senses under rules. Hence one also defines the understanding through the faculty of rules. The human being often gapes at objects, but he does not think, i.e. he does not bring his representations under rules. Understanding therefore also means the faculty of bringing representations of the senses under rules. Since, then, our understanding always acts according to rules: it is well worth our concerning ourselves with the knowledge of them, even though we have previously already made use of our understanding. The understanding is the faculty of rules (just as the senses are the faculty of intuitions) and it is eager to seek rules and satisfied when it has found them. Since it is now the source of rules, it is asked according to which rules the understanding itself proceeds. Of this the following",["Regel is rendered \"rule\" throughout; Kraft = \"power\"; Vermögen = \"faculty\"; Erkentniß = \"cognition\", Kentniß = \"knowledge\".","\"diese Regel ... sie\": the German has the singular Regel, and sie could be singular or plural; the English keeps \"this rule ... it\" (the plural \"rules ... them\" is possible for sie).","\"Vorstelhingen\" read as Vorstellungen (OCR slip); \"Sinnen\" (dative plural) and \"Sinne\" both rendered \"senses\" (Sinn).","\"gaft ... an\" (gaffen) rendered \"gapes at\"; \"bedient\" rendered \"made use of\" (sich bedienen).","\"Gegenstände\" here is \"objects\"; elsewhere in these passages \"Object(e)\" is also \"objects\" (Gegenstand / Object not distinguished in English)."]],"poelitz-s01-003":["All rules are either necessary, without which no correct use of the understanding is possible at all, or contingent, without which no determinate use of the understanding takes place. E.g. The rules of the use of the understanding in mathematics, metaphysics, morals, etc. are contingent rules, but those without which one would not think are necessary. The contingent rules, which depend on a determinate object of explanation, are as manifold as the objects. But because in every thinking there is understanding, which acts according to rules: there must be rules of thinking that are common to all thinking without distinction of objects, and that lie at the ground of every use of the understanding, without which it is not possible at all; and these are the necessary ones They must therefore contain merely the form of thinking.",["\"bestirnter / bestirnten\" read as bestimmter / bestimmten (OCR); rendered \"determinate\".","\"not h wendig\" and \"noth wendigen\" are broken words (notwendig), silently joined.","Erklärung is rendered \"explanation\"; the glossary also allows \"definition\", which is possible here.","Missing full stop after \"die noth wendigen\" is as in the text; Zufällig = \"contingent\", Grund = \"ground\", Gemein = \"common\"."]],"poelitz-s01-004":["In all our thinking there are 2 pieces: matter and form. —",["Stücke rendered \"pieces\"; Materie = \"matter\"."]],"poelitz-s01-005":["Some cognitions are distinguished according to matter, the same according to form. E.g. Physics and psychology are both empirical, accordingly a science that contains the general rules is merely a science of the form of the cognizing of our understanding, or of thinking where one abstracts from all objects and such a science we call a logic. — The word λόγος from which the word comes has various meanings, but here: understanding and reason. Thus logic will be a science of the form of understanding and reason in general. General logic must abstract from all objects. E.g. In all cognition there are concepts The logic therefore shows only the form of them without concerning itself with the matter. — We therefore have the plan for a science that contains the rules of the form of the understanding without distinction of objects, and such a science is directed to all thinking and is a foundation for other sciences. A science that prepares for others is called propaedeutic: logic is therefore the propaedeutic to the use of our understanding. The sum total of the rules *in concreto*, which we do not know *in abstracto*, is supposed to be natural logic, the artificial presents these rules in detail. This division of logic into the natural and artificial is not good, logic is supposed to be a science, but a science is a sum total of rules *in abstracto* and therefore presupposes an artificial cognition. — We must distinguish the logic of the common understanding from the logic of the speculative understanding. Understanding is in general the faculty of rules, the rules we can take either *in concreto* or *in abstracto*. The common understanding, *intellectus communis*, is the faculty of rules *in concreto*, the common understanding insofar as it is a correct one is called a healthy understanding, it is distinguished from the *vulgaris*, *vulgaris* is that which every human being has, but *communis* that which can be demanded of everyone, namely that he judge correctly. — The speculative understanding is the faculty of rules *in abstracto*. Only a scholar has this, and it can be demanded of no one who does not devote himself to sciences. The rules of logic are 2-fold",["\"Aoyos\" in the OCR read as Greek λόγος (doubtful reading, inferred from the sense and from \"das Wort ... herkomt\").","Wissenschaft = \"science\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Erkennen (noun) = \"cognizing\"; Inbegriff = \"sum total\"; Eintheilung = \"division\"; Bedeutung = \"meaning\".","\"Anschlag\" rendered \"plan\" (not in the glossary); \"geht auf\" rendered \"is directed to\"; \"legt sich auf\" rendered \"devotes himself to\".","\"Propädevtik\" (sic, for Propädeutik) rendered \"propaedeutic\"; \"2fach\" kept as \"2-fold\".","Gemein (\"common\") in \"gemeiner Verstand\" renders intellectus communis, and is kept distinct in the text from *vulgaris*; \"urteile\" is subjunctive (\"that he judge\").","Missing stops after \"Begriffe\" and after \"Z. B.\" sentences are as in the text."]],"poelitz-s01-006":["1.) how the understanding thinks and",["The passage breaks off after \"und\"; not completed."]],"poelitz-s01-007":["2.) how it should think, and this second teaches us logic. Logic therefore teaches the correct use of the understanding, i.e. the use of the understanding that agrees with itself. — Correct understanding is that which in its use agrees throughout with itself, the rules that it then follows are correct. If the understanding in its use is often in conflict with itself: then the rule is false, that is the sole criterion *a priori*. The subjective rules would be the laws according to which we make use of our understanding. The objective rules are those according to which we should make use of our understanding. The objective and subjective rules are in logic, if we abstract from the content, one and the same. Everything empirical must be left out of logic and nothing come in except what can be cognized *a priori*. One can nevertheless retain it as explanation. If one wanted to leave out everything empirical: then logic would become too short. But one must set it apart and not place it among the proper members of logic. E.g. the whole chapter on prejudices would have to drop out because it is borrowed from psychology, but psychology considers the phenomena of the human soul, but these are taken from experience. All cognition is either *a priori*, independent of all experience, e.g. 2 × 2 = 4, or *a posteriori*, empirical, whose source lies in experience. This is contingent and its rules are not necessary. From this there flows therefore the previous proposition, logic must have no empirical principles at all, for because in logic one abstracts from all content: so one abstracts also at the same time from every way in which the objects have been given to us through experience, and such a science one calls *rationalis* in distinction from the empirical. A logic will therefore have to be *rationalis* according to its source. Logic is borrowed from reason, and has reason as its object. One defines logic thus: *rationalis* through *Scientia*, in this lie two things, that it is not taken from experience, that it has reason as object. Now it is asked what the end of this science is. — The end of a science is twofold 1.) to expand our cognition 2.) to clarify and rectify it. General logic can be no expansion but indeed the rectification of our cognition — A science that brings forward principles *a priori*, according to which the powers of our understanding can be correctly used, is *canon*. *Canon* is a precept resting on pure reason without which no correct use of the understanding is possible. Logic is therefore also a *canon* of the understanding and of reason. Besides the rectification of our cognition we also want to have an expansion of it, and if we ask this of logic: then we demand something that conflicts with its nature. A science that is the ground of the expansion of our cognition is called *organon*, e.g. mathematics.",["Ground-text OCR slips silently corrected: Logüi (Logik), weü (weil), nnsers (unsers), 2erle}^ (2erley), Wiederstreit kept as 'conflict'.","Grundsatz / Principium / Prinzip all appear; 'Satz' in 'vorige Saz' is rendered 'proposition'; 'principle' stands for both Prinzip and Grundsatz in the wider text.","Erweiterung = 'expansion' (not in the glossary; Ausdehnung = extension); Berichtigung = 'rectification'; erläutern = 'clarify'; Erklärung = 'explanation' (glossary).","'solte' rendered 'should'; Kräfte = 'powers'; 'Canon' and 'Organon' italicized as foreign words.","'hergelehnt' (hergeliehen) rendered literally 'borrowed'; 'wieder' read as 'wider' (against), 'conflicts with'.","'Phänomene' kept as 'phenomena'; 'Scientia' retained in Latin, capitalized as in the source."]],"poelitz-s01-008":["Logic as canon is a science, but logic as organon is an art. Logic however is no organon, for otherwise there would also have to be a practical logic; it is only a canon of assessment. That logic cannot be divided into the theoretical and practical will still be shown.",["Beurteilung = 'assessment' (the glossary pairs beurteilen = assess with Beurteilung = estimation; judgment; 'judgment' is kept for Urteil).","'Canon' and 'organon' are Greek words printed in roman; italics not added here beyond the method's convention for foreign terms; kept in lower case."]],"poelitz-s01-009":["Now we can give a proper definition of logic, namely *Logica est regularum vniversalium vsus intellectus et rationis*. Aesthetics contains the principles of taste. Logic and aesthetics would therefore already distinguish themselves through the difference of the powers of cognition that are presented in the disciplines. The object of logic is the higher faculty of cognition. The definition of logic as *scientia rationalis* is ambiguous, it is rational according to the form, but therein it is not yet at all distinguished from the others, the proper cause of the definition lies in this, that reason is its object and indeed according to the general laws of the use of the same. This expression is therefore not fitting. From the definition given before we now want to derive the remaining properties of logic, namely 1.) if it is to be a science of the general laws:",["'2deutig' read as zweideutig, rendered 'ambiguous'.","allgemein = 'general'; Latin universalium = 'universal' (kept in the Latin, as printed 'vniversalium').","Eigenschaften = 'properties' (Beschaffenheit is also 'property' in the glossary).","'paslich' read as passlich, 'fitting'; 'vorgetragen' rendered 'presented'.","Erkenntnißvermögen = 'faculty of cognition'; Erkenntniß Kräfte = 'powers of cognition'."]],"poelitz-s01-010":["it must also be so of the necessary ones; for if they are",["Passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next. 'es' and 'sie' left as in the German."]],"poelitz-s01-011":["general; then they must hold of every understanding without distinction of the objects. 2.) A science that rests on principles *a priori* is a demonstrative science. Logic is therefore a demonstrative science. Therefore everything that it presents is capable of a strict proof.",["'Verstände' (dative singular) rendered 'understanding'; 'Object' = 'object'.","Prinzipien = 'principles'; Beweis = 'proof'."]],"poelitz-s01-012":["To see the truth of a thing from experience is not yet",["Sache = 'thing' (Ding is also 'thing' in the glossary); einsehen rendered 'see'. Fragment continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s01-013":["to demonstrate, for then I do not see the necessity.",["Continues the preceding fragment."]],"poelitz-s01-014":["Rules that are necessary must be derived *a priori*,",["Fragment; sentence continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s01-015":["those that are taken from experience are constantly contingent, because experience teaches how they are and not how they",["Fragment; continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s01-016":["must.",["Ellipsis of the verb 'be' as in the German ('wie sie müßen')."]],"poelitz-s01-017":["is a doctrine. — Doctrine is a science that can be proved from principles *a priori*. Empirical sciences cannot be assigned this name. Every doctrine",["Begins and ends mid-sentence; the subject of 'ist eine Doktrin' is lost to the break, and the final sentence is completed in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s01-018":["dogmatic, i.e. it can be proved *a priori*. Aesthetics cannot be doctrine, for it can never be a science. Home therefore calls it better criticism. But since it contains the guideline of our understanding, or the rules of the assessment of the right use of the understanding and of reason: so it is also called canon. According to every canon I can assess whether the procedure is correct: Logic is therefore the *principium* of the *dijudication* of the use of the understanding but only of the correctness according to the form. Logic however is no organon, only through logic do we become conscious of the rules according to which we have already long thought. It is a canon that afterwards serves for",["Begins mid-sentence ('[...] dogmatisch'). 'Aesthetik' stands in the text although the sense seems to require 'Logik'; translated as printed, possible error in the transcript or the OCR.","*Principium* and *Dijudication* kept in the Latinate form as printed; Kritik = 'criticism'; Richtschnur = 'guideline'.","Home (Kames) kept as printed; 'Beurteilung' = 'assessment'."]],"poelitz-s01-019":["criticism whether what is thought is correct. — Logic is divided into the analytic or logic of truth and dialectic or logic of illusion. (Our author divides it so",["OCR 'Wahrund' read as 'Wahrheit und' ('truth and'); the reading is a conjecture from the context.","Schein = 'illusion'."]],"poelitz-s01-020":["we find that Aristotle had already divided it so.) The",["Text of the preceding passage's closing clause continues here without punctuation ('teilt sie so / wir finden'); left as is. 'Der' is followed in the next passage by 'Theil'; a word (e.g. 'erste') seems to be missing."]],"poelitz-s01-021":["part discovers all actions of reason that we exercise in thinking through their dissection, it contains the necessary rules of all truth. It is called the logic of truth because without it our cognition, regardless of the objects, is also untrue in itself. It is therefore only a canon of assessment. If someone wanted to use it as organon, this would already be dialectic. It is a misuse of the analytic, insofar as we want to use it as organon. The canon seems to be organon, but is not. — Dialectic was studied with much diligence in former times; it sought to confirm things according to the illusion, and therefore presented false principles under the illusion of truth. — (The dialecticians were also otherwise called sophists, *Sophistae*, from which also the word *Sophisma*, a deceptive inference, derives.) Dialecticians were among the Greeks the advocates and orators, who could prove a thing with just such grounds",["Begins with 'Theil' after the preceding 'Der'; a word between them appears to be lost ('Der [...] Theil').","Trugschluß = 'deceptive inference' (Schluß = inference); Grundsätze = 'principles', Gründe = 'grounds'.","'ohnangesehn' (ohnangesehen) rendered 'regardless of'; 'Zergliederung' = 'dissection'; 'Sachwalter' = 'advocates'."]],"poelitz-s01-022":["and overthrow could, and thereby deceived the people. Nothing is",["'konten' read as konnten ('could'); it completes the preceding clause ('beweisen und stürzen konten'), so the English word order follows the German. 'Volle' read as 'Volk' ('people'), an OCR slip. 'hintergangen' stands without an auxiliary in the German. Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"poelitz-s01-023":["more unseemly to a philosopher than this science. We shall treat dialectic as the art of distinguishing false illusion from truth. — We have, then, 2 parts in logic, namely analytic, which presents the formal criteria of truth, and dialectic, which will contain the marks by which we cognize whether something does not agree with the formal criteria of truth, even if it really seems to agree with them. — How can we contrive an illusion, and why must logic have antidotes? This comes from the fact that in our judgments and inferences we are very much subject to illusion. Logical illusion in the form of cognition arises from certain means to truth, by which, however, one often brings out only an illusion of truth. E.g., we are often deceived when we believe that the agreement of the cognition with the logical form of thinking is already a sufficient sign of truth. But this it is not, for the agreement with the logical rules is *conditio sine qua non*; it cannot be lacking, but alone it is still not sufficient, because it abstracts from all content. Just as there is an oratorical dialectic, namely to say a multitude of words in the form of a speech in which, however, there is no sense and understanding; so there is also a logical one, to conduct the language of the understanding, even if the latter is empty of all content. The author and others opine that dialectic is the logic of probability, in which, however, there is a difference from illusion. — Probability is a partial truth. Illusion is opposed to truth, but not probability. — Probable is a judgment about truth from certain but insufficient grounds, and the probable belongs to truth just as the insufficient belongs to the sufficient. For if still more grounds are added to the probable, it becomes true. — Logic can be divided into the pure and the applied. Pure logic considers how the understanding ought to act. Applied logic should properly not be called logic, for it is a psychology, in which we consider the phenomena of the understanding, i.e. how it is accustomed to act, not how it ought to act. It is said that the technic, or the way of building a science, ought to be presented in applied logic; but this is in vain, indeed even harmful, for one cannot build without materials. The technic must be presented in the case of every science. — One also divides logic into the theoretical and the practical. But logic as canon cannot be practical, because logic abstracts from all content. A practical logic presupposes a certain kind of objects to which it is applied. — We say every science is an applied logic. For in every science we must have a form of thinking. — We shall divide logic into the dogmatic and technical part. The first contains all rules of the use of the understanding in general, i.e. the theory; the 2nd is the prescription of the rules of the school, according to which we term all logical distinctions. It thus indicates all the technical terms that afterwards serve for criticism. This part is therefore not superfluous, because without terminology we cannot criticize. The technical part of logic could be logic insofar as it contains the form of a system. — The division of logic into the logic of the common and of the speculative understanding is more important. The common understanding is the faculty of having insight into the rules of thinking *in concreto*. (The logic of the common understanding is the acquaintance with the rules *in concreto*.) If the common understanding makes right use of these rules *in concreto*, it is called the sound understanding. (The logic of the speculative understanding is the acquaintance with the rules *in abstracto*.) The speculative understanding is the faculty of having insight into the rules *in abstracto*. E.g. if someone promises something to another and does not keep his word, this is unfair *in concreto*. Yet everyone easily has insight into this, but *in abstracto* it is difficult even for the jurist. But it is thoroughly necessary to have insight into rules *in abstracto*, because without that one cannot have insight into their boundaries; we must therefore, if we want to extend our cognition, pass over to speculative reason. If the common understanding always confined itself to judgments into which it can have insight *in concreto*, it would be very sufficient. The common understanding must therefore never go beyond the guiding thread of experience. Logic as a product of the common understanding, which contains the rules insofar as they are cognized *in concreto*, is not possible. If, then, logic is to be a science of the rules *in abstracto*: it can be no product of the common understanding.",["Opening is a fragment (\"more unseemly … than this science\"); the subject of the sentence lies in the preceding text, which is not given.","Schein = illusion throughout; Kriterien = criteria; Merkmahle = marks; Erkenntniß = cognition; Urtheil = judgment; Schluß = inference; zureichend/unzureichend = sufficient/insufficient; Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability; Verstand = understanding; Wißenschaft = science.","Kennzeichen (\"sign\") is not in the glossary; \"sign\" is chosen so as to keep it apart from Merkmal (\"mark\"). Gegenmittel is rendered \"antidotes\"; erkünsteln is rendered \"contrive\".","einsehen is rendered \"have insight into\" throughout. Kenntniß is \"acquaintance\" (glossary). Grenzen is \"boundaries\".","OCR corrected silently: \"istesaber\" to \"ist es aber\", \"giebt s\" to \"gibt es\", \"dieKenntniß\" to \"die Kenntniß\". The stray sign \"■\" before \"Man theilt\" is omitted as an OCR/apparatus mark.","\"The author\" (der Autor) is left as it stands, unresolved. \"The 2nd\" (der 2te) is left without a noun, as in the German. In \"Er zeigt also …\" the \"he/it\" (Er) is rendered \"it\", since its antecedent is ambiguous (the part or the prescription).","The sentence \"The technical part of logic could be logic insofar …\" is kept as it stands (Der technische Theil der Logik könte seyn, die Logik …); the construction is awkward in the German.","\"Phänomene\" is given as \"phenomena\", since the German is Phänomene and not Erscheinungen. Latin terms in italics are *conditio sine qua non*, *in concreto*, *in abstracto*."]],"poelitz-s01-024":["One can treat or present logic in a 2-fold way. Presentation is the way and manner in which a doctrine can be made comprehensible. Method is the way in which the object is to be completely cognized. — Presentation is either scholastic, if it is suited to the desire for knowledge, capacity and cultivation of those who want to treat the cognition as science; or popular, for those who do not want to treat logic as science, but only need it to enlighten their understanding. Popular presentation avoids all technical terms and scientific arrangement. It is very beneficial for the commonwealth, and every scholar has much merit if he can condescend to popularity. In the scholastic sense the rules must be universal, in the latter, however, as few as possible, but everything *in concreto*. No one can learn to write popularly; rather it must come about through acquaintance with the world, or through practice in presenting one's school instruction to the commonwealth. The French have the advantage in this. — Scholastic presentation is the foundation of the popular.",["Vortrag = presentation; abhandeln = treat; Methode = method; Object = object; Fähigkeit = capacity; Erkenntniß = cognition; Wißenschaft = science; Weltkenntniß = acquaintance with the world.","\"faslich\" is read as \"faßlich\" (comprehensible); \"woUen\" as \"wollen\" (OCR).","\"Im scholastischen Verstände\" is rendered \"In the scholastic sense\" (Verstände read as a sense/meaning, not as the faculty); the alternative reading is \"understanding\".","das gemeine Wesen is rendered \"the commonwealth\" (Wesen elsewhere is \"being\"). \"The latter\" (im lezteren) is left as in the German, referring to the popular presentation.","Kunstwörter is rendered \"technical terms\"."]],"poelitz-s02-001":["Term and history of logic.",["Benennung = term (glossary); it might also be \"designation\" or \"naming\"."]],"poelitz-s02-002":["Epicurus called logic *canonica*; it was very compendious and the term good, namely canon of reason. Aristotle can be regarded as the father of logic, at least by us. His way of teaching is very scholastic and goes to the most universal concepts that lie at the ground of logic. In fact, however, no one has use from it, because almost everything turns out to be subtleties. The terms for various actions of the understanding have been drawn out, which is almost its only use. — Aristotelian philosophy had the upper hand for a very long time, until the reformer Ramus came, who however lost his life in the Parisian Blood-Wedding. Further, Malebranche and Locke, who however did not treat a proper logic, because they also speak of the content of cognition. Among the moderns there are 2 who consider universal logic as a whole, namely Leibniz and Wolff, and the universal logic of the latter is the best one has. — Others have combined Wolff's logic with the Aristotelian. — Wolff's logic was concentrated by Baumgarten, a man who has much merit in this, and Meier commented on him. Let us remark briefly: Aristotle presented it as organon and divided it into analytic and dialectic. He proved almost the greatest acuteness in his writings among all the ancients, yet he nevertheless had many micrologies. — The scholastics, who always vied over who could best explain Aristotle, lost their standing after the Reformation; thereafter came the skeptics, among whom Lullus, David Hume and Bayle, who can also be called antilogicians (i.e. those who posited the understanding in uncertainty). Further belonging here is the book whose author is unknown, *recherches de la verité*. Lambert has determined something with regard to logic in his Organon.",["Benennung/Bennenung = term; Begriffe = concepts; Verstands Handlungen = actions of the understanding; Erkenntniß = cognition; Inhalt = content; Grund = ground; setzen = posit.","OCR/spelling corrected silently: \"compendioes\" = compendiös, \"Bennenung\" = Benennung, \"Nuze\" = Nutzen, \"verlohr\" is retained as \"lost\". \"Wolf\" is rendered with the usual English spelling \"Wolff\".","\"Pariser Bluthochzeit\" is rendered literally as \"Parisian Blood-Wedding\".","\"bemerken wir kürzlich :\" begins a new sentence without a clear link to the preceding one; it is kept as it stands, with the subject unresolved, and the sense of the passage here may be corrupt or cut.","\"bestirnt\" in the last sentence is doubtful (perhaps \"bestimmt\" or \"berührt\"); \"determined\" is a tentative reading and the word cannot be confirmed from the OCR.","\"Lullus\" and \"recherches de la verité\" (French title, italicized) are kept as printed; the OCR has \"verite\" for \"vérité\"."]],"poelitz-s03-001":["Treatment of logic. On cognition.",["Abhandelung = treatment (cf. abhandeln, \"treat\"); Erkenntniß = cognition. \"Von der\" is rendered \"On\"."]],"poelitz-s03-002":["All our cognitions have a twofold relation",["Erkenntniße = cognitions (*cognitiones*)."]],"poelitz-s03-003":["1.) a relation to the object, i.e. representation",["Object (Latinate) and Gegenstand both = \"object\"; here the German is *Object*.","\"d. i.\" rendered \"i.e.\"; the text as it stands equates the relation to the object with \"representation\" (grammatically ambiguous: apposed to \"relation\" or to \"object\"); left so."]],"poelitz-s03-004":["2.) a relation to the subject, i.e. the consciousness of this representation. Actually consciousness is a representation that another representation is in me. The author endeavors to explain [*erklären*] the representation, but this is in vain, for a representation would always have to be explained through another representation.",["Bewustseyn = consciousness; Vor-Stellung (hyphen from line break) = representation (*repraesentatio*).","erklären rendered \"explain\" (glossary also allows \"define\"); same word kept in both occurrences.","\"Eigentlich\" = \"actually\" (could also be \"strictly speaking\")."]],"poelitz-s03-005":["In every cognition one must distinguish matter, i.e. object, and form, i.e. the way we cognize the object. E.g. a savage sees a house in the distance, whose use he does not know, so he has just the same object in his representation that another has, but the form is distinguished. With him it is mere intuition, but with the other intuition and concepts, namely that it is a dwelling for human beings. —",["Gegenstand = object (the German here is *Gegenstand*, then *Object*); Erkenntnis = cognition; erkennen = cognize; kennen = know.","Art = way; Begrife (sic) = concepts (*Begriffe*); \"unterschieden\" rendered \"distinguished\", following the German's adjectival/participial form (possibly \"different\")."]],"poelitz-s03-006":["A representation that contains what is common to several is a concept; but a single representation that contains what is common only to one is intuition. Cognition through concepts is a discursive cognition, cognition through intuition is an intuitive cognition",["Anschauung = intuition; *discursive*/*intuitive* are Latinate in the German and are italicized as foreign words only if so printed; kept here in plain English as the cognates.","\"das enthält was nur einem gemein ist\" kept literally (\"common only to one\"); the German is odd, and no emendation is made.","The passage has no final punctuation in the OCR."]],"poelitz-s03-007":["The difference of form rests on consciousness; if I am conscious of the representation: then it is clear; if I am not conscious of it: then it is obscure. Consciousness is the essential condition of all logical form of the cognitions. Logic does not deal with the way we occupy ourselves with obscure representations, but assumes that they are clear, and insofar can also occupy itself only with them, for if I do not know that I have a representation: then I also cannot apply the logical rules to it. Obscure representations are therefore not an object of logic, because here we do not see how representations arise, but only how they agree with the logical form. — All clear representations can be distinguished with regard to distinctness and indistinctness. If I am conscious of the whole representation, but not of the manifold that is contained in it: then the representation is indistinct. E.g. I see a country house in the distance, and am conscious that this is a house, so I also necessarily have a representation of the parts of the house, e.g. of the window etc., for if I did not see the parts I would not see the whole house either, but since I am not conscious of this representation of the manifold: I have an indistinct representation. Another example: in the concept of beauty various things occur, namely there must be something that falls into the senses, there must be something that pleases universally: everyone has a clear concept of the beautiful: but as soon as he cannot set out its manifold, the concept is nevertheless indistinct. An indistinct representation is called confused by the pupils of Wolff, but this expression is not appropriate, because the opposite of confusion is not distinctness, but order. Distinctness is indeed an effect of order and indistinctness an effect of disorder. But they are not the same; indeed every confused cognition is indistinct, but not every indistinct one confused. Indistinctness often does not stem from confusion, but from a lack of consciousness. In cognitions where there is no manifold, there is no order, but also no confusion. Thus one must call all simple representations not confused but indistinct. Something can be distinct in accordance with form, i.e. I can be conscious of the manifold in the representation, but in accordance with matter distinctness can decrease, when the degree of consciousness becomes smaller, although all order is there. —",["klar = clear; dunkel = obscure; deutlich/Deutlichkeit = distinct/distinctness; undeutlich = indistinct; verworren = confused; Verwirrung = confusion; Ordnung = order.","Mannigfaltige = manifold (n.); Wirckung = effect; Ausdruck = expression; Grad = degree; Materie = matter; Form = form.","\"der Form nach\" / \"der Materie nach\" rendered \"in accordance with form/matter\" (could be \"as to\").","\"Eine undeutliche Vorstellung nennen die Schüler des Wolfs verworren\": \"Wolfs\" retained as in the source for Wolff; spelling normalized.","\"wenn ich nicht weiß\" etc. keep the colon-clause articulation of the German; \"Sinne\" = senses; \"algemein gefält\" = \"pleases universally\" (OCR/orthography silently corrected).","\"denTheilen\" OCR word break corrected; \"auseinander sezen\" rendered \"set out\"."]],"poelitz-s03-008":["An indistinct representation is when I am indeed conscious of the whole, but not of the parts of it — a distinct one when I am also conscious of the manifold, or of the parts in the whole, although the consciousness of the manifold in the representation does not directly constitute the distinctness, for order is still required for it",["Ganzen = whole; Theile = parts; \"geradezu\" rendered \"directly\".","No closing punctuation in the source."]],"poelitz-s03-009":["Distinctness can be twofold: 1.) a sensible one, which consists in the consciousness of the manifold in the intuition. E.g. the Milky Way I see as",["sinnlich = sensible; \"l.)\" OCR for \"1.)\".","Passage breaks off mid-sentence; continued in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s03-010":["a whitish streak in the sky, its stars",["\"weislichen\" = *weißlichen* (whitish); fragment of a sentence, continued in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s03-011":["must necessarily have come into my eye, but the representation was only clear, through the telescope it becomes distinct, because",["\"noth wendig\" OCR word break corrected; \"Vor-Stellung\" hyphenation silently removed; \"Teleskop\" = telescope. Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"poelitz-s03-012":["I now see all the small stars contained in it. 2.) Distinctness in concepts or distinctness of understanding consists in this, when I analyze the concept in accordance with the manifold that lies in it. E.g. virtue: here is contained first:",["Verstandesdeutlichkeit = distinctness of understanding (Verstand = understanding); zergliedern = analyze.","Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s03-013":["the concept of freedom, then: adherence to rules, and:",["Anhänglichkeit = adherence (could also be \"attachment\"); Freiheit = freedom. Fragment continues."]],"poelitz-s03-014":["the power to overcome the inclinations when they are contrary to these rules,",["Macht = power (not in glossary; \"Kraft\" is \"force; power\"); \"zuwieder\" = *zuwider*, \"contrary\". The infinitive \"zu überwältigen\" follows in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s03-015":["to overcome.",["Completes the clause begun in the previous passage: \"die Macht … zu überwältigen\"."]],"poelitz-s03-016":["Through such making-distinct I have added nothing to my concept, here e.g. of virtue, but only explained it. — With distinctness, therefore, the representations are improved not according to the matter but according to the form. — The author speaks of rational cognitions in order to distinguish them from the historical. All cognition through which it is represented that something is, is historical, that something must be necessary, is cognition of reason — they are therefore cognitions of the necessity of the judgments. Otherwise one also already calls cognitions rational, if they agree with reason. I cognize something historically, insofar as it is communicated to me by others, it comprehends in itself the experience of the judgment of others. Reason infers necessarily, because it concludes everything from the necessary conditions of truth. — All our cognitions are either intuitions or concepts. The faculty of intuition is sensibility, the faculty of concepts is understanding. One can define sensibility still otherwise, namely metaphysically, where it is considered from another side, namely it is the faculty of receptivity, the understanding however is the faculty of spontaneity, which only represents things as they are, not as they affect us. — Sensibility is called *facultas inferior*, because it gives us the material for thinking, understanding is called *facultas superior*, because it disposes over this material, and brings it under a representation or a concept. A cognition can be perfect according to laws of sensibility, then it is aesthetically perfect, or according to laws of the understanding, then it is logically perfect. — Sensibility is therefore a perfection insofar as it serves to give material for thinking and to make the rules of the understanding distinct *in concreto*. In this it is indispensable. Sensibility is however imperfection, if it confuses the concepts. — Understanding accompanied by sensibility is elevated, but if the latter comes into the place of the former, then confusion arises. Without sensibility the concepts would have no object and they have significance only in intuition, i.e. in sensibility. With regard to sensibility a cognition can have much perfection that is far different from the logical. E.g. if it is new, easy and lively, i.e. has subjective clarity. A mathematical proof cannot however be rejected on that account because it is difficult. — The perfection of cognition insofar as it can be assessed only according to norms *a posteriori* is aesthetic, insofar as it can be assessed according to norms *a priori* is logical. It is asked: What is perfection? Properly this occurs in metaphysics. Instead of perfection we want to posit It pleases, for we call that perfection, if something contributes to its pleasing. If something pleases according to laws of reason: then it must please, according to laws of sensibility: then it is not necessary. The constitution of the objects, through which they agree with the universal laws of sensibility, is aesthetic perfection, and one learns it merely from experience. — The faculty to assess whether something agrees with the laws of sensibility, is taste, with the laws of the understanding, is power of judgment. What pleases, pleases either mediately or immediately. — In general: what agrees with the laws of the power of representation, pleases, the representations may be sensations, concepts or intuitions, hence something pleases intellectually or sensitively. That which pleases mediately is in itself indifferent, can quite well even displease in itself, but it pleases as a means to a thing which pleases in a higher degree, that something pleases as a means belongs to the understanding, but is no object of our present investigation of the universal laws of what pleases and displeases. That which pleases, one divides into the agreeable, the beautiful and the good. The concepts are correct, the division however not properly logical. Better is this division: everything that pleases is either an object of private or of universal satisfaction. If the latter: then I also cognize the necessity. Nothing else takes place here, than that I must be able to say: It must please everyone, and then it lies in the object. — That which is a ground of private satisfaction, is agreeable. Hence one does not say the object is agreeable, but it is agreeable to me. The agreeable is either charm or emotion. That which pleases universally pleases either according to laws of sensibility, and then it is called beautiful or according to laws of the understanding, and then it is called good. True beauty must therefore be entirely separated from charm and emotion. The agreeable pleases the senses in sensation and is the cause of private satisfaction — The beautiful pleases the senses in intuition, and is the cause of universal satisfaction, hence one also says, the faculty to distinguish the agreeable from the disagreeable, is feeling, that everyone has. To distinguish the beautiful from the not beautiful (not from the ugly, for what is not beautiful, is not always ugly) is taste. Both belong to the sensible faculty of assessment. He has taste who chooses that which pleases universally, and insofar as it pleases the understanding of every rational human being, it is good. Good and beautiful agree in this, that they both go to the object. In both there is a universally valid satisfaction, but they are different in this: in the former there is strict and in the latter comparative universality. The objective highest satisfaction is in the good, the subjective in the agreeable. All strict universality must be able to be demonstrated, i.e. it must be able to be shown why it pleases and that it must please. — The good can also be demonstrated from universal principles, — but the rules of taste let themselves not be proved, i.e. not be cognized *a priori*, for they are rules of sensibility. — Accordingly experience alone can decide on the correctness of taste. All logical perfection rests on the agreement of the cognition with the object, the sensible on the agreement of the cognition with the subject. Logical perfection therefore contains rules that must hold for every understanding and cognition, for the cognition of all thinking beings must be one and the same with ours, if we consider one and the same object. The perfection of understanding therefore rests on universally valid laws, the aesthetic however on the particular sensibility of each human being. A logically perfect cognition can also be aesthetic. E.g. if a cognition that is true, is also at the same time easily spread. — Among all aesthetic perfections none lets itself be combined better with logic, and none seems to be more essential to it than intuition i.e. form, in which there are also universal laws. Sensations i.e. matter let themselves not be communicated, and no universal laws take place here either. — Our cognitions, if they already have logical perfection, which consists in distinctness, truth, and universality, can also still acquire aesthetic perfection. The aesthetic perfection is with regard to the items cited not only different from the logical, but also in some items contrary to it: First with regard to distinctness through concepts. The aesthetic perfection is distinctness in intuition thus merely liveliness. The liveliness of the cognition is promoted by the object being represented to the senses. The distinctness through concepts is promoted, when it is abstracted from the senses. Logical truth may not take place, or the cognition may not agree with the object, but with the subject. Aesthetically and poetically true, is that which is in accordance with sensible illusion. — To truth belong investigation, comparison and testing, that is difficult, if I let merely the imagination play, it is easier i.e. something enlivening, i.e. something that brings my powers of mind into a harmonious play. — Universality, this concept is the opposite of what is required for aesthetic perfection. With logical perfection universality *in abstracto* is required. With aesthetic perfection this universality is clarified *in concreto* through particular cases, and here the aesthetic perfection is disadvantageous to the logical. The aesthetic perfection can therefore be considered from 2 respects, namely as advantageous to the logical, and harmful, the first is the essential beautiful, the last the non-essential beautiful. — Of what belongs to sensation not to intuition, of that one has no concept. Sensations permit no logical perfection at all. Charm belongs to the agreeable, emotion to the sublime, and both belong to aesthetic perfection but to the non-essential beautiful. Only that which is in intuition belongs to the essential beautiful. Some objects occupy our feeling more, some more the intuition. Our sensible cognitive power can therefore also have more intuition or from another object more sensation. Sensation belongs to beauty and to that which is in accordance with taste, but to logical perfection it is most contrary, just like charm and emotion, for thereby my cognition becomes neither more certain, nor truer, but charm and emotion even corrupt logical perfection in our cognitions and judgments; from this it follows: logical perfection is the basis without which no perfection can take place. Aesthetic perfection cannot subsist by itself alone, the logical can indeed yield, but not be wholly canceled, I may not e.g. give the concept all possible distinctness, in order to concede something to the aesthetic, and one can say in general: The aesthetic perfection always takes something away from the logical, but the need of human nature requires it. Both, sensibility and understanding must be paired together and the school-correct (logical) perfection must become popular i.e. yield. The rule of the unification of logical and aesthetic perfection therefore follows from this 1.) The logical perfection is the foundation 2.) Sensibility can be added, to explain the concepts *in concreto* and to make them comprehensible 3.) but one must look more to intuitions than to sensations, because the former are the essential beautiful 4) Finally charm and emotion can still be added, with which however one must be very cautious, because thereby the attention can be drawn from the object to the subject, which is very disadvantageous. That one gives the preference to logical perfection, comes from this, that the understanding has the advantage that what it says, is universal, because therefore the judgment of the understanding has universal validity, sensation has little worth with regard to it, because it goes only to some things not to the universal. But because logical and aesthetic perfection must be together: there arises from this always a combination of praise and blame, because one cannot do enough for both cognitive powers e.g. a presentation is beautiful, but shallow, thorough but dry, here one must therefore necessarily look to the end: Certain cognitions are to instruct, these must be thorough, or entertain, thus be beautiful, and in the reconciliation of these two items in cognitions that are to do both, instruct and entertain, rests that which one calls genius. The understanding wants to be taught, sensibility enlivened, the first desires insight, the second comprehensibility. Between both there is therefore a kind of conflict that cannot be removed. One must therefore seek here to do enough for the greatest end. E.g. with a speech logical perfection is more necessary than aesthetic although the latter is not to be neglected. with the poem aesthetic is more necessary. Many cognitions can become very tasteful if they are first presented dry and thoroughly e.g. morality.",["Vernunfterkenntniß = 'cognition of reason'; vernünftig(e Erkenntniße) = 'rational'; Verstand = 'understanding'; Gegenstand and Object are both 'object'; Erkenntniß = 'cognition'.","Deutlichmachung/Deutlichkeit = 'making-distinct'/'distinctness'; erklären = 'explain'; beweisen = 'prove', but erweisen = 'demonstrate', to keep the two apart.","Wohlgefallen = 'satisfaction'; gefallen/mißfallen = 'please'/'displease'; Reiz and Reizung both = 'charm'; Rührung = 'emotion'; algemein = 'universal'; faßlich/Faßlichkeit = 'comprehensible'/'comprehensibility'.","OCR: 'vorkommen seyn' (\"Ein Erkenntniß kann vorkommen seyn\") is read as 'vollkommen seyn' ('can be perfect'); the reading is a correction of a clear OCR slip but not certain.","'Verstand von Sinnlichkeit begleitet wird erhoben': the German has no conjunction or auxiliary linking the participial clause to 'erhoben'; the English keeps this loose construction ('Understanding accompanied by sensibility is elevated').","'der nur Dinge vorstellt': the masculine relative 'der' could refer to 'Verstand' or to 'Spontaneität' (grammatically feminine, so 'Verstand' is the likelier antecedent); the English 'which' leaves this open.","Stray marks '■' and the duplicated 'Vorstellungskraft Kraft' were treated as OCR noise ('Kraft' dropped once); 'derlogischen' was read as 'der logischen'; stray page/line numbers omitted.","'Logische Wahrheit darf nicht statt finden' kept literally ('may not take place'); 'darf nicht' could mean 'need not' or 'must not'."]],"poelitz-s04-001":["On the perfection of cognition in general.",["Volkommenheit = perfection; Erkenntniß = cognition."]],"poelitz-s04-002":["The perfection of cognition is 1.) logical 2.) aesthetic 3.) practical.",[]],"poelitz-s04-003":["Note. Practical perfection does not belong in logic for that deals with the faculty of cognition, not with the will. Also the aesthetic one actually not, but we take it only in order to clarify the logical one better through the opposition. — These perfections rest on the 3 faculties, understanding, feeling, desire. A perfection can thus be considered in a threefold aim 1.) on the faculty of cognition itself 2.) that it fill with liking. 3.) that it not be an idle cognition, but have an influence on our action. — *Perfectio logica consistit* (logical perfection consists)",["OCR: 'x4bsicht' read as Absicht (glossary: aim); 'Volkomnienheiten' read as Volkommenheiten. The stray marks ■ were omitted as OCR debris, not Academy apparatus.","Erkenntnißvermögen = faculty of cognition; Wohlgefallen = 'liking' (not in glossary); Erläuterung/erläutern rendered 'clarify'.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence; the Latin is translated as it stands. 'auf das Erkenntnißvermögen selbst' is elliptical and is rendered with a bare 'on'."]],"poelitz-s04-004":["1) *respectu quantitatis in universalitate* (with respect to quantity, in universality).",["OCR 'vniversalitate' read as *universalitate*."]],"poelitz-s04-005":["2) *qualitatis in perspicuitate* (of quality, in perspicuity).",["The *respectu* of the preceding line is understood in the Latin as it stands; nothing is supplied."]],"poelitz-s04-006":["3) *relationis in veritate* (of relation, in truth).",[]],"poelitz-s04-007":["4) *modalitatis in neceßitate* (of modality, in necessity).",["*neceßitate* = *necessitate* (spelling as in the source)."]],"poelitz-s04-008":["The logical perfection of a cognition is considered according to 4 main parts.",["OCR 'Avird' read as wird; 'Erkenntnißes' as in source. Hauptstücken = 'main parts'."]],"poelitz-s04-009":["1.) according to quantity when it is universal.",["OCR 'algemem' read as allgemein; Quantitaet kept as 'quantity'."]],"poelitz-s04-010":["2.) according to quality, when it is distinct.",[]],"poelitz-s04-011":["3.) according to relation, when it is true.",[]],"poelitz-s04-012":["4.) according to modality, when it is certain. On 1. A cognition that serves as a rule and has other cognitions",["Gewißheit/gewiß = certainty/certain."]],"poelitz-s04-013":["under itself is more perfect than that which has only a particular",["Fragment: the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s04-014":["use. On 2. One asks here: is a cognition distinct or indistinct?",["deutlich/undeutlich = distinct/indistinct."]],"poelitz-s04-015":["If it is distinct: then it is more perfect according to quality. On 3. Relation of the cognition to the object is truth. If the",["Object = object (Latinate; see next passage on Gegenstand)."]],"poelitz-s04-016":["cognition of the object is not true, then it is no cognition or not of the object, that I believed to cognize,",["Gegenstand and Object are both 'object'. 'oder nicht des Objects' is elliptical in the German and kept so."]],"poelitz-s04-017":["and it is the main thing — If one looks merely at sensation,",["'sie' (feminine) is left as 'it'; its antecedent is not resolved. Empfindung = sensation."]],"poelitz-s04-018":["then one does not ask much about truth. On 4. Certainty is that which banishes all doubt, it is the consciousness of the necessity of truth.",["OCR 'Noth wendigkeit' and 'Bewustseyn' read as Nothwendigkeit and Bewußtseyn."]],"poelitz-s04-019":["We now want to take the aesthetic perfections that correspond to these. — I can think to myself",["Fragment: the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s04-020":["1.) subjective truth, which is not the truth of a thing in itself, but of the thing as it seems to us and affects our senses 2.) subjective distinctness or distinctness in intuition which is attained through examples. 3.) aesthetic universality that is popularity namely that it pleases everyone and is appropriate to every understanding 4.) aesthetic necessity and certainty rests on this, that a cognition is necessary according to our senses i.e. that experience confirms it. Here there always occur 2 pieces that make up the perfection, manifoldness and unity. Our power of cognition is eager for the former but if the latter is lacking to it: then it cannot satisfy us. With the understanding the unity is concept, with the senses intuition. Truth however is the main perfection, because it is the ground of unity, through the relation to the object. Also in aesthetic perfection truth is the foremost negative condition, for this is the agreeableness, but untruth also cannot subsist with aesthetic perfection. No one therefore gets ahead in the beautiful sciences, unless he has laid logical perfection in his cognitions as ground. No science, one can say, is in itself beautiful, because rules must always precede the exercise, the beautiful must however be assessed from the effect, which belongs for sensibility. — Taste however cannot be brought under any rules. One can indeed give some, which however serve for estimation, not hold as laws, which have in themselves an inner certainty. The rules of the understanding however are all laws. Everything beautiful has the particular, that it cannot be treated as science, there can therefore also be no beautiful science. — There is thus no other way to come to the possession of the beautiful and of taste than through beautiful models, which one finds among the ancients. They are of the kind that they have withstood the criticism of so many centuries, and have always been found good. So something constant seems to be here. The mark of taste is this: that what pleases one also pleases others, it thus has something social, and is fashion-like and imitative. — Taste however is what comes last, for it is a power of judgment which however comes later than all culture of memory and culture of the understanding for one must already have much to choose from if one wants to judge. — We can occupy ourselves in a twofold way, through leisure and proper occupation or work. All our occupation with the beautiful is play and so far to be approved, but if this play becomes a business: then it is ridiculous. All of this gives us guidance how taste is to be formed, namely not according to rules, but according to models, they are however not to be imitated, but are only to leave behind an impression in us.",["OCR: 'Begrif' = Begriff (concept); 'Algernemheit' = Allgemeinheit; 'm der Anschauung' read as 'in der Anschauung'; 'Kerne' = Keine; 'findt' = findet. Stray page/line marks omitted.","Sache = 'thing' (distinct from Ding, also 'thing'); Kennzeichen = 'mark' (the glossary's Merkmal is also 'mark'); Effect (Latinate) = 'effect'; Beurtheilung = 'estimation', beurtheilt = 'assessed', urtheilen = 'judge'.","'gehört für die Sinnlichkeit' kept literally ('belongs for sensibility'). 'Vermögen' is not at issue here; Erkenntniß Kraft = 'power of cognition'.","Ambiguities kept: 'und ist modeartig und nachahmend' (taste or the one who has it) is left unresolved; 'sie sind von der Art' refers to the models by grammatical position only. 'Ausübung' = 'exercise'.","Wißenschaften schöne = 'beautiful sciences'; 'Muster' = 'models'. 'Haupt Volkommenheit' = 'main perfection'."]],"poelitz-s05-001":["On the concept of science.",["Wißenschaft = science (*scientia*); Begrif = Begriff = concept."]],"poelitz-s05-002":["A science is a system of cognitions. The cognitions are either common or systematic. — One can have very many common cognitions, which are nevertheless not in systematic combination, and thus do not have the utility that they could have. Sciences are either sciences of reason or erudition. The first can only be cognized from universal principles of reason, the other can be taught, or it can be learned historically. Acquaintance with and criticism of language and books are instruments of erudition and they can also be reckoned to it. The philosopher is thus no scholar, he must only look to the last ends of erudition. He is also no artist of reason, but he studies the legislation of reason. Philosophy and mathematics also do not belong to erudition, but to know the philosophy of others is erudition. The philosopher can adorn his philosophy with erudition.",["OCR 'sein1' is doubtful; read as 'sehr' ('very many'), with the stray '1' (a note mark) dropped. 'Vernunftwißenschafft' read as Vernunftwißenschaft.","Gelehrsamkeit = 'erudition'; Gelehrter = 'scholar'; Kenntniß = 'acquaintance'; kennen = 'know'; Prinzipien = 'principles' (Grundsatz is also 'principle'); Zwecke = 'ends'.","'Vernunft Künstler' = 'artist of reason'; Gesezgebung = 'legislation'."]],"poelitz-s05-003":["Erudition is the sum total of historical sciences, but to cognitions of reason one can attain without being taught by anyone as e.g. a fellow in England who still understood nothing of mathematics gradually of himself hit upon many Euclidean theorems one calls indeed the philosopher and mathematician, in opposition to the citizen, scholars but in Latin not *eruditos* but *litteratos* (those are actually only such who can read and write). The sum total of all historical sciences is called *polyhistory*, the sum total of all sciences of reason taken together *polymathy*. — The sum total of these two *pansophy* The historical cognition of reason combined with philosophy could be called *encyclopedia*.",["OCR: 'Phüosophen' = Philosophen; 'gelahrt' = gelehrt; 'Vernunfter kenntnißen' = Vernunfterkenntnißen ('cognitions of reason'); 'Lehrsatze' = Lehrsätze ('theorems').","Punctuation is as in the source (no stop after 'Lehrsatze' or 'Gelehrte'); not supplied. The semicolon-less run-on is kept.","Inbegriff = 'sum total'. The foreign words (polyhistorie, Polymathie, pansophie, Encyclopädie) are rendered by their English cognates in italics; the German spellings are as in the source."]],"poelitz-s05-004":["In vastness, scholarship takes precedence over cognition of reason, for it is itself science of cognition of reason, although it is not cognition of reason, but both are distinguished from art. The beautiful mind, orator and poet are not philosophers, not mathematicians, not scholars but artists, for the beautiful cannot be taught. All imperfection of cognition concerns either quantity or quality. With regard to both it is material or formal. Logical or formal magnitude is universality, aesthetic or material magnitude is extensiveness. The more things the cognition can be applied to, the logically greater it is; the more I cognize, the aesthetically greater it is. Material imperfection with respect to quality consists in error or falsity, or that the cognition does not agree with the object. A cognition can be true but indistinct, true but not universal. The imperfection is either imperfection of privation or of the counterpart, *imperfectio contradictorie sic dicta* and *imperfectio contrarie sic dicta*. One could also call the first *imperfectio negative sic dicta*, where a mere not-being of perfection takes place. Imperfection of lack is called in logic *generaliter* ignorance, that of privation is called error. With the latter the former always takes place, and the latter is worse than the first. The author divides imperfection into *defectum* and *vitium*. *Imperfectio privative dicta est defectus*, *Imperfectio contrarie dicta est vitium*. The imperfection of lack is not to be censured, with regard to the imperfection *in sensu contrario*, the faults are either logical or aesthetic, essential, which concern the end itself, and *extra* essential. The aesthetic imperfection is dryness. The logical is shallowness. Aesthetic imperfection is not ugliness, for dryness is a lack of that which pleases the senses. True ugliness also cannot be reconciled with logical perfection. One must not flatter the senses far too much, but also not take too much from them. The ancients excused their barbarisms by thoroughness, but when the sciences were better cultivated one saw that it is necessary not to do injury to aesthetic perfection. All our cognitions, even when they are from experience, we must bring to concepts, and the concepts again conversely to intuitions, because we soon become confused in the universal concepts.",["OCR corrections: 'sie dieta' read as *sic dicta*; 'dieta' (privative/contrarie) as *dicta*; 'clefectus' as *defectus*; 'exeolirt' as *excolirt*; 'Alle3o' as 'Alle'. A stray 'f' after 'Bey diesem' is omitted as an OCR artifact.","'Vastität' rendered 'vastness'; 'Gelehrsamkeit' 'scholarship'; 'Vernunft Erkenntniß' literally 'cognition of reason'; 'Algemeinheit' 'universality'; 'Weitläuftigkeit' 'extensiveness'; 'Gründlichkeit' 'thoroughness'.","'Wiederspiel' is rendered 'counterpart'; the sense of the word is doubtful. 'excolirt' is rendered 'cultivated' (Latin *excolere*).","'Unvolkommenheit' = 'imperfection' (cf. Vollkommenheit = perfection). 'Beraubung' = 'privation'; 'Mangel' = 'lack'; 'Irthum' = 'error'.","Ambiguity kept: 'die Fehler sind entweder logisch oder ästhetisch eßentiel die den Zweck selbst betreffen, und extra eßentiel' (punctuation as in the source; the groupings of 'logical or aesthetic' and 'essential... extra essential' are left unresolved). 'Bey diesem... jene... leztere... erste' keep the source's shifting demonstratives.","'Sinnen' = 'senses' (Sinn); 'Object' = 'object'; 'Erfahrung' = 'experience'; 'Anschauungen' = 'intuitions'."]],"poelitz-s05-005":["all cognizing can have twofold perfections",["'Erkennen' (infinitive noun) = 'cognizing'."]],"poelitz-s05-006":["1. A scholastic one which is commensurate with the objective rules.",["OCR 'Ehie' read as 'Eine'; 'objeetiven' as 'objectiven'. 'angemeßen' = 'commensurate'."]],"poelitz-s05-007":["2. A popular one which is commensurate with the subjective rules of social intercourse, the former is called: truth, the latter taste.",["OCR 'subjeetiven' read as 'subjectiven'. The German has a comma, not a semicolon, before 'erstere'; kept. 'Umgang' = 'social intercourse'."]],"poelitz-s05-008":["He who observes scholastic punctiliousness insofar as it unnecessarily conflicts with taste is called a pedant, but not he who shows it before the connoisseur. *Definitio generalis* Unnecessary punctiliousness in formalities is called pedantry, e.g. to bring forth school-sentences in company of the unlearned.",["OCR 'generahs' read as *generalis*. 'Schillsentenzen' is garbled; 'school-sentences' (Schulsentenzen) is a conjecture.","'Kenner' = 'connoisseur'; 'Pedant'/'Pedanterie' = 'pedant'/'pedantry'."]],"poelitz-s06-001":["On the horizon of cognition.",[]],"poelitz-s06-002":["Horizon is the congruence of the boundaries of our cognition with the ends of humanity; it would thus be the sum total of our cognition which, taken together, is adequate with the end that we have. If we take the ends universally, then the horizon is absolute, but if we take the ends particularly in this or that aim; then the horizon is relatively determined. To determine the horizon belongs to culture of our power of judgment, although this however is a difficult work; yet it is useful, because thereby much confusion in our concepts and judgments is avoided. One can determine the horizon 1.) logically in relation to our faculty of cognition, how far we can get, and how far one must go, or in relation to logical aim, insofar as cognitions serve as means to our main cognition as our end. In the cutting-out everything must be laid out for completeness. It comes to the reduction anyhow. The first is necessary for philosophy. 2.) aesthetically in relation to taste. The horizon in relation to taste one could also call popularity. I can determine the horizon either according to the judgment of the public or of the connoisseurs. One who does not want to become a scholar by trade, but wants to know only so much that he can join in the conversation in company seeks current scholarship, which pleases everyone, where even ladies can take interest in it; he thus determines the horizon with regard to taste. Since it is more charming to have the applause of the public than of some closet scholars, one looks merely to the aesthetic horizon or sets up the science according to the taste of the public, and thereby one falls into shallowness. That is the case with the French. 3.) practically in relation to utility. The practical horizon with regard to the influence that it has on our morality is pragmatic. One must not determine the horizon too early, because one then still knows no science, and thus can choose none. One must also not change it too often, that happens with those who have not determined it for themselves properly and too early. What I cannot know is above my horizon. What I may not know is outside my horizon. That, ignorance of which is even laudable, is below my horizon. The horizon is immeasurable with regard to historical knowledge, and of these one must procure for oneself as much as possible, especially in youth, since memory still has the most receptivity, and since one still finds taste in it; afterwards, when one thinks for oneself, taste for historical knowledge is lost. The horizon of mathematics can be determined thus, namely to what kind of objects mathematics cannot be extended. With respect to cognition of reason insofar: how far reason can go *a priori* without experience, and this is useful in order not to make fruitless undertakings. Error is an imperfection of privation. Ignorance an imperfection of lack, and the former is worse than the latter, for to remove error one must again procure ignorance. This is something negative, that something positive. In the removal of ignorance one is always in danger of errors, on that account we may not remain ignorant. Two items contribute to error, ignorance and presumptuous curiosity of wanting to know more than one can know. Ignorance is no great reproach that one makes to someone, but this relates only to those who have very much knowledge, and with all that nevertheless are astonished at the multitude of that which they do not know. The author speaks of a laudable ignorance. Ignorance is never laudable; to ignore something or not to want to know might well be laudable, but there one does not praise ignorance, but rather the reflection according to which I exclude the cognition from my remaining ones, that is properly to abstract, not to ignore.",["OCR corrections: 'Avie' read as 'wie'; 'nehmhch' as 'nämlich'; 'findt' kept as 'finds'. Stray spaces before ':' and ';' ignored.","'Gränzen' = 'boundaries' (Grenze); 'Zwecke' = 'ends'; 'Absicht' = 'aim'; 'Inbegrif' = 'sum total'; 'Urtheilskraft' = 'power of judgment'; 'Erkenntniß vermögen' = 'faculty of cognition'; 'Mittel' = 'means'.","'Zuschnitt' ('cutting-out') and 'Abschlag' ('reduction') are idiomatic and obscure; rendered literally. 'Komts' = 'comes'; subject 'es' left as in German.","'Wißen' = 'know', 'Kenntniße' = 'knowledge', 'kennt' = 'knows'; 'Unwißenheit' = 'ignorance'. 'Vorwitz' rendered 'presumptuous curiosity'. 'Stücke' = 'items'.","'Reflexion' rendered 'reflection' (Latinate; cf. Überlegung = reflection); 'Philosophie' = 'philosophy' (as Weltweisheit).","'Diese ist etwas negatives, jener etwas positives': 'this'/'that' kept as in German without resolving the referents. 'Aufhebung' = 'removal'.","'Sittlichkeit' = 'morality'; 'Object' = 'object'; 'Erfahrung' = 'experience'; 'a priori' italicized as Latin."]],"poelitz-s06-003":["The ancients distinguish idiots and sophists. An idiot is a human being who fancies himself to know a great deal, and yet knows nothing. Not knowing (*Nichtkenntniß*) his ignorance is a reproach, and acquaintance (*Kenntniß*) with it is science, for he who knows nothing also does not know that he is ignorant. The opposite of ignorance is knowing much. Historical knowledge without determinate boundaries is polyhistory; rational knowledge extended without determinate boundaries is called polymathy. But he must also have historical information, because otherwise he has no application of his speculations. Both can be called pansophy. To the former belongs philology, a science of the instruments of scholarship. Critical information about books and languages makes up a philologist. A part of philology is called *humaniora* and he who treats it is called a *humanist*. *Humaniora* are the instructions in that which serves the culture of taste. All this information must be reduced to one principal science, and the others one regards only as means to arrive at it. All sciences are in an interconnection, and one can go for sciences without interconnection, which is then practical polyhistory; but if one sets before oneself one principal science and regards the others as means to arrive at it, then that is a system. One can divide the scholars into guild scholars and scholars free of the guild. Had the sciences not become bread-arts, we would never have come so far in them. Every science as a free art has many advantages, it has its complete freedom and can purify its knowledge of all delusion and prejudices, but it does not have sufficient drive and exertion of forces to perfect itself. The author now speaks of a fault that concerns the use of our knowledge, namely of pedantry. In the use of our cognition it can be distinguished that the use is more suited to the school than to the world. The first contains the method and the form of teaching and learning useful science, which however can drop out in use. Scholastic perfection consists therefore in the didactic form, which is fit to learn a cognition more precisely, as well as to teach it to a beginner according to all the rules of the art of instruction. Our presentation can therefore be school-correct or popular. A scholar by *métier* must have the scholastic presentation. What is school-correct is always the first, and in scholastic perfection we observe all the rules of thoroughness, and without them science would be only trifling. But if we want to communicate our information to the world, the didactic teachings must drop out and the methods must be left out that serve to maintain the didactic form. Some sciences cannot become popular, e.g. mathematics. But popular perfection must not displace the scholastic; in order to please the public, thoroughness must not drop out. Pedantry now is the imperfection in which one wants to present something to the public with the manner in which it is presented in schools. Of all, the pedant in the scholarly field is the most tolerable. One can learn from him, for it is presupposed that he is learned. One does indeed not find with him as much agreeableness as with him who can express himself popularly, but",["OCR corrections made silently: 'se}\\' → 'seyn' (rendered 'is ignorant'), 'bestirnte' → 'bestimmte', 'eine3S' → 'einer', 'erstem' → 'ersten', 'Volkommenlieit', 'konmiuniziren', 'Avird', 'Volle' → 'Volke' (rendered 'public' following 'Publiko' nearby is not claimed; 'Volke' read as 'people', but translated 'public' for the parallel sentence only where the German has 'Publiko'). Note: 'um dem Volle zu gefallen' is rendered 'in order to please the public' on the reading 'Volke' (people); this is a doubtful reading.","Nichtkenntniß / Kenntniß: Kenntniß = acquaintance, information (glossary 'Kenntnis'); 'Kenntniße' rendered 'information' in the plural; Erkenntniß = cognition; Wißen/Wißenschaft = knowledge/science (*scientia*).","'Der muß aber auch historische Kenntniße haben': the subject 'Der' has no clear antecedent in the passage (presumably the polymath); left as 'he'. 'Beides kann heißen Pansophie' kept ambiguous as in German. 'der erstern' rendered 'the former' (OCR 'erstem').","'Wißen' (to know) / 'Wißenschaft' kept distinct; 'Gelehrsamkeit' = scholarship; 'Gelehrte' = scholars; 'Zunftgelehrte' = guild scholars. 'Brodkünsten' = 'bread-arts'.","'Gebrauch' = use; 'Vollkommenheit' = perfection; 'Zusammenhang' = interconnection; 'Mittel' = means; 'Gründlichkeit' = thoroughness; 'Wahn' = delusion.","'Unvolkommenheit da man…' rendered literally as 'the imperfection in which one…'; 'schulgerecht' = 'school-correct'. 'Vortrag' = presentation; 'populär' left as in German.","The sentence 'Das schulgerechte ist immer das erste' and 'Der erste enthält' are rendered with 'first' as in German; the antecedent of 'Der erste' (the school use) is not made explicit. The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('aber') and continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s06-004":["he is nevertheless always usable. The pedants in other fields are commonly empty heads. The opposite fault to pedantry is gallantry. Gallant is he who attributes to someone excellences that the latter would not attribute to himself, and indeed from the superiority of his refinement over the other. It extends also over the style of writing, and no one writes as gallantly as the French. It has something charming and seductive about it, which is dangerous because one is thereby misled to esteem the applause of dilettantes more highly than that of connoisseurs, and from this there also comes a shallowness among the French.",["Continues the previous passage's broken-off sentence ('aber … zu brauchen ist er doch immer'); the first words here complete it.","'Ueberlegenheit' = superiority; 'Verfeinrung' (sic) read as 'Verfeinerung' = refinement. 'Kenner' = connoisseurs; 'Beyfall' = applause.","'Fehler' = fault; 'Galanterie' = gallantry (foreign word in German)."]],"poelitz-s06-005":["The division of the author concerning the magnitude of learned cognition is bad, for he has already earlier spoken of the extensiveness of learned cognition, and that is the same. A cognition",["Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in the next passage.","'Größe' = magnitude; 'Weitläuftigkeit' = extensiveness (wide scope); 'Erkenntniß' = cognition; 'gelehrte' = learned."]],"poelitz-s06-006":["*extensive*¹) according to the multitude of learned cognition, *intensive* according to the importance of the same, i.e. the magnitude of cognition according to the consequences. Logical importance is that which",["Fragmentary and syntactically broken: the verb 'seyn' ('be') at the end of the first clause does not connect grammatically; rendered as far as it can be made out. 'Größe der Erkenntniß ... seyn' omitted-verb construction is left unresolved. 'Erkenntnißes' here rendered 'cognition'.","The superscript '1)' is an OCR remnant, retained as '¹)' since it may be an apparatus mark; doubtful.","'Wichtigkeit' = importance; 'Menge' = multitude; 'Folgen' = consequences (Folge = sequel in the glossary; here plural 'Folgen' rendered 'consequences').","Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"poelitz-s06-007":["furthers the logical perfection of cognition. Practical perfection consists in the utility that can be drawn from the cognition, and this cannot be presupposed. Yet a cognition can respectively have sufficient logical perfection that in another aim is nevertheless still logically imperfect. Content rests on the manifold, which is combined as parts that make up a whole. Fruitfulness rests on the",["'Vohkommenheit' read as 'Vollkommenheit' (perfection); 'Erkenntgezogen' read as 'Erkenntniß gezogen' ('drawn from the cognition'), the 'Nuzen der aus den Erkenntgezogen' being garbled; doubtful. 'Avas' read as 'was'.","'Absicht' = aim; 'Inhalt' = content; 'Mannigfaltige' = manifold; 'Theile' = parts; 'verbunden' = combined (Verbindung = combination); 'Nuzen' = utility; 'respective' kept as in German in the sense 'respectively'.","'hinreichende' rendered 'sufficient' (glossary zureichend = sufficient). Breaks off mid-sentence."]],"poelitz-s06-008":["of the consequences. One otherwise distinguishes weight (heaviness) and importance. E.g. someone can sooner even burn his finger in a pot of hot water than in a frying pan, if the intensive magnitude, i.e. the warmth, is one and the same.",["'der Folgen' continues the broken-off sentence of the previous passage ('beruht auf der | der Folgen'; the doubled 'der' is an OCR/page-break duplication, silently reduced).","'Schwere' rendered 'weight (heaviness)' since the German could mean heaviness or difficulty; the later passage (s06-014) uses it for difficulty ('schwer'), so the ambiguity is preserved. 'Braatpfanne' = frying pan (roasting pan). 'Größe' = magnitude."]],"poelitz-s06-009":["Great objects do not always give great cognitions, e.g. God, and likewise important objects not always important cognitions. A cognition can become comparatively great according as someone has set himself an end, and it can for that very reason become too small, because we have set a great end",["'Gegenstände' = objects. 'Zweck' = end. 'comparative' kept as comparative adverb ('comparatively'). The passage ends without a full stop in the source."]],"poelitz-s06-010":["Ambition is to have properties appropriate to one's station. Deficiencies can be cognized only relatively to the *object*, and one need not be",["'object' is printed in Latin form in the source and kept in italics; elsewhere 'Gegenstand' is also 'object' (glossary), so Latin 'object' and German 'Gegenstand' are both 'object'.","'Eigenschaften' rendered 'properties' (glossary Beschaffenheit = constitution; property); 'Stande' = station; 'erkannt' = cognized. Breaks off mid-sentence ('davor | schämen' continues in the next passage)."]],"poelitz-s06-011":["ashamed of them, for it is partly not possible, partly not necessary, to exhaust everything. But deficiency in that which one has set before oneself as ends,",["'davor' ('of them') continues from previous passage. 'möglich' = possible; 'nöthig' = necessary (glossary notwendig = necessary; 'nöthig' here rendered 'necessary'; German word differs). 'Zwecken' = ends.","Breaks off; the verb is evidently in the next passage ('ein Fehler')."]],"poelitz-s06-012":["a fault. Understanding does not come before years. The value of things learns",["'Verstand' = understanding. The sentence 'Den Werth der Dinge lernt' lacks its subject in the passage and continues in the next fragment; it is left broken off. 'Werth' = value."]],"poelitz-s06-013":["not before years, and to the *quantum est quod nescimus* we come",["The Latin phrase is kept in italics as in the source, untranslated ('how much it is that we do not know'). The sentence is broken off between this passage and the next."]],"poelitz-s06-014":["then, when we have already gone through much. A cognition can be difficult and not important, and a cognition can not be difficult and yet important. Difficulty is therefore no value of cognition, but also no *objection* against the value of the same. Importance rests on the magnitude of the consequences. The more consequences, the more importance.",["'Objection' in the source is a Latin-derived word (spelled 'Objection') and is kept in italics as 'objection'; it is distinct from 'Object' (object).","'Schwere' = difficulty, here (see note at s06-008 where 'Schwere' was rendered 'weight (heaviness)'); 'schwer' = difficult. One German word, two renderings, both marked. 'wieder' read as 'wider' (against)."]],"poelitz-s07-001":["On the truth of learned cognition.",["Heading. 'Wahrheit' = truth; 'gelehrte Erkenntniß' = learned cognition."]],"poelitz-s07-002":["We come now to an essential condition of the perfection of a cognition, namely to truth. Truth is the agreement of the cognition with the object, but that is merely an explanation of the meaning of the word. Truth is not the only perfection, also not in every aim the chief perfection. To be sure a wholly false cognition is nothing, for thereby I cognize no object, it is thus an inseparable condition of perfection, but the degree of truth is not necessarily the same, rather it is different according to the different aims. In logical aim truth is the chief perfection. The question, what is truth? means as much as: one is to give a certain, universal criterion of truth that is usable in application. The demand is important and seems to be fair to make of the logician. The material criterion would consist in the agreement of the cognition with the object, the formal in the agreement of the cognition with itself. — Is there a universal material criterion of truth or only a formal one? Answer. This is impossible, because it would have to be a criterion of truth without regard to all objects and would have to abstract from all differences of them. But all truth consists in this, that the cognition agrees with the object to which it is related. For the cognition that is true of one object can be false of another. A universal mark can therefore not consist in the agreement of the cognition with the object, consequently a universal material criterion of truth is not possible. Logic cannot give it in any case, for it abstracts from all content and looks merely to the form. Thus only formal criteria of truth can be encountered here. The formal criteria are taken from the principle of contradiction. What contradicts itself is false. From the principle of identity as its consequence. If the opposite of the proposition contradicts itself, the proposition is true, but that is not sufficient. If the proposition does not contradict itself, it is on that account not always true. If the latter is the criterion, then it is indeed true, but that does not always take place. A cognition can be called possible only insofar as it does not contradict itself, thus in possibility there is also a criterion of truth, but still far from sufficient, for I can only say: A cognition is possible but not the object. Thoroughness [*Gründlichkeit*] is also a criterion according to the principle of sufficient ground. — For the possible ones are sheer arbitrary propositions that do not contradict themselves at all, where however there is also nothing further. That too is insufficient. Those are logical objective formal criteria of truth. We must acquaint ourselves with the objects through experience and refer our cognition to them. Besides these objective criteria we have yet a subjective one, that is the agreement of our judgment with the judgment of other human beings. With the jurist that is very frequent, but in philosophy ridiculous. Yet one cannot entirely dispense with it either. The formal criteria of logic are merely *conditiones sine quibus non*, namely interconnection and unity. The agreement of the cognition with itself, according to the universal laws of the understanding that are contained in logic, is also a formal criterion. The rules of the agreement of the cognition with itself are",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'aUem' = allem; 'xmsres' = unsres/unseres; 'nehmlicli' = nehmlich; 'findt' read as 'findet'.","Erkenntniß/Erkenntnis = 'cognition' throughout; Kenntnis (s07-005) = 'acquaintance' in the glossary, but rendered 'cognitions' is not used; see that passage. 'bekannt machen' rendered 'acquaint'.","Gegenstand and Object are both 'object'; Gegenstand occurs in the first sentences, Object/Objecte thereafter.","Erklärung = 'explanation' (glossary offers also 'definition'); Wortbedeutung = 'meaning of the word'.","Absicht = 'aim' throughout ('in aller Absicht', 'in logischer Absicht').","Uebereinstimmung = 'agreement'; Einstimmung (the subjective criterion) also 'agreement'.","Saz des Wiederspruchs = 'principle of contradiction'; Saz = 'principle' there, 'proposition' where a judgment is meant ('Saz ... wahr', 'willkührliche Säze'): one English word for the German Satz is not possible in both senses under the glossary.","'Wenn sich das Gegentheil ... wiederspricht' and 'Ist das lezte Criterium' preserved as in the text; 'das lezte' ('the latter') is ambiguous in reference. 'wo aber auch weiter nichts ist' is obscure and kept literal. 'die mögliche' read as 'die möglichen' (plural), rendered 'the possible ones'."]],"poelitz-s07-003":["1.) It must not contradict itself",["'Es' has no stated antecedent in this passage (the context gives none); left as 'It'. 'wieder sprechen' = 'contradict' (Widerspruch)."]],"poelitz-s07-004":["2.) If a cognition is connected as ground with true consequences, then this too is a criterion of truth.",["Folgen = 'consequences' (the glossary gives 'sequel; sequence' for Folge, but the logical sense here is rendered 'consequences'); zusammenhängen = 'be connected' (cf. Zusammenhang, 'interconnection')."]],"poelitz-s07-005":["3.) Between different acquaintances there must be unity.",["Kenntnißen = 'acquaintances' by the glossary (Kenntnis = acquaintance; information; knowledge); the sense may be 'cognitions', but Kenntnis is kept distinct from Erkenntnis. The first note on s07-002 should be read accordingly."]],"poelitz-s07-006":["The *oppositum* of truth is falsity, and if this is taken for truth: then it is error. An error therefore contains falsity and an illusion of truth. A judgment which confuses the illusion of truth with truth itself is an erroneous one. Illusion is a ground for taking false cognitions to be true. The ground of illusion always lies in the subject. If the ground of taking to be true that lies in the subject is confused with that in the object; then there is illusion. Everyone who is concerned about being stuck in error must examine whether he does not have subjective grounds that he falsely took for objective. How is error possible *formaliter*? or how is the form of thinking contrary to the understanding possible? It is hard to comprehend how a force is supposed to depart from its essential laws. The understanding therefore also cannot deviate from its essential laws, and to this extent error would be impossible. If we therefore had no other source of cognition than the understanding; then we would never err, but error as judgment has its seat in the understanding. Since, however, we would have no object through the understanding; so we must have yet another force, which gives us material for thinking, and that is sensibility, it acts according to other laws, but sensibility and understanding always act together, which as it were gives a third diagonal direction. And so we infer: sensibility is the cause of illusion and the source of all errors although the understanding too is to some extent at fault, because it is not sufficiently attentive and cautious. No errors arise from sensibility itself either, for the senses do not judge at all. One can be relieved of error in a twofold way, either always through judging correctly or through not judging at all. So error lies neither in the understanding alone nor in the senses alone, but in the influence of sensibility on the understanding, in that we do not notice it. Logic cannot say more about sensibility than that it holds it to be the subjective, the understanding the objective ground of judgments. It is false to posit the ground of errors in the limitations of the understanding: here lies the ground of ignorance but not of errors. Limitations are the necessary ignorance and lack of cognition that cling to us and not a cause of errors. Nature has denied us much information but it does not cause error, but our inclination to judge and to settle something there where we are bounded, leads us to errors. But a human being can never err wholly and entirely, something true is always in his cognition. All error is therefore partial, for a total error would be a complete conflict against the laws of the understanding and of reason, for then it could not come from the understanding at all, thus be no judgment, this is therefore not at all feasible. — If the talk is of the criterion of truth, then it can here consist only in the agreement of the cognition with the rules of the understanding in general. The rules of the agreement of cognitions with themselves are 1.) the proposition of contradiction and 2.) the proposition of sufficient ground which are to be sufficient *formaliter* but not *materialiter*. A cognition that contradicts itself is false, but if it does not contradict itself it is not always true, that is a negative criterion. If a cognition is interconnected with a true ground, and again if a cognition has true sequels, then it is true. That is a positive criterion. In logic we have no more of them. If the opposite of a proposition is false: then the proposition is certainly true. From non-contradiction no unity arises and so I also cannot infer to truth, there must still be added the connection of ground and sequels. Of the proposition of sufficient ground as a criterion of truth, from the interconnection of the cognitions as ground and sequels. The interconnection is twofold: *a priori* an interconnection as sequel with the grounds and *a posteriori* an interconnection as ground with the sequels. A cognition is connected *a priori* if it is interconnected with grounds, and *a posteriori* if with sequels. How can one infer from the truth of the sequel to the truth of the cognition as ground? *negative* if a sequel is false, then the cognition is false. Among false sequels one also mixes dangerous ones, but what is dangerous is not false. That the inference is correct we see: because if the ground were true the sequel would also have to be true. The *principium rationis sufficientis* can be used well *a posteriori*, also *a priori*. If the ground is true then the sequel is also true. But I cannot infer thus: because a sequel is true, the ground is also true, but if all sequels are true; then the ground is also true, for a cognition can have something true from which precisely this one sequel flows. But if all sequels are true: then the cognition must also be true; for if something false were in the cognition, a false sequel would also have to take place. So here there is a positive inference. With the negative inference there is an advantage, for I need only derive one false sequel and the proposition is false, but with the positive I have a disadvantage, namely the *onus probandi* is incumbent on me. *Ascendendo* I properly cannot infer, because one cannot know all the sequels of a cognition, this therefore gives only a probable inference and takes place in all our hypotheses. *Directe* I cannot infer from the sequels to the truth of the proposition; but surely *indirecte* I can infer from the falsity of a sequel that is drawn from a proposition to the truth of the opposite of the proposition, for since that proposition is false, the opposite must be true. The proposition of contradiction serves for the positive criterion with the necessity of concepts. — The author now speaks of total and partial falsity. A cognition can be either totally or partially false and sometimes it matters to us to attend also to the partial truth, although a partially false cognition is always false, still degrees can take place here. E.g. whoever believes in many gods errs, but whoever believes in no god errs still more. In errors one must always develop and explain the illusion from which they have arisen, but the fewest philosophers have done that. They have indeed refuted the errors, but not shown the illusion, thereby the sources of errors are not removed, through which one can err again in other cases. One finds in every false cognition something true, sometimes it is not necessary to seek out the true, but sometimes it is good. — If a cognition goes to the whole object, or if I cognize everything that is in the object, then the cognition is totally true. If one wants to distinguish total and partial truth: then one must make a distinction between cognition as cognition and cognition to the object. If the cognition is partially true in the first case: then something false is certainly in it. In the other case it can be true in itself only we do not cognize the whole object through it. — A cognition is *tolerabiliter* true, if it has partial falsity, which, however, is not wholly contrary to the end. In all errors one must look to the *tolerabiliter* true. — Further, an exact and a crude cognition are distinguished. A cognition is exact if it is adequate to its object, or if with regard to its object not the slightest error takes place; it is crude if errors can be in it which, however, are not a hindrance to the aim. One must distinguish determining *late* and *stricte*. At the beginning it is necessary at times to determine *late*, especially in historical matters, but in cognitions of reason everything must be *stricte*. To call something crude exact depends on the aim of the cognition. The *late* determination always leaves a space over for error, which, however, still has its determinate boundaries. Error takes place especially when *late* determination is taken for *stricte*. From exact cognition the subtle can still be distinguished. Exactness is objective perfection. Subtlety is subjective perfection. A cognition is subtle if it requires a great degree of attention and force of understanding in order to be perceived. One often blames subtleties and calls them useless. In many things subtlety is extremely necessary. But if one could have attained the end with lesser attention and exertion of the understanding, then one calls it useless subtlety. An adherent to subtleties is micrological. Coarseness is opposed *diametraliter* to useless subtlety. A cognition is coarse, insofar as it still has marks of the common understanding. — All our cognition is historical or rational. The first we possess, insofar as it is given to us. Subjective rational cognitions are those that are drawn from reason, such as the mathematical and philosophical, historical objective ones are those that by their nature cannot be cognized *a priori*, but through immediate experience, report, and teaching. With some the cognition of reason is subjectively merely historical, but here we consider it as objectively rational. All certainty of historical cognition is empirical and that of cognition of reason apodictic. Apodictic certainty is taking to be true with the consciousness of necessity, the empirical is a taking to be true with the consciousness of contingency. Apodictic truths from concepts are *dogmata*. Apodictic truths from the construction of concepts are *mathemata*. Morals is very rich in dogmas, and theology too, insofar as it is a morals applied to God. — In mathematics there are indeed apodictic propositions, but not *dogmata*, these take place only in philosophy.",["OCR corrections made silently: 'Sehern' → Schein (cf. the next sentence); 'Irthimi' → Irthum; 'der3» Saz' → der Saz; 'materiahter liinreichend' → materialiter hinreichend; 'Hegt' → liegt; 'Dmgen' → Dingen; 'bestirnte' → bestimmte; 'glanbt' → glaubt; 'subjeetive/objeetive' → subjective/objective.","Erkenntnis = cognition throughout (cf. erkennen = cognize); Kenntnis ('Kenntniße') = information (glossary also allows acquaintance; knowledge). Folge = sequel (glossary), Folgen = sequels; Schluß/schließen = inference/infer; Satz = proposition throughout, including 'Satz des Widerspruchs' and 'Satz des zureichenden Grundes'.","One English word for two German words: 'sufficient' for zureichend and hinreichend; 'determine/determination' for determiniren/Determination (Latinate) and bestimmt ('bestimmte Grenzen' rendered 'determinate boundaries'); 'boundary' for Grenze, 'bounded' for begrenzt, 'limitation' for Schranke.","Schein = illusion; Irthum = error; irrig = erroneous; irren = err; Fürwahrhalten / für wahr halten = taking to be true; 'für Wahrheit gehalten' = 'taken for truth'.","'die formaliter aber nicht materialiter hinreichend seyn' is a syntactically odd plural/subjunctive construction ('seyn' after a relative), kept as 'which are to be sufficient'; the antecedent could be the two propositions or the rules.","'indem wir denselben nicht merken': 'denselben' (masculine) could refer to Einfluß or to Verstand; 'it' keeps both readings open.","'dies geht also gar nicht an' rendered 'this is therefore not at all feasible'; 'Z. E.' (zum Exempel) rendered 'E.g.'; 'Anhänger an Subtilitäten' kept as 'adherent to subtleties'.","Latin kept in italics: oppositum, formaliter, materialiter, a priori, a posteriori, negative, principium rationis sufficientis, onus probandi, ascendendo, directe, indirecte, tolerabiliter, late, stricte, diametraliter, dogmata, mathemata. Dashes are the transcript's own; the sentence 'Vom Saz des zureichenden Grundes als ein Criterium...' is fragmentary in the German and kept so."]],"poelitz-s07-007":["Apodictic judgments are those whose truth is sufficiently illuminated from itself, i.e. from inner grounds",["Urtheile = judgments; Gründe = grounds; 'hinreichend' rendered 'sufficiently' (not in glossary; cf. zureichend = sufficient).","'erhellet' (is illuminated) kept literal; the German could also be rendered 'is evident'."]],"poelitz-s07-008":["Cognitions whose truth has its grounds in experience or in sensibility in general are empirical",["Erkenntniße = cognitions; Erfahrung = experience; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility.","'überhaupt Sinnlichkeit' rendered literally as 'sensibility in general'; the German lacks an article."]],"poelitz-s07-009":["Hypotheses are such judgments as one assumes to be true in order to explain from them the adequacy of certain consequences, but here one must be cautious, for from a given effect one cannot infer a determinate cause. E.g. One found counterfeit coins in a mint-master's chest in England, he was executed, and after his death it came out that another had placed them there.",["Würkung = effect; Ursache = cause; bestimmte = determinate (bestimmen = determine); schließen rendered 'infer'.","Zulänglichkeit = adequacy (not in glossary; cf. zureichend = sufficient); Folgen = consequences (Folge in the glossary is 'sequel; sequence').","'Z. E.' (zum Exempel) rendered 'E.g.'."]],"poelitz-s07-010":["On the System.",["System kept as the cognate 'system'."]],"poelitz-s07-011":["The author defines system as a sum total of dogmatic truths. The object makes no difference in the systems, for everything must be made systematic if it is to be truth. In every system an idea must be as the whole, which determines the division and the end, and this idea constitutes the systematic unity. If this is lacking: then a manifold system is combined rhapsodistically, but that is not yet tumultuary, for this is opposed to the methodical. Something can be methodical that is nevertheless not systematic, and it is a mark of the system that if something in it is lacking this gap is immediately to be noticed, because we are supposed to have a concept of the whole. The system is thus a touchstone of completeness and correctness. Truth is cognition in the system, and common cognition is every cognition that is not system; it can be very extensive, but is nevertheless not science. One can thus have historical and rational science. Many believe the systematic belongs only to the presentation, not that the cognition itself is supposed to arise systematically. Presentation concerns only the way of the communication of the cognition, but the origin of the cognition too is in many cases already systematic. — Aphorisms are isolated propositions that are not members of a system. — The author speaks of aesthetic and scholarly cognitions. A whole of cognitions as aggregate is common cognition. A whole of cognitions as system is scholarly cognition, or if a whole is combined in accordance with a principle, e.g. geography can be rhapsody as well as system, but what makes it systematic is the globe. — A false cognition and error are also distinguished, for error is first the taking to be true of the false cognition. Thus in the case of error, besides the false cognition, illusion must also be added. There are many illusions, but we can speak only of the logical one, which arises from the agreement of the cognition with the form; the form of a cognition can be correct, merely the content is false, and this logical illusion is commonly present in our cognition.",["Inbegrif = sum total; Object = object; Einteilung = division; Zweck = end; Einheit = unity; Begrif = concept; Fürwahrhalten = taking to be true; Schein = illusion; Inhalt = content; Wißenschaft = science.","Erkenntniß = cognition throughout; 'gemeine' = common; 'gelehrte' = scholarly; 'ästhetischen' kept as 'aesthetic'.","Ganze/Ganzes = whole; 'manigfaltiges' (OCR/orig. spelling) rendered 'manifold', as adjective.","'Systehm' and 'Kennzeichen' : the latter rendered 'mark' (Merkmal is also 'mark'); OCR 'undlrtum' read as 'und Irtum'; 'Irtum' = error.","'ist ... Schein' and 'beym Irtum ... hinzukommen' left as in the German; 'bey unserm Erkenntniß' rendered 'in our cognition'.","'Vortrag' rendered 'presentation'; 'Mitteilung' = communication; 'Probierstein' = touchstone.","'rhapsodistisch' and 'tumultuarisch' rendered 'rhapsodistically' and 'tumultuary'; 'aber systematisch macht sie der Globus' kept with the German word order ambiguity minimal."]],"poelitz-s08-001":["On avoidable and unavoidable error.",["Irthum = error."]],"poelitz-s08-002":["No error is unavoidable in itself. Ignorance, however, is sometimes unavoidable, for this rests not on our will but on the limitations of our understanding, yet there are cases where one must judge, and if we then err: then the error is relatively unavoidable but not absolutely. — A falsity is manifest that is clear to the common understanding; if it is not manifest, then it is concealed and can be made manifest, and on this all refutations rest. The error that has manifest falsity is called absurd, but for him who assumes it to be true, it is not so manifest. — This absurdity also relates to persons, because they are thereby made unworthy to stand in the class of human beings who have only common understanding.",["an sich = in itself; Schranken = limitations; Verstand = understanding; gemeiner Verstand = common understanding; Unwißenheit = ignorance.","'dem aber der ihn für wahr annimmt, ist er so nicht offenbar' kept literal: 'dem' (him) is left dangling as in the German.","'ungereimt' rendered 'absurd' (literally 'unrhymed'); 'Ungereimtheit' = 'absurdity'."]],"poelitz-s08-003":["One must not say that the limitations of our understanding are the cause of error, for first, the understanding that knows its limitations will not go further than it can, and then one would also ascribe to God the cause of error. The limitations of the understanding are, however, causes of ignorance.",["Ursache = cause; Schranken = limitations; 'kennt' = knows (kennen).","'erstens ... und denn' : the 'zweitens' is missing in the German and is not supplied; 'denn' in 'und denn' is read as 'then'.","'legt man ... bey' rendered 'one would ascribe'; 'so' left as 'also'."]],"poelitz-s08-004":["Every error nevertheless has a partial truth, and not even the judgment of the deranged is wholly false, for the understanding judges, i.e. it expresses that it here or there agrees with itself. —",["Urtheil = judgment; urtheilt = judges; 'des gestörten sein Urtheil' is colloquial genitive, rendered 'the judgment of the deranged'.","'äußert' rendered 'expresses'; 'übereinstimmt' = agrees."]],"poelitz-s08-005":["Whoever wants to free another from error must 1st *speciem evoluere*, i.e. prove the falsity of a judgment taken to be true, 2nd *speciem exponere*, explain the illusion and show how he was here deceived by the illusion",["Latin kept in italics; 'ltens' (OCR) read as '1tens' = '1st'.","'für wahrgehaltenen' = 'taken to be true'; Schein = illusion; 'erklären' = explain.","'hintergangen' rendered 'deceived'; 'hier durch den Schein' kept in order."]],"poelitz-s08-006":["On the clarity of cognitions.",["Klarheit = clarity; Erkenntniße = cognitions."]],"poelitz-s08-007":["Marks belong to clarity. What can be regarded as part of a whole possible representation of a thing is mark of the thing, e.g. red color is a partial representation of the whole possible representation of a rose. E.g. The harmfulness of the exhalation of the rose is a partial representation not of the actually whole representation that we commonly have of it, (for few believe it), but of the possible one. Marks can thus be found through analysis, insofar as they are partial representations of actual concepts, — insofar as they are partial representations of possible concepts, but only through synthesis, in that we add one to the other, and here there is no bound, for what thing does one already know wholly. We can thus also define a distinct concept as one that has clear marks, or that can be resolved into marks. — Marks, according to connection or among one another, are divided into coordinated and subordinated. They are coordinated if each is represented as an immediate mark of the thing, they are subordinated if a mark is represented in the thing only by means of the other. The former is called combination of an aggregate, the latter, combination of a series. The aggregation of the marks constitutes the totality of a concept. Through the subordination of the marks I cognize not the least more in the thing, but indeed through the aggregation. Through the subordination of the marks I cognize up to the most general, the series of the marks subordinated to one another has bounds, (for I must after all at some point arrive at the simplest, which lets itself be explained no further) and the distinctness through subordinated marks is the depth of distinctness. — A body is a whole impenetrable and extended, these marks are *compartes*, and coordinated marks. And the series of these coordinated marks is infinite. A cognition thus becomes twofold distinct",["Merkmal = mark; Teilvorstellung = partial representation; Vorstellung = representation; Begrif = concept; deutlich = distinct; Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Klarheit = clarity.","Grenze = bound; Verknüpfung = connection; Verbindung = combination; Totalitaet kept as 'totality' (cf. Allheit = totality); Zergliederung = analysis; erkennen = cognize; kennt = knows.","OCR corrected: 'VorsteUung', 'Teilvorstelungen', 'Begrif en', 'hie', 'coorclinirte', 'last' (= läßt) read as plainly intended; 'vorgestelt' = represented.","'compartes' is Latin, kept in italics.","'Erkenntniß wird also 2fach deutlich' breaks off, continued in the next passage; 'Ganzes Undurchdringliches und Ausgedehntes' rendered literally as 'whole impenetrable and extended'.","'in the thing' for 'am Dinge' (literally 'on the thing') is a flattening; 'am' could be rendered 'on'."]],"poelitz-s08-008":["1.) through the aggregate of the coordinated marks, and here",["Merkmale = marks; continues the previous sentence."]],"poelitz-s08-009":["the distinctness grows extensively through the arrival of every other",["'extensive' is Latin adverbial form, rendered 'extensively'.","Sentence continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s08-010":["mark.",["Merkmals (genitive) completes the preceding phrase 'every other mark'; the genitive is not recoverable in English."]],"poelitz-s08-011":["2.) through the series of the subordinated marks, and there",["Reihe = series."]],"poelitz-s08-012":["the distinctness grows intensively. As agreeable as the first is, so dry",["'intensive' rendered 'intensively'."]],"poelitz-s08-013":["is the last.",["'lezte' rendered 'last' (could also be 'latter')."]],"poelitz-s08-014":["The depth of distinctness that arises here, however, serves very much for the thoroughness and certainty of our cognition. It belongs to the peculiarity of logical perfection, the former to aesthetic. Extensive distinctness one could call the extended, and this the poet seeks in particular, and in this aim poems are useful to read, for in logic too one can use the extended distinctness, it is the easiest and most entertaining. The deep distinctness, however, is a matter of philosophy. It is driven highest in metaphysical investigations, for here one always asks after the Why of the Why. Our cognitions become through it very dry but all the more thorough. The marks are grounds of cognition, and are either (inner) for inner use, insofar as they serve to derive the manifold from the object itself or to explain the object in itself; or (outer) for outer use, insofar as they serve to compare objects with one another in sameness and difference. Through the fact that we cognize the certain marks of the identity of things we make genera, through the fact that we cognize the marks of the diversity of things we make species, or find the *differentia specifica*; or without a concept of the genus one cannot classify, and without a concept of species one cannot specify. The marks that serve for inner use are also good for outer, but not conversely. The latter use is the more current, the former the most important. — The marks are further divided with regard to their relation to the thing itself, into affirmative and negative. There are negative and affirmative concepts and so there must also be such marks. Marks are affirmative when I cognize what the thing is, negative when I cognize what it is not. In some sciences negative marks have much value. E.g. in natural theology. If the object is a lack, then the concept is negative. If we cognize a thing distinctly in that we negate something: then they are negative marks, and if we affirm something then they are affirmative marks, these we properly seek, for by virtue of them we can cognize a thing according to its content with more clarity. The negative are means to arrive at distinctness by my excluding something from a thing, they serve to keep us from errors, and affirmative marks ground our cognition. E.g. the soul is not corporeal, through this I have not understood what the soul is, but have only *removed* an error, and this merit is just as important as that of the affirmative, for this is *conditio sine qua non*. Negative marks can therefore only be of importance if they keep us from important errors into which we can easily come. Then they are useless if they keep us from an impossible error; the affirmative marks serve to understand concepts, the negative not to misunderstand them. One can without disadvantage be ignorant in certain things that do not belong to our purpose. From ignorance is to be distinguished arbitrary ignoring, and this is one of the most excellent prudences in thinking. E.g. the judge ignores when the plaintiff accuses the defendant of all sorts of things that do not belong to the matter and are only supposed to serve perhaps to bring the judge to his side.",["Erkenntniß = cognition; Merkmal = mark (nota); Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Gegenstand = object; Gebrauch = use; Absicht = aim; Zweck = purpose; Vollkommenheit = perfection; verstehen = understand.","'Das Mannigfaltige' = the manifold; 'Gründlichkeit/gründlicher' = thoroughness/thorough (cognate with Grund, 'ground', not marked in English).","'Sie gehört zum Eigentümlichen der logischen Vollkommenheit, die erstere zur aesthetischen': the referents of 'sie' and 'die erstere' are left as ambiguous as in the German.","'oder ohne Begrif vom genere kann man nicht claßificiren': 'oder' kept as 'or' although it reads oddly; possibly a slip for 'denn' in the transcript.","OCR corrected: 'cliff erentiam' to 'differentiam'; 'Gottesgelartheit' read as 'Gottesgelahrtheit' (natural theology); 'Irthum/Irtum' = error; 'removirt' = 'removed' (italicized for the Latinate verb).","'Alsdenn sind sie unnüz, wenn...' is rendered literally ('Then they are useless if...'); the German is awkward."]],"poelitz-s09-001":["On important and unimportant marks.",["Merkmal = mark (nota)."]],"poelitz-s09-002":["Important is a cognition insofar as it is a ground of great use. It is thus only relative, for what is important to one is not so to the other. (Absolute importance or dignity has nothing but morality and the religion grounded on it) One has great, the other has many final ends. Marks have importance with regard to inner use if they are sufficient to cognize thereby very much in the thing itself, with regard to outer use, if they serve to cognize its similarity with many others and to distinguish it from many others. To convince someone of the importance of an end is difficult, of the importance of the means to an end that is already important to him is easy; here there is talk only of the relative importance of the means. — A cognition is important if it serves to make another cognition clear and indeed formally important. If it is a ground of great consequences, then it is materially important; if it is a ground of numerous consequences, then it is fruitful. Marks are sufficient if with regard to the aim they accomplish everything, the sufficiency rests thus on their accomplishing not something but everything, which however is always relative to certain ends. Marks are necessary that cannot be separated from the concept of a thing, those that are not *constitutiva* are contingent, these one calls extra-essential and those essential. *Essentia, est complexus notarum necessariarum interne sufficientium*. But we speak here of the essence of the concept, not of the essence of things. Among the necessary there is a difference, some belong to a thing as grounds of other marks of one and the same thing, and some belong to a thing as consequences of other marks, e.g. the 3 angles belong to the triangle as *rationata* of the 3 sides, but these belong to it as *rationes*. *Notae quae sunt rationes notarum aliarum sunt essentialia, quae sunt rationata notarum aliarum sunt attributa*. The *attributa* must therefore always be derived from the *essentialia*, they also belong to the essence but not as *constitutiva* but as *consectaria*. *Extraessentialia* are also twofold, either they belong to the inner determination, then they are *modi*, or to the outer relation and then they are *relationes*. E.g. Learnedness is a *nota extraessentialis interna*, it belongs to the inner determination. E.g. to be master or servant is a *nota extraessentialis externa*, it indicates only the outer relation, for the human being is by nature neither master nor servant, they are therefore *relationes*. The essence of things in themselves is real essence, and the essence of things according to concepts that we have of them is the logical essence. Although we cannot have insight into the real essence of things, we can nevertheless easily cognize the logical essence of things, for it is nothing but *complexus notarum omnium conceptuum primitive constituentium*. For the logical essence there is thus required totality of the marks that belong to the thing not as consequences but as grounds, as *constitutiva primitiva*. If we want e.g. to determine the logical essence of body, we need not study it in nature, but we take what the concept that we have of it contains in itself. The logical essence concerns nothing but the resolution of the concept into its marks and attention to the *primitiva*, hereby our complaints about the limitation of our understanding are thus restricted: that we namely cannot have insight into the real essence, for that goes beyond our understanding. The real essence is otherwise also called nature, of which one cannot speak here in logic.",["Wichtigkeit = importance; Endzweck = final end (distinct from Zweck = end/purpose, both 'end'); Zweck/Zwecke = end(s); Absicht = aim; Mittel = means.","zureichend/hinreichend/Hinlänglichkeit: 'sufficient'/'sufficiency' used for all three; 'hinreichend' and 'zureichend' are both rendered 'sufficient'.","Wesen = essence; Schranke = limitation; Verstand = understanding; Begriff = concept; Bestimmung = determination; Verhältniß = relation; Totalität = totality (Allheit in the glossary).","'einsehn/einsehen' rendered 'have insight into'; not in the glossary.","'Gründe/Folgen' = grounds/consequences; Folge is rendered 'consequence' here, where the glossary offers 'sequel; sequence' for Folge and 'consequence' for Folgerung (one word for two German words).","OCR corrected: 'Eßentia' etc. to the Latin spellings with 'ss' normalized to 'ss'; 'WTesen' to 'Wesen'; 'üin' to 'ihn'; 'fast' to 'faßt'; 'Begrif s' to 'Begriffs'; 'comen' to 'kommen'. 'Eßentialia' normalized to 'essentialia'.","'Wichtig ist ein Erkenntniß...' 'Erkenntniß' treated as neuter ('ein Erkenntniß'), rendered 'a cognition'.","'Ein Erkenntniß ist wichtig wenn es dient ein ander Erkenntniß klar zu machen und zwar formal wichtig': the 'und zwar' clause is kept as in the German.","'Merkmale sind noth wendig, die vom Begrif ... können getrennt werden': the relative clause follows the noun in the German; the English keeps that order."]],"poelitz-s09-003":["The author now speaks of clear and obscure cognitions. This matter does not belong here, for logic cannot give us rules for representations of which we are not at all conscious. Logic does not teach to make cognitions clear, but distinct; we must however be conscious of our cognition, i.e. it must be clear. One calls a concept obscure that does not have enough clarity for our end. There is thus logical obscurity; which, however, is very different from the psychological. It is therefore comparative obscurity with which logic occupies itself. One must always become relatively obscure if one wants to climb up to an uncommon clarity. One also calls that obscure which not the common understanding, but certainly the speculative understanding has insight into. It is particular that the understanding often works in the dark; that this is true, we see from the improper expression: I feel that this or that is true, but one knows no ground to give for it. Of the reflection of the understanding it is not conscious, but certainly of the result of it, that something is true or false. The latter one calls scruple, one is conscious of a doubt against a thing, but does not know to give ground, is it the latter, then one calls it objection. All truth and falsity lies in judgment, accordingly it cannot be sensed, consequently the expression is entirely false. One must seek to learn what reflections the understanding has made in the dark, through which it has come upon accepting or rejecting something. For this a guidance belongs, namely I go back to the nearest known, and see whether that which is unknown to me does not hang together with it. This action one calls *evolution* and it is often very difficult both in ourselves and in others. The author still speaks of exhaustiveness. The division relates properly not to clarity, but to distinctness. All distinctness consists in the clarity of the marks. If all marks have come to clarity: then my distinct concept of the thing is adequate. The distinctness through the coordination of the marks is of another kind than that through subordination of the marks. The adequate can rest on coordination of the marks and then it is *completudo*, or on subordination of the marks, and then it is *profunditas*. Intensive as well as extensive distinctness belong to logic.",["Erkenntnis = cognition; Vorstellung = representation; Begriff = concept; klar = clear; dunkel = obscure; deutlich = distinct; Merkmal = mark; Zweck = end; Absicht not present here; Grund = ground; Einteilung = division.","OCR: 'Avieder' read as 'wieder' (here 'against'); 'genung' = genug. 'Erkenntnißes' (genitive, 'bewust ... unseres Erkenntnißes') left as in the German.","'Das leztere' occurs twice in the Scrupel/Einwurf sentence ('ist das leztere, so nennt man es Einwurf'); the second may be a slip or an omission, but it is translated as it stands. The clause 'weis aber nicht Grund anzugeben' is also as printed.","'Es ist besonders' rendered 'It is particular' (besonder = particular); 'einsieht' = 'has insight into'; 'Ausführlichkeit' rendered 'exhaustiveness' (not in the glossary); 'empfunden' = 'sensed'.","Latin or Latinate terms kept: *evolution*, *completudo*, *profunditas* (printed 'profunditaet'); 'coordination', 'Subordination', 'comparative', 'relative', 'Reflexion', 'resultats' are rendered by English cognates."]],"poelitz-s10-001":["On total and partial clarity",[]],"poelitz-s10-002":["*In cognitione quadam notae singulae possunt eße obscurae, vniversae, clarae.* That is the case with an indistinct representation. E.g. when one mixes blue and yellow, green arises. On the whole we are conscious of the green, but not of the parts of which it consists. E.g. when one uses the word right, one always has a clear concept, but what it comprises in itself, few know. We have therefore a multitude of representations that are clear to us on the whole but not in their parts. Philosophy however must drive clarity as far as possible. If we put the concept together arbitrarily, then it is clear to us also in its parts, for we know the parts from which it is put together.",["OCR: 'Avenige' read as 'wenige'; 'Teilen' restored; stray space in 'zusammen -sezen' closed.","'Recht' rendered 'right'; the German word could also be rendered 'law', but no choice is imposed beyond the cognate.","Latin sentence left in Latin with the spelling as printed ('eße', 'vniversae'). Teil = part; Menge = multitude; Vorstellung = representation."]],"poelitz-s11-001":["On liveliness.",[]],"poelitz-s11-002":["The logically greater clarity is distinctness, the aesthetically greater clarity is liveliness. There can be liveliness of the mere impression or of the idea, which consists in the multitude of the marks that make up the representation; for each mark brings forth movement in us. Certain representations hang together with the interest of the mind, i.e. they ease the free play of the powers of the mind. We reproach an author for dryness if he does not accommodate himself to taste, for drowsiness if he does not bring forward something that stimulates our attention. One also calls him dry who merely presents his cognition in logical concepts. Liveliness is accordingly entirely different from logical perfection and belongs to aesthetics, it is as it were the *vehiculum* of logic, serves to introduce it, yet it must be applied with caution, so that the effect of the logical aim is not hindered. Liveliness is thus extensive and intensive. The intensive degree is called strength and emotion, the extensive serves to give our concept more intuition or to make it distinct *in concreto*, it is thus very important. The magnitude of intensive liveliness rests merely on feeling, through this however we do not cognize the object better, but we are in the most unfit state to cognize and to judge things. To the liveliness that rests on intuition belong insight, erudition and thoroughness. The extensive can also be produced by a common head. Since we have said, logic makes concepts distinct, it is asked: in what kind of way it makes them distinct. By our author and Wolf the making-distinct is always considered analytically, they think namely that it arises merely through dissection of the concept. It arises indeed thereby with regard to the marks that we already thought in the concept, but with regard to the marks that are added to the concept as parts of the whole possible concept it arises through synthesis. Through analytic making-distinct our cognition is not expanded, but we learn only to distinguish better that which was already contained in our cognition. E.g. when I say: the body is an extended, impenetrable thing that has a figure, I have added nothing to the concept of body, but only dissected what lay in it before. Logic occupies itself with the form of distinctness so that the content remains the same and the cognition does not grow according to content. The analytic procedure of producing distinctness is the first and chief and that which is logical. Sometimes it is also necessary to expand one's concept, then distinctness grows and indeed synthetically E.g. virtue is the harmony of the power of choice with reason, one always thinks that under the concept of virtue, if I add further that it brings advantages with it, then I have, although it is only a contingent property, made my concept synthetically distinct. There is a difference that must well be noted between the proposition: to make a distinct concept (from this springs synthetic distinctness) and to make a concept distinct (from this springs analytic distinctness); in the first there are no marks yet, I begin therefore from the parts, and go to the whole and here I expand my cognition according to content; in the second it does not grow according to content, but the form is only altered. Some want to assert that making-distinct harms energy, which however is false, for the more distinctly I have insight into something, the stronger and livelier it is, only the analysis must not go so far that the object finally even vanishes. — The mathematician proceeds synthetically, the philosopher analytically. To synthesis belongs the making-distinct of objects, to analysis the making-distinct of concepts. Here the whole goes before the parts, there the parts before the whole. Sometimes one proceeds synthetically, when the concept is even given, when one is not yet satisfied with the marks, this often takes place with propositions of experience.",["Gegenstand (and Object) = object, both rendered 'object'; here 'Objecte' and 'Gegenstand' both occur.","erweitern/erweitert = expand (also in 'erweiternde' in the next passage); Erweiterung not otherwise used. Willkür ('Wilkühr') rendered 'power of choice'; 'willkührlich' elsewhere 'arbitrarily'. Eigenschaft = property (not in glossary).","Einsicht/einsehen = insight / have insight into; Gelehrsamkeit = erudition; Gründlichkeit = thoroughness; Rührung = emotion; Zergliederung = dissection; Kopf = head (not in glossary).","OCR: 'Teüen' read as 'Teilen'; 'Avieder' not present here. 'Wolf' left as spelled. 'Lebhaftigkeit' rendered 'liveliness'.","'Saz' in 'dem Saz: einen deutlichen Begrif machen' rendered 'proposition'. The sentence 'Sometimes it is also necessary... synthetically E.g.' has no punctuation before 'E.g.' in the German; kept so.","Latin or Latinate words kept: *vehiculum*, *in concreto*; 'Analysis', 'Synthesis', 'Effekt', 'Aesthetik' rendered by cognates. The dash '—' is in the source."]],"poelitz-s11-003":["Synthesis and analysis",[]],"poelitz-s11-004":["Analytic judgments are elucidating, synthetic however expanding. All analytic ones are judgments *a priori*; and all judgments of experience are synthetic.",["erläuternd = elucidating (Erläuterung = elucidation); erweiternd = expanding, as in the preceding passage."]],"poelitz-s11-005":["The synthetic are either *a priori*, or *a posteriori*. In arithmetic and geometry there are almost nothing but synthetic concepts *a priori*, i.e. my cognitions can here be expanded independently of all experience.",["The German has 'Begriffe' (concepts), not 'Urteile', in the second sentence; translated as it stands.","'fast lauter' rendered 'almost nothing but'."]],"poelitz-s11-006":["The author now talks of comprehending, and calls it *concipere*, which properly means to cognize something through concepts, i.e. through the understanding, for otherwise we find no German word for *comprehendere*. Yet not much really depends on this. We want to take the degrees according to which our cognition can be raised with respect to the object. The 1st degree is — to represent something to oneself. The 2nd is — to perceive, or to represent with consciousness, *percipere*. The 3rd is to cognize something, *cognoscere*. The 4th is to understand something, *intelligere*, to cognize something through the understanding by means of concepts; one could also call that *concipere*, and this is very different from comprehending. I can conceive (*concipiren*) everything, e.g. a *perpetuum mobile*, although in mechanics the impossibility of it is shown. The 5th is to cognize something through reason, *perspicere*, when I have insight into the ground of which. In many things we get as far as understanding but not to insight. Our cognitions become ever fewer in number the more we want to perfect them. The 6th degree is *comprehendere*, comprehending, to cognize something through reason to the degree that it is sufficient for our aim. If one cognizes something to the degree that it is sufficient for every aim, it is called comprehending simply, and that goes beyond the human faculty. When one learns something one must necessarily comprehend what belongs to the aim, otherwise it is in vain and all use falls away. A synthetically distinct concept, where none is contained in the other and also none is lacking, can be called a definition. Remark. We have a use of reason that seems to be sufficient to comprehend something when a thing is there, yet we find, after closer investigation, that we would not have had insight into this *a priori*, and this is very much the case with investigators of nature. I would therefore comprehend something that I could have determined *a priori*. What I cannot determine *a priori*, that I also do not comprehend, although I can explain it.",["begreifen = comprehend (*comprehendere*); the Latin *concipere* is left as printed, and the verb \"Concipiren\" is rendered \"conceive\" with the Latin in parentheses.","erkennen = cognize; Erkenntniß = cognition; wahrnehmen = perceive; einsehen / Einsehen = have insight into / insight; verstehen = understand.","hinreichend (twice) and zureichend (elsewhere in this batch) are both rendered \"sufficient\"; the German words differ.","\"den Grund wovon einsehe\" is awkward as printed (no subject pronoun before einsehe in the text, \"ich\" stands earlier); kept as \"the ground of which\".","\"lste\" is read as OCR for \"1ste\"; \"Object\" = object; \"Naturforscher\" = investigators of nature; \"Absicht\" = aim."]],"poelitz-s12-001":["On total and partial distinctness.",["Deutlichkeit = distinctness."]],"poelitz-s12-002":["The totality of extensive distinctness rests on the multitude of coordinated marks, and of intensive distinctness on the multitude of subordinated marks.",["\"totalitaet\" is the Latinate form; the glossary's Allheit is also \"totality\".","The ellipsis in \"und der intensiven Deutlichkeit\" is kept (\"and of intensive distinctness\").","Merkmal = mark; Menge = multitude."]],"poelitz-s13-001":["On complete and adequate concepts",["\"completen\" and \"adaequaten\" are Latinate German adjectives, rendered \"complete\" and \"adequate\"."]],"poelitz-s13-002":["A concept is distinct if its marks are clear. The distinct concept that contains all marks that exhaust the whole concept is a complete concept, and this perfection is called *completudo*. If no mark is lacking to a distinct concept, there can still be a fault, that one is too many. The perfection insofar as a concept does not have too many marks is called *precision*. — *Cognitio simul completa et precisa est adaequata.* Wolf and his followers define it as a concept whose marks are distinct. This is indeed a high degree of distinctness, but not a firm determinate, rather only a wavering concept, for I can think to myself, of the marks of the marks, again marks, etc. Through this, however, the concept is not fixed. The right definition is: *cognitio adaequata objectum si habet omnes notas quae conceptum exhauriunt sed non abundantes*. Through precision one can become certain that there are neither too many nor too few marks. The lack of it rests on tautology, that I say the same mark 2 times only in another way. If I say, e.g.: God is infinite, almighty, etc. etc., then almightiness already lies in the concept of infinity. There are cases where precision causes some obscurity, but to a deep-seeing head it is all the more valuable, for precise concepts are always appropriate to the thing, and in one versed in art the lack of them is a great fault. At the end of this section the author talks of the concepts of a bright head, which matter we also want to take along, although it does not belong in logic. Under brightness is understood popular distinctness combined with the scholastic. The more precision there is in a concept, the more school-correct it is. One can also call logical distinctness school-correct and the aesthetic popular. Popular distinctness consists in the combination of the representations *in abstracto* with those *in concreto*, and for this much talent belongs. Many authors are very popular, but they have neglected the school-correct in the process, and one cannot rely on them. The brightest head is the one who along with logical perfection is at the same time so distinct that he can also be understood by the most common understanding.",["OCR slips silently corrected: \"Merlanale\" = Merkmale, \"zAvar\" = zwar, \"bestirnter\" = bestimmter, \"öhiezu\" = hiezu, \"Averden\" = werden.","\"ihn\" (\"definiren ihn\") is masculine and its referent is not explicit in the passage; rendered \"it\".","The Latin definition is left untranslated as printed, including \"objectum\"; it is a quoted Latin sentence, and the case of \"objectum\" is as printed.","Vollkommenheit = perfection; Merkmal = mark (*nota*); \"Kunstverständigen\" = one versed in art; \"hell\" = bright, \"Helligkeit\" = brightness; \"schulgerecht\" = school-correct; \"gemein\" = common.","\"Autores\" (Latin plural) rendered \"authors\"; \"Art\" in \"auf eine andere Art\" = way."]],"poelitz-s14-001":["On the certainty of cognitions.",["Gewißheit = certainty; Erkenntniß = cognition."]],"poelitz-s14-002":["There are 3 degrees of taking to be true, it is either",["Fürwahrhalten = taking to be true."]],"poelitz-s14-003":["1.) problematic, then it is called opining or",["\"denn heists\" is read as \"dann heißt es\"; meinen = opine (Meinung = opinion)."]],"poelitz-s14-004":["2.) assertoric, if I regard my cognition not as possibly true but as actually true, then it is called believing, or",["\"würklich\" = actually; \"aßertorisch\" = assertoric; glauben = believe."]],"poelitz-s14-005":["3.) apodictic, if it is combined with the concept of necessity, so that I declare it to be irrevocably true, that is called knowing.",["\"Noth wendigkeit\" read as one word (OCR); wißen = know. Capitalized infinitive nouns (Meinen, Glauben, Wißen) are rendered \"opining, believing, knowing\"; Wissen with an article is \"knowledge\" (glossary)."]],"poelitz-s14-006":["The 1st degree is opining.",["\"lste\" read as \"1ste\"."]],"poelitz-s14-007":["Opining is an, with consciousness, insufficient objective taking to be true. With opinions we for the most part begin in our cognitions. Sometimes one has an obscure presaging, where however we ourselves do not know what we actually think, although the thing seems to have a mark of truth. Opining is a provisional judgment and we cannot easily dispense with it, but one must guard against holding opinions to be something more than opinions; this is also well to be observed with hypotheses or philosophical opinions. In many sciences it is not permitted to opine, e.g. in metaphysics, mathematics, morals, etc. etc. In physics there are many opinions, especially with regard to causes and occurrences. From our definition it flows that opining is also a subjectively insufficient taking to be true, because we are conscious that it is objectively insufficient.",["Ambiguity kept: \"mit Bewustsein unzureichendes objectives Fürwahrhalten\" — \"with consciousness\" may go with \"insufficient\" or with the taking to be true; \"objectives\" is printed as an adjective, though the later clause has \"objectiv unzureichend\".","\"phüosophischen\" read as \"philosophischen\" (OCR); Begebenheit = occurrence; Bewustsein = consciousness; unzureichend = insufficient.","\"Vorausahnden\" rendered \"presaging\"; \"Definition\" = definition."]],"poelitz-s14-008":["The 2nd degree is believing.",[]],"poelitz-s14-009":["Believing is indeed a subjectively sufficient but objectively insufficient taking to be true. Belief can be theoretical and practical. The first grounds itself on the testimony of others and is the historical, the other on interest, but also on reason, that is moral belief.",["Glauben (infinitive noun) = believing; Glaube = belief; \"Zeugniß\" = testimony; \"gründt\" = grounds (gründet)."]],"poelitz-s14-010":["Remark. In mathematics one can also believe what a great man has said, but not in philosophy, because there are not such means there to cognize the misstep",["\"Anmerkung\" is run on to the text in the source; a period is supplied after \"Remark\" only for legibility. The passage has no final period.","erkennen = cognize (it could be \"recognize\"); Mittel = means; \"Philosophie\" = philosophy."]],"poelitz-s14-011":["The 3rd degree is knowing.",["\"Der3te\" read as \"Der 3te\"."]],"poelitz-s14-012":["Knowledge is contradistinguished from belief. One can have a belief ever so rational, and yet one cannot say that one knows it. For knowing is the objectively sufficient taking to be true, then I cognize something from logical grounds. Sometimes I cognize something from practical grounds, believe it quite firmly, but I do not yet know it. And so, e.g., are many truths of religion.",["\"contradistinguirt\" is kept as a Latinate German verb (\"contradistinguished\"); \"das Wißen\" with article = knowledge; \"Wißen ist\" = knowing.","\"noch einen so vernünftigen Glauben\" rendered \"a belief ever so rational\"; vernünftig = rational.","\"Für wahrhalten\" read as Fürwahrhalten (OCR spacing); \"Religions Wahrheiten\" = truths of religion."]],"poelitz-s14-013":["Persuasion is the subjective of taking-to-be-true, insofar as no ground of the objective is contained therein. Being persuaded is thus the consciousness of a subjective ground, so in persuasion no logical grounds take place, i.e. ones that are valid for everyone. Conviction is the state of the subject in which it is impossible that it take the opposite to be true. Here there is a subjectively necessary taking-to-be-true, so it must also have its ground in the object. All conviction or complete taking-to-be-true is twofold, it can either be communicated and then it is logical, or not, and then it rests either on merely subjective grounds, then it is persuasion and aesthetic, or on objective and subjective practical grounds, i.e. ones that determine the will, and that is practical belief. What is the difference between logically and practically sufficient conviction? If we are free of all interest, and there is then conviction in us, it is logical, whether we have made ourselves free of the interest, or it may itself have had none. One also calls this conviction speculative, but if we have an interest in it, it is practical, i.e. it is sufficient to determine us to something, but we cannot communicate that to anyone else, because interest is something subjective, and we cannot bring others to an interest in that in which we have one. To be preoccupied means to have a subjective ground in one's judgment, which determines one's assent before the objective grounds have yet been investigated. They are very common in human beings. The doctrines of youth always preoccupy us, and we can free ourselves from them only with difficulty. We are further preoccupied through resentment, hatred, enmity and predilection etc. etc., which flow in upon us very strongly. In taking-to-be-true the hardest thing is to see from what it stems, whether from sensible influence or from the understanding, and often we ourselves distinguish persuasion and conviction through feeling. In opining we do not even take the cognition to be subjectively sufficient, but in believing we do. Practical belief is often firmer than all knowledge. In the latter one still listens for counter-grounds, but in practical belief one does not. E.g. the belief in God and another world is firmer than all knowledge, because we have so much interest therein. Practical conviction is the greatest possible, and only a few propositions are capable of it, which are called morally certain; these are such as are the ground of all morality, and which, when we follow them, agree very much with our conscientiousness. Practical belief is thus a firm unchangeable conviction, and indeed for the reason that no opponent can refute a morally certain proposition, although objections are made against it. — Sensible certainty can be divided into the certainty of the senses and the certainty of experience or empirical certainty. The senses alone do not constitute experience, but rather the judgment of the understanding about the combination of sensible representations. Senses and understanding are the sources of our cognition. Through the senses we have appearances and the understanding combines them, and the relation of the same or their unity is experience, it is thus the cognized connection of appearances. All experience thus contains judgments that come from the understanding. The logicians have now been occupied with deciding the question of the certainty of the senses. Here the question is not whether something true is in the object which they represent to us, but whether they have objects, or whether they only deceive us through illusion, to which no object corresponds. This question does not belong in logic, because it does not occupy itself with objects, but in metaphysics, where illusion is spoken of. But from logic we can nevertheless already see that neither falsity nor truth lies in the senses, because they do not judge at all. Here the question of certainty comes up rather. An empirical cognition is a cognition through the understanding about objects that are represented through sensibility. All empirical certainty thus goes to the relation of the sensible representations, and with this we must let it rest. Sensible cognition is distinguished from rational cognition when its object is an object of reason, like morals, which is thus *materialiter* rational. Logic alone, however, is *materialiter* and *formaliter* rational, insofar as it has the understanding as its object. Our cognitions can concern objects of experience and the certainty can nevertheless be empirical and rational. (Empirical certainty is either *originarie*, when I know something from my own experience, or *derivative* empirical when I know something from another's experience). Reason adds necessity to all certainty, which experience cannot do, e.g. that a thing is useful experience teaches, but that it must be necessarily useful reason alone teaches. Of everything we cannot have rational certainty, but where we can have it, we must prefer it far to the empirical. But we must not confuse rational certainty with the empirical; one is rationally certain of that which one would also have had insight into *a priori* without experience. Rational certainty is again twofold, for since our cognitions are either from concepts, discursive, or from the construction of concepts, intuitive, the certainty is also discursive and intuitive. The intuitive is mathematical, the discursive, philosophical. Empirical certainty is assertoric, rational apodictic, i.e. the certainty of a cognition that is combined with the consciousness of necessity. Mathematical certainty is also called evident, because the object is exhibited in the intuition. An intuitive cognition is clearer than a discursive one. Both are equally certain but not equally clear, i.e. the way of their certainty is different. The author also speaks of the *modis* of taking-to-be-true. *Modus* is the difference in the way in which I take something to be true. The *modi* are the following: 1.) Something is assumed when a thing receives assent without knowing whether it is certain.",["Fürwahrhalten = 'taking-to-be-true' (hyphenated where used as a noun, as in the first sentence); the verb phrase für wahr halten = 'take to be true'. Ueberredung = persuasion; Ueberzeugung = conviction; Glaube = belief; Wissen/Wißen = knowledge; Meinen = 'opining'.","'Subjective' and 'objective' are German substantivized adjectives ('das Subjective', 'des Objectiven'); 'the subjective of taking-to-be-true' and 'ground of the objective' are kept literal and the syntax of 'kein Grund des Objectiven hierin' left as in German.","zureichend and hinreichend are both rendered 'sufficient' (hinreichend is not in the glossary); Gegenstand and Object are both 'object'; Gewißheit = certainty; Erkenntniß = cognition; Verbindung/verbinden = combination/combine; Verknüpfung = connection; darstellen = 'exhibit' (not in glossary).","OCR slips silently corrected: Avelchem, nrnß, immöglich (read unmöglich), Avir, «erden, geAvis, verbindt, last (läßt). 'klärer' read as klarer, 'clearer'. Garbled or doubtful: 'Bei lezterm' read 'in the latter'.","Ambiguities kept: 'wenn sein Object ein Object der Vernunft ist, wie Moral, die ist also materialiter rational' ('die' could refer to Moral or to the cognition; English 'which' leaves it open); 'Sie sind dem Menschen sehr gewöhnlich' (Sie = the preoccupations, plural; kept as 'They').","Latin kept in italics: materialiter, formaliter, originarie, derivative, a priori, modis, Modus, modi. 'Praeoccupirt' = 'preoccupied'; 'Praedilection' = 'predilection'. 'denn' in 'und denn' = 'then' (for dann); 'Z. E.' = 'e.g.'; 'd. h.' = 'i.e.'. 'Intereße' = interest; 'aesthetic' as in original.","'beruht ... auf' = 'rests on'; 'bewenden laßen' = 'let it rest' (idiomatic). 'Beifall' = 'assent'; 'angenommen' = 'assumed'. The passage breaks off after the first mode; its continuation is in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s14-014":["2.) Something is settled when it is assumed because its truth is known.",["ausgemacht = 'settled'; angenommen = 'assumed' (as in the preceding passage); bekannt = 'known'."]],"poelitz-s14-015":["3.) Something is undeniable, if it is impossible to take it to be false.",["unläugbar = 'undeniable'; 'es für falsch zu halten' = 'to take it to be false', parallel to für wahr halten."]],"poelitz-s14-016":["These are the 3 degrees of assent. If something is settled: then it is presupposed that it is objectively and subjectively true. Undoubted propositions are not called settled, because controversies have never been raised against them, e.g. the Pythagorean theorem. A cognition can be thorough in various ways, e.g. a thoroughly moral presentation is of a different kind than the mathematical. Thoroughness does not signify a logical completeness of certainty, but a cognition is thorough if it has the perfection that is appropriate to the nature and the end of the cognition. Certain cognitions are shallow, which one calls by another name: *superficielle*, although these expressions are very different. The first indicates the lack of depth of the cognition. What offers itself immediately to the common understanding is often also quite appropriate to the end. But if a cognition is supposed to go beyond the purposive perfection for a common understanding and one nevertheless takes only what is equally distinct to the common understanding, then it is *superficial*. — The author now speaks of assent and withholding of assent, or postponement of our judgment. The expressions, I give assent, I reject, I withhold my assent, indicate that with us there is something arbitrary in our judgment, in that we take something to be true because we want to, or in short: the question is whether willing has an influence on our judgment. Immediately the will has no influence on our understanding, as e.g. with mathematical truths, which nevertheless remain true even if they are repugnant to us, but on the use of our understanding it has an influence, to drive it to the inquiry into a truth, or to hold it back from it, but not on the conviction of the same, since the latter, however, depends very much on the use of our understanding: so the will also indirectly has an influence on conviction; but if the will had such an influence, to convince us of what we gladly wished, we would constantly make ourselves chimeras of a happy state, and also always take them to be true. But it cannot contend against convincing proofs of things that we do not gladly wish, these nevertheless always remain in the right. —",["Beifall = assent (not in glossary); ausgemacht = settled; gründlich/Gründlichkeit = thorough/thoroughness; seicht = shallow; Vortrag = presentation.","'superficielle' and 'superfiziell' are the transcript's own foreign words; the second is rendered 'superficial' and italicized as Latinate.","Erkenntniß = cognition throughout; Gewißheit = certainty; Überzeugung = conviction; Verstand = understanding; gemeiner Verstand = common understanding.","'nicht wieder überzeugende Beweise ... streiten': 'wieder' is read as 'wider' (against); the final clause 'die behalten doch immer recht' is elliptical and is kept so, with 'these' as the antecedent of 'die' (could also be read as 'those [proofs]')."]],"poelitz-s14-017":["*De suspensione judicii*",["Latin heading kept in Latin."]],"poelitz-s14-018":["If we can sometimes find errors in our cognition, we can happen by chance upon becoming distrustful of our judgment through comparison with other cognitions, from which *suspensio judicii nostri* arises, which is the resolution not to let a provisional judgment become a determining one. I judge definitively if I determine straight out: that is true; or problematically, if I declare I have grounds for accepting something as true which, however, are not yet sufficient. The consciousness that my judgment is problematic is *suspensio judicii*. The *suspensio* can occur with a twofold aim 1.) in order to seek out the grounds of the determining judgment, *suspensio judicii indagatoria*, 2.) or never to judge, which is *renunciatio judicii*. The first is called critical, the other skeptical suspension. — One can also demand and command assent, i.e. the other, if he has seen my grounds, cannot reject them without falling into absurdity, e.g. the mathematician. The philosopher does not have this good fortune, his propositions are also apodictically certain, but not evident. With philosophical cognitions one can give one's assent only conditionally, whether everything will so maintain itself in the sequel. For often a proposition seems to be quite correct, but in use, i.e. when one draws sequels from it, difficulties are found. One must always investigate how a cognition conducts itself in the whole of its use, for which a completion and an overview of the whole always belong. Withholding assent according to maxims can only be done by a practiced power of judgment, and it is found only with increasing age. Our understanding is eager to extend itself and to enrich itself through judgments, whence it comes that one takes to be true a thing that has the illusion of truth, and this way of judging is hard to remove in lively minds, but if he has become wise through harm, then he will be careful not to give his assent quickly, if he has often had to take it back. To leave something *in dubio* is still different from *suspensio judicii*, for here I have an interest, but with the latter it is not always in keeping with the end to decide whether something is true or not. Judgments are divided into provisional (*praevia antecedentia*) or determining (*determinantia consequentia*), the former go before, the latter follow upon the investigation. Provisional judgments are very necessary in the sciences, so that one can say logic would be very much enriched by the treatment of them, if it were only not too deep for it, for the objects about which I can judge provisionally or determiningly must be given to me here. The human being judges immediately, however few *data* he has, and makes, as it were, a trial whether his judgment will be correct or not, which has a strong influence on the human being, so that often the determining one cannot drive it out of him with all its grounds. E.g. In almost the majority of books one judges according to the titles, if one hears the author is a man of merit, then I place more confidence in the book, and if the matter is new, then I have still more right to a favorable judgment, and such judgments are necessary in meditating, when I make the plan for myself, although often much must still fall away afterwards. Since these judgments serve to direct the understanding to various means, they are very necessary in that aim. Determining judgments are those that are sufficient for assent. The provisional judgments one can call *judicia praevia* and we must distinguish them from prejudices. By means of provisional judgments one can think maxims for the investigation of a thing. One could also call these judgments *anticipationes*, because one anticipates one's judgments before one yet has the determining one. They are the taking to be true from grounds insufficient with consciousness, which are supposed to spur us on to seek the sufficient ones. One also otherwise calls them philosophy of the common understanding, for it alone is capable of them, because it cannot engage in deeper investigations.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'judicn.' to judicii, 'entstellt' to entsteht, 'Aveil' to weil, 'gemeinenVerstand' spacing; 'Schwürigkeiten' read as Schwierigkeiten.","Folge(n) rendered 'sequel(s)' per the glossary (Folge = sequel); Folgerung would be 'consequence'. 'Folgen daraus zieht' is therefore 'draws sequels from it'.","bestimmend = determining; Fürwahrhalten = taking to be true; 'aus mit Bewustseyn unzureichenden Gründen' kept in German word order ('grounds insufficient with consciousness').","Ambiguities kept: 'er' in 'wenn er aber durch Schaden klug geworden' has no clear antecedent (Verstand or Kopf); 'für sie' in 'zu tief für sie' (feminine; Logik is the likely antecedent, rendered 'it'); 'das Bestimmende' / 'ihn' ('drive it out of him') left as in German.","Schein = illusion (glossary); Zutrauen = confidence; Mittel = means; Absicht = aim; Zweck = end; zureichend = sufficient.","Latin terms in the transcript kept in Latin and italicized; 'data' kept as in the original. 'maintaniren' (*mainteniren*) rendered 'maintain'."]],"poelitz-s15-001":["On Prejudices.",["Vorurteile = prejudices."]],"poelitz-s15-002":["Remark. There is a difference between investigating and reflecting. Many propositions can be accepted without investigation, e.g. that the whole is equal to all parts taken together, but I must nevertheless reflect on it, i.e. I must be conscious of what that is which I think. Reflection is a holding together of the cognition with the cognitive power from which it is supposed to arise. E.g. if someone says: between 2 points only one straight line is possible; then an operation arises in my mind, whereby I compare and hold together this with intuition (i.e. here with the cognitive power), I thus try it out, as it were, in thought, and this always happens even if we do not investigate the cognition. If a cognition arises from a cognitive faculty that is invalid with regard to the object, then the cognition must either be rejected, or the judgment must be suspended, and one must distinguish which cognitive power has an influence here, and this distinction is also necessary where no investigation takes place. We also accept judgments without reflection; and then they are prejudices.",["untersuchen = investigate; überlegen/Ueberlegung = reflect/reflection (glossary: Überlegung = reflection).","Erkenntnißkraft = cognitive power and Erkenntnißvermögen = cognitive faculty, kept distinct following the glossary (Kraft = power; Vermögen = faculty).","Gemüt = mind; Anschauung = intuition; Object = object; 'in Ansehung des Objects' = 'with regard to the object'; 'ungültig' = invalid.","'Zusammenhaltung' rendered 'holding together' to keep it distinct from Zusammenhang (interconnection)."]],"poelitz-s15-003":["What prejudices are? Prejudices can be taken either in the subjective or in the objective sense. A prejudice *in sensu subjectivo* is (to say it briefly) the propensity to persuasion. Persuasion is the taking to be true from merely subjective causes that are falsely held to be objective. The prejudice arises either from imitation, habit or inclination. The prejudice *in sensu objectivo* is illusion insofar as it is made into the *principium* of truth.",["“im subjectiven oder objectiven Verstände”: Verstände rendered “sense” (the word is the dative of Verstand, which the glossary renders “understanding”); the reading may be a slip, but it is left as it stands.","Hang = propensity; Neigung = inclination; Ueberredung = persuasion; Fürwahrhalten = taking to be true; Schein = illusion; Nachahmung = imitation; Gewohnheit = habit.","OCR slips silently corrected: subjeetiven/objeetiven (subjectiven/objectiven)."]],"poelitz-s15-004":["Prejudices are preliminary judgments, insofar as they are accepted as principles. One must distinguish prejudice and the false cognition that arises from the prejudice. A cognition that with regard to particular objects is something determined is no prejudice. Sometimes they are true preliminary judgments, e.g., Young accustomed, old done, but that is wrong if they are accepted as principles, and that comes from this, that subjective grounds are falsely held to be objective, and this comes from this, that we have not reflected. Every prejudice is a *principium* of erroneous judgments. Out of prejudices there arise, therefore, not prejudices but erroneous judgments, and they are always sources of the same, e.g., the apple does not fall far from the trunk, this one can always accept as a prejudice, because it is not a universal proposition and counts as a *principium*. With erroneous judgments one must always look to the prejudice from which they have arisen. — The 3 sources of prejudices are imitation, habit and inclination. — Imitation has a universal influence. The understanding seeks to extend itself and does it through imitation, especially in the child and the common man. It is a strong ground for taking to be true what others give out for it. — Habit is facility in doing something, and that is good, but not acquired habit, that is the necessity of doing something in the way one has done it before. Habit works more strongly in the old than in the young, because these have not yet by far lived long enough to make something habitual for themselves. If a cognition has become habit, and moreover has arisen from imitation, then such a human being is completely incurable. Prejudices of habit can only be eradicated through length, if the understanding is gradually held up and delayed by counter-grounds. — One could distinguish, in the prejudice of imitation, the active and passive use of our reason, for prejudice from imitation is nothing other than the propensity to the passive use of our reason. I act actively if I derive something from the natural rules of our reason. The first is *contradictio in adjecto* and it is nevertheless widespread among very many human beings. Reason is an active *principium* that can borrow nothing from another, not even from experience, if we want to use pure reason. But it comes from the laziness of human beings that they would rather tread in the footsteps of others than want to exert their own powers themselves. E.g., The certainty of a future world is an objectively rational cognition, but for the most a historical cognition. — Some means by which we can accustom ourselves to imitation are: 1.) Formulas, rules whose expression to the model of imitation",["Passage breaks off mid-sentence (“dient” follows in the next passage); translated as it stands.","Gewohnheit = habit; Angewohnheit marked as “acquired habit” (two German words, the contrast is in the text). Kräfte = powers (glossary: force; power). überlegt = reflected (Überlegung = reflection).","“in Ansehung besonderer Gegenstände was bestimmt ist”: “was” = etwas; construction left open (“is something determined”, or possibly “determines something”).","“Jung gewohnt alt gethan”: proverb, rendered literally. “Ich handele activ … Das erste”: the antecedent of “Das erste” and of “sie” (contradictio) is not stated in the text; left as is.","allgemein = universal; Grundsatz = principle; Satz = proposition; Menschen = human beings. OCR corrected: Urteüen, Stam, subjeetiv, Vorurtheile as in the source."]],"poelitz-s15-005":["serves. Formulas are facilitations when something is entangled, and",["Fragment: continues the previous passage (“dient”) and breaks off before the next. verwikelt = entangled."]],"poelitz-s15-006":["the most enlightened head seeks to invent such. 2.) Saying, whose expression has a measuredness of its pregnant",["Fragment; “Sinnes” follows in the next passage. Spruch = saying; Abgemeßenheit = measuredness (“commensurateness” is also possible)."]],"poelitz-s15-007":["sense, *dicta* are pronouncements that serve for a rule or for the",["Fragment: “Gesez” follows in the next passage. Aussprüche = pronouncements; Sinn = sense."]],"poelitz-s15-008":["law. Sayings, *dicta*, must always be borrowed from others",["Fragment continuing from the preceding passage."]],"poelitz-s15-009":["and to these we must ascribe a certain infallibility, because we accept it, because he says it. 3.) Sentences, propositions that have their authority, because they recommend themselves of themselves through the",["Sentenzen = “sentences” (Sentenz, Latin *sententia*); distinct from Satz = proposition. Ansehn = authority (so throughout)."]],"poelitz-s15-010":["emphasis of the thoughts. (Sentences could",["Nachdruk = emphasis. Fragment, continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s15-011":["be called in German sense-sayings.) They are distinguished from *bon mots*,",["Sinnsprüche rendered literally as “sense-sayings”."]],"poelitz-s15-012":["which are propositions that recommend themselves more through wit than",[]],"poelitz-s15-013":["mature power of judgment. Sentences maintain themselves for centuries,",["Urteilskraft = power of judgment."]],"poelitz-s15-014":["because a judgment that is an original product of reason, and",[]],"poelitz-s15-015":["in which much sense is encompassed in a concise expression, recommends itself very",["umfaßt = encompassed; the sentence is split across this and the next passage (“sehr / empfiehlt”)."]],"poelitz-s15-016":["much.",["Translated from “empfiehlt.” in the source: the verb “recommends” belongs to the preceding fragment, so what stands here is only the rest of that verb phrase; the source breaks the sentence between “sehr” and “empfiehlt”. A literal rendering of this passage alone is “recommends.”"]],"poelitz-s15-017":["*Canones* (*Canones* in German doctrinal sayings and *Proverbia*",["Lehr Sprüche = doctrinal sayings (Lehre = doctrine)."]],"poelitz-s15-018":["thought-sayings.) are universal doctrinal sayings that serve the sciences",["Denksprüche = thought-sayings; Wißenschaften = sciences."]],"poelitz-s15-019":["as foundation, they can also be expressed sententiously,",[]],"poelitz-s15-020":["and then they please more.",[]],"poelitz-s15-021":["*Proverbia*, proverbs are the customary expressions for",["Sprüchwörter = proverbs (OCR “Sprüclrwörter” corrected). Ausdruck = expression."]],"poelitz-s15-022":["a popular judgment of the common understanding. They contain the",["gemein = common."]],"poelitz-s15-023":["current wit of the common understanding and serve to give to cognize the understanding of the same. They are provincial whereas the sentences are universal. Although they also serve as sentences and canons for the most common rabble, hence they are also not to be encountered among people of finer upbringing. Prejudices are grounded thereby, if one posits one’s wisdom in sentences and proverbs, directs oneself by it and never undertakes to think for oneself. The author now speaks of the prejudice of authority. Authority is manifold, of the times, of the person, of the multitude, of the age and of an entire people. From this arise prejudices or here the senses run ahead of the understanding, on account of which they are also called *praejudicia*, the judgment is as it were decided beforehand for the multitude, person etc. These are grounded on the propension (propensity) to fall in with others’ judgments, and such a judgment from imitation one could call a servile one. There are also prejudices from self-love, if one, toward that which is a product of one’s own understanding, is taken with predilection.",["“erkennen zu geben” rendered literally (“give to cognize”); the idiom may mean “make known”.","Propension / Hang: both rendered “propension / propensity”, marked here; Sinne = senses; Menge = multitude; Ansehn = authority.","“anderer Urteile beizufallen”: genitive of “others” with “judgments” left as in the German (“others’ judgments”).","“von Praedilection eingenommen ist” rendered literally; “gegen” rendered “toward”. Verstand = understanding throughout; Eigenliebe = self-love."]],"poelitz-s16-001":["On the authority of the person.",[]],"poelitz-s16-002":["In historical matters it must be the foundation of our judgments, for with things that rest on experience and testimonies we cannot encompass everything with our understanding, but must build our cognition on the authority of other persons, which however is not a *praejudicium*. But if cognitions are so constituted that they can be settled with the understanding alone, then the authority of others is no genuine ground of taking to be true, but if it is a ground that makes a propension in our understanding toward the matter; then it is *praejudicium auctoritatis*. Truths of reason hold anonymously, i.e., one must not ask who has said them, but what he has said. The authority of the person has something in agreement with our inclination, because it serves to flatter our vanity indirectly. If a man is exalted as learned: then one praises him highly on that account, because all praise him highly, and they are equal in this, because against the great man they are nothing, and the difference that the admirers have among themselves is counted as insignificant. Citations of learned men can serve as no proof in cognitions of reason, although they have a certain weight to strengthen our belief, it is an outer criterion of truth, the universal agreement of the human understanding. With metaphysical propositions this does not work, but indeed with jurisprudence, whence one can see how uncertain the juridical judgment is. The cause here is probably the manifoldness of the cases, which can be neither determined in a law nor foreseen.",["The source has no full stop after “Verstandes” (before “Bei metaphysischen Säzen”); I have supplied one there and a comma after “belief”, as the OCR/transcript punctuation is lacking.","“wol” (wohl) rendered “indeed” in the first occurrence and “probably” in the second; both readings of the particle are possible in each place.","Sachen/Sache = matters/matter; Ding = thing; ausgemacht = settled; ächt = genuine; Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness; Glaube = belief; Beweis = proof; Kriterium = criterion; Neigung = inclination."]],"poelitz-s17-001":["On the authority of the multitude.",[]],"poelitz-s17-002":["To this the rabble is inclined, for the merits, capacities etc. of a person it cannot assess, it therefore holds to the multitude. It presupposes that what all human beings say is true, but also only with historical things, in matters of religion, where it is interested, it leaves this to the learned.",["OCR “überlast” read as überläßt; “intereßirt” = interested. beurteilen = assess; Fähigkeit = capacity; Menge = multitude."]],"poelitz-s17-003":["Prejudices may not always be false, but if I accept a thing from prejudice, it can nevertheless be true, but that I accept it with prejudice is logically harmful. — With regard to the way of cognition it is particular that the ignorant person has a prejudice for scholarship, in that he credits the scholar with more knowledge even where he cannot have more. E.g. about the state after death. The scholar, in turn, has a prejudice for common understanding, in that he gets a mistrust of scholarship, and indeed then, when all his efforts do not procure him proper satisfaction; it is found, then, in one who has already fairly run through the circle of the sciences, and in such speculations as cannot make their concepts sensible, and whose foundations are wavering (that happens in metaphysics and morals); he believes, then, nevertheless to find the key to this somewhere, and seeks it in common understanding, which however is deceptive, for where the cultivated faculty accomplishes nothing, there the uncultivated will certainly accomplish nothing. The appeal to healthy human understanding is nothing other than the appeal to the multitude, for everyone lays claim to healthy human understanding. In metaphysics this does not work at all, for there no case can be exhibited *in concreto*, but in morals it is particular that all cases can be given *in concreto*, hence here common understanding often judges more correctly than the speculative. E.g. the juridical proposition: *casum sentit dominus* is correct to the speculative understanding, but common understanding well sees that this is false, although it cannot give the cause of it. In general the practical use of reason is more in accordance with the use of common than of speculative reason, and all rules of the doctrine of morals *in abstracto*, if they are not weighed *in concreto*, are faulty. Opposed to the prejudice of authority is logical egoism. This is the *principium* of indifference with regard to all others' judgments as criteria of tru-",["\"dürfen nicht immer falsch seyn\": \"may not always be false\" kept ambiguous (German could also be read as \"need not always be false\").","\"Erkenntniß Art\" (OCR, probably the compound Erkenntnißart) rendered \"way of cognition\"; Art = way. \"Kenntniß\" = knowledge (cf. Wissen, also \"knowledge\" in the glossary); \"besonders\" = particular.","\"Vorurteil des Ansehns\": Ansehen rendered \"authority\" (the word could also mean regard or standing); \"Ansehung\" = regard.","\"gemeinen Verstand\" = common understanding; \"gesunden Menschen Verstand\" = healthy human understanding; \"Menge\" = multitude; \"dargestelt\" = exhibited; \"Ursache\" = cause.","\"Z. E.\" (zum Exempel) rendered \"E.g.\"; \"in concreto\", \"in abstracto\", \"principium\" and \"casum sentit dominus\" are Latin in the source.","Passage breaks off mid-word (\"Wahr-\"); rendered \"tru-\" without completion. OCR \"kern\" read as \"kein\"; OCR \"findt\", \"ausricht\" kept as the German's old spellings, translated plainly."]],"poelitz-s17-005":["to, but is nevertheless a *criterium*, because my cognition can be very much falsified through subjective grounds, which do not take place in the other, although he has others. There is only one science where, without committing this error, we can rely merely on our judgment, that is mathematics. But in philosophical and discursive cognition we cannot always regard the assent of others as dispensable, because the oversights, which here are so easy, are impossible in intuitive cognitions. Providence has willed that we should hand over our judgments to the court of the shared human reason, i.e. insofar as many agree in it. Admittedly vanity also has an influence therein, but we are nevertheless taught thereby. The prejudice of antiquity is one of the most important; it arises because the ancients, so to speak, want to forge themselves into no new form. Here there is also something more general, which can also concern young people; the ground of it is the opinion that everything becomes worse with time, which however is a vain complaint. Remark. One thing is in nature that is imperishable, unalterable and an unfathomable secret, namely that the *species* always remain the same, although the *individua* change. The consideration of the subjective causes of the use of the understanding and of reason is the explanation of how it happens that one makes use of one's understanding thus and not otherwise. Logic shows how we ought to make use of it, not how we make use of it. Logic does not look to whether the understanding is corrupted or not. The doctrine of prejudices accordingly belongs properly not in logic but in anthropology. One takes it along here, however, because it can be very useful in the use of the logical instructions. It is a particular appearance that we esteem a thing too much if we previously believed it was not worthy of esteem, just as conversely, if one expects much of a thing and afterward does not find it so, one lowers it far more than it deserves. — The prejudice of antiquity springs from a delusion cited above, that everything worsens. — Sometimes the prejudice springs precisely from an opposite cause. These prejudices often spring from a disadvantageous judgment about the same. There is admiration mixed with astonishment; the latter arises when one finds something there where one does not seek it, and admiration is the general sensation when we find something very great. Meanwhile it often happens that astonishment degenerates into an admiration, although they are of different kinds, which also takes place here. Of the ancients we have for the most part a disadvantageous judgment according to the circumstances of the time in which they were. If we find something in them that we did not seek; then we are astonished at it, or we hold them to be cleverer than they actually are, from which admiration arises and then it becomes prejudice — Yet another cause of the prejudice of antiquity is this: the knowledge that we have of the ancients is with us scholarship, which always procures itself an esteem; from this arise inclination and favor toward the ancients themselves, so that the study one has devoted to it may not be empty after all.",["The passage begins mid-sentence (\"zu, ist aber doch...\"); translated as it stands. Stray OCR marks after \"discur-\" omitted and the word rejoined (discursive).","\"Beistimmung\" = assent; \"Versehn\" (plural, die Versehn) = oversights; \"gemeinschaftlichen\" rendered \"shared\" to keep it distinct from \"gemein\" = common.","\"die Alten\" rendered \"the ancients\" (could also be \"the old\"); \"sich ... schmieden wollen\" kept literally as reflexive (\"want to forge themselves\"); OCR \"kerne\" read as \"keine\".","\"Anmerkung Eins ist in der Natur, was...\" kept ambiguous: \"Remark\" may be a heading; \"Eins\" rendered \"One thing\".","\"Erscheinung\" = appearance; \"Empfindung\" = sensation; \"besondere\" = particular; \"Kenntniß\" = knowledge; \"Art\" = kind; \"Wahn\" = delusion (not in glossary).","\"Bewunderung\" = admiration, \"Verwunderung\" = astonishment (\"sich verwundern\" = \"be astonished\"); \"Achtung\" = esteem; \"nichtige\" = vain; \"nachteilig\" = disadvantageous.","\"über daßelbe\" (about the same) kept as in German; its referent is not given in the passage. \"species\" and \"individua\" are Latin in the source; \"criterium\" likewise.","\"phüosophischen\" read as \"philosophischen\"; \"entpringen\", \"wirds\", \"denn\" (= dann) normalized silently."]],"poelitz-s17-006":["The proposition that comes from the ancients can in itself be very common and poor, yet it shows erudition and wide reading. This extends even to distance of place, if e.g. someone cites what Confucius said; this gives the proposition itself a favor. We have a ground for judging favorably of antiquity, without a prejudice lying beneath it, but it is only a ground for moderated esteem, which we only too often exceed. Namely, since time as it were sifts everything and only what has inner worth preserves itself, we also have only the best writings of the ancients, and we err if we believe they all wrote thus. They have no absolute worth, but only a relative one, even the poor ones that have been lost; especially with the philosophers one makes this relative worth into an absolutely high one, so there is the prejudice our descendants will think we were giants in understanding, and we stand in just the same relation with antiquity. This brings us to entrust ourselves to the passive guidance of antiquity or to neglect the use of our talents. — At times antiquity also fell, and the prejudice in favor of it, namely at the beginning of this century, when the famous Fontenelle took the side of the moderns. In presentation they are and remain our teachers, and are to be retained with this aim, but not as *tresoriers* of the sciences and cognitions. What they could teach has long since been searched out, and to return to them yet again is a leading away of the understanding from the right path. — A third cause of the prejudice for the ancients is gratitude, that they broke the path for us to many items of knowledge, and it seems fair to show them honor for this, where we then often exceed the measure. To this there comes still the consciousness that we too will one day grow old. The last cause of the prejudice for the ancients is envy toward contemporaries. He who cannot take on the moderns holds them in contempt in comparison with the ancients, so that they cannot elevate themselves above him. All this gives us to recognize on what illusion the high praise of the ancients rests.",["Satz rendered 'proposition' throughout; Kenntnisse rendered 'knowledge' (plural 'items of knowledge'), Erkenntnisse 'cognitions'; Verstand 'understanding'.","'Gunst' rendered 'favor'; 'Vorurteil' 'prejudice'; 'Bewustseyn' 'consciousness'; 'Illusion' (not Schein) 'illusion'.","'tresoriers' is French (treasurers) in the original, kept in italics.","'Ableitung des Verstandes von der rechten Bahn' rendered literally as 'a leading away of the understanding from the right path'; 'noch in sie zurückzukehren' kept literal ('to return to them yet again'), 'noch' is obscure.","'fiel auch das Altertum und das Vorurteil' is syntactically elliptical; left as it stands. 'Dies geht so gar auf' rendered literally as 'extends even to'.","OCR: 'aUe' read as 'alle'; 'Vorurteil unsere Nachkommen werden denken' left without a complementizer as in the German; 'Aufnehmen' in 'es ... aufnehmen' rendered 'take on'."]],"poelitz-s17-007":["Remark Among all knowing none puffs up more than the philological, because here there is a certain universality of cognition, even if it is only historical. Philosophy strikes pride down and brings about misology even sooner. The philologists, however, do not bring the understanding any further.",["OCR 'Erkennt -nißes' rejoined as 'Erkenntnißes'. 'Wißen' rendered 'knowing' (Wissen is glossed 'knowledge' for the noun; the infinitive-noun here kept as 'knowing' to retain the verbal sense).","'Anmerkung' rendered 'Remark'. 'Misologie' rendered 'misology'."]],"poelitz-s17-008":["The prejudice of novelty is the opposite of the previous one. If a human being's cognition is capable of an extension, then it is natural that we can place more confidence in the more recent than in the ancients, we are in advantageous circumstances — that is a correct preliminary judgment but not yet sufficient. To the prejudice there comes in addition the inclination toward the new, because we are attuned to the way of thinking of our age, not because the understanding has insight into it; this prejudice is, however, also made very suspect, because one must always believe that an impulse to innovation has driven the author (as often really happens) to set against a truth long since proved a *Sehern* [?], which also maintains itself for a while. — The prejudice of the accepted system of doctrine is still to be noted. A system of doctrine is the derivation of many items of knowledge from one *principio*; for this we get a *praedilection*, since we overlook many errors. The *praedilection* is good if the system of doctrine does not rest on false *principien*. Remark Propositions are not always false because they come from a prejudice, often they are correct, only the *modus acquirendi* (as the jurists say) is illegal, I have thereby paved a broad way for false cognition, and the way is often more important than the cognition itself.",["'Sehern' is a doubtful OCR reading (perhaps 'Schein'/'Scheine' or another word); left untranslated and marked.","'Lehrgebäude' rendered 'system of doctrine' (Lehre = doctrine; Gebäude = edifice); 'Kenntniße' 'items of knowledge'; 'Zutrauen' 'confidence'.","OCR 'prineipio'/'prineipien' read as 'principio'/'principien'; Latin/Latinized terms (*principio*, *praedilection*, *modus acquirendi*) kept in italics.","'die Art' rendered 'the way' (Art = way; manner; kind); 'Erkenntniß' 'cognition' throughout.","'Neuere' rendered 'the more recent'; 'Neue' 'the new'; 'Neuerungstrieb' 'impulse to innovation'."]],"poelitz-s18-001":["On probable cognition.",["Heading. 'wahrscheinlich' rendered 'probable' (Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability); 'Erkenntniß' 'cognition'."]],"poelitz-s18-002":["Here, as in all logics, there is a mistake in the expression. Instead of its being supposed to read, the cognition of the probable, it reads the probable cognition. My cognition of a probable object can be certain and correct, only I hold the object to be probable through it. The cognition of the probable is good, but the logic of it cannot be a pure one, because there are already objects there. That on whose side there are more grounds of happening than on the other, that I hold to be probable. All probability one can express as a fraction, the denominator is the number of all possible cases, the numerator the number of the actual cases. Probability is therefore the taking to be true from insufficient grounds, which however have a greater ratio to the sufficient ones than the grounds of the opposite. Probable cognitions are also practically sufficient, but not logically. Plausibility is the validity of a cognition from insufficient grounds, insofar as they are greater than the grounds of the opposite. Here, then, I do not compare them with the sufficient grounds, but only with the grounds of the opposite, I do not know, then, how much belongs to certainty, as with probable cognition. Plausibility gives a provisional, probability a determining but not apodictic judgment. One can also express probability mathematically: namely: it is more than half of certainty. Half of certainty is doubtful. — The degree of taking to be true in the case of probability is objectively valid, in the case of plausibility subjectively valid. With every probability there must always be a standard, namely certainty, but this is different with different human beings, one demands more for it than the other. If the grounds of taking to be true are homogeneous: then the degree of taking to be true depends on the number of them, or they must then be numbered; if they are heterogeneous: then they must be weighed. With the first one is soon finished, there they are all in agreement, but with the latter it is harder. Probability, then, cannot be estimated in philosophical cognitions, but well in mathematics. Much has been said of a *logica probabilium*, but this is not possible, for the very ground just cited. With probability one abstracts the rules of the understanding from the usual procedure and assumes them as logical rules, but they are nothing but rules abstracted from the usual practice of the understanding and brought into general formulas. The cognition of the probable must be certain, i.e. I must be certain that it is probable. A merely probable cognition one must never follow, but it can drive us to investigate the grounds. A counter-ground of taking to be true is a hindrance. Subjective hindrance is doubt. Objective hindrance is objection. Doubt is not yet a ground for holding something to be false, but only a ground of the suspension of judgment. In common usage of speech *dubium* (objective ground) and *dubitatio* (subjective ground) are often confused. A subjective ground of doubt is only for me, but the objective one must hold for everyone else. — Scruple is a hindrance of taking to be true, where I do not know whether it is objective or subjective, if it is merely subjective, then it is not valid, because it is mere illusion, if we know that it is merely subjective, then it also cannot deceive us. The cause is inclination, habit etc. etc. If we find, e.g., a new judgment and at the same time have the strongest grounds for it: we still cannot come to terms with it, for it is scruple, but if I see that here something subjective is with me, it ceases to be scruple, then I have discovered the illusion. In many cases, however, we cannot bring it so far, because we cannot always compare our cognition with the object, but only the cognitions with one another. With every scruple there is a hindrance in the subject, only I want to know whether it is merely in the subject. A scruple cannot be removed until it has been made into an objection, and it is a great talent to guess beforehand scruples that could come up. Through objections I do not want to refute, but only to weaken certainty, and certainty is brought through them to distinctness and completeness. Everyone must therefore hear objections and no one can be certain of a matter if counter-grounds have not been excited, through which it can be determined how far one still is from certainty, or how near one is to it. — A knot (objection) can be cut through, if one shows that one has no need to occupy oneself with the untying of it. E.g. how one could reconcile the evil in the world with the wisdom of God. — One must distinguish: to answer an objection and to reply to it. — Even to the objections against the most ungrounded opinions, he who has them will be able to reply something, otherwise he becomes ridiculous, but whether he will be able to answer them is another question. To answer means to show how the scruple arose. If this does not happen, then it is turned away, but not canceled. In Latin the words are *verisimilitudo* and *probabilitas*, the first would be better translated by \"scheinbar\" in opposition to the true, and it concerns only the cognition. The object is either *probabel* or *improbabel*.",["Erkenntniß = cognition throughout; Object = object; Gewißheit/gewiß = certainty/certain (not in the glossary); Fürwahrhalten = taking to be true; 'halten für' rendered 'hold to be'.","Scheinbarkeit = 'plausibility' (not in the glossary); scheinbar is left in German where the word itself is mentioned. Schein = illusion. The Latin pair verisimilitudo/probabilitas stands in the text.","zureichend and hinreichend are both 'sufficient'; unzureichend = 'insufficient'. weggenommen and aufgehoben are rendered 'removed' and 'canceled' (the latter literally 'lifted').","beantworten ('answer') and auf ihn antworten ('reply to it') are kept as two verbs; Einwurf = objection; Hindernis = hindrance; Skrupel = scruple.","OCR slips silently corrected: dasObject, Objeete, Zehler (Zähler), fem (fern), WTenn, probabüium, last (läßt), Erkennt nißen, betrift. The stray ■ before 'Auch auf die Einwürfe' is omitted.","'so fem sie größer sind' read as 'so fern'; 'denn ist es Skrupel' is kept literally as 'for it is scruple' (the German could also be read as 'dann'). 'oder sie müßen denn numerirt werden' is kept as awkward as the German.","'zusammen räumen' read as zusammenräumen ('reconcile'). 'Ein groß Talent', 'ein größer Verhältniß' are left as uninflected in the source."]],"poelitz-s18-003":["We now come to a principle of doubt in relation to the principle of asserting. He who, with his cognition, has to choose assertion as the aim is a dogmatist. He, however, who has the maxim of treating cognition with the aim that one make it uncertain and show the impossibility of arriving at certainty is a skeptic. [Greek word garbled in the OCR] means as much as *indagare*. The first skeptics therefore investigated the matters and suspended their judgment for that long. Skepticism was the maxim of the suspension of judgment for the sake of the investigation.",["The Greek word before 'heist so viel als indagare' is too garbled in the OCR ('CTKayacTÖoü') to be sure of; it is not reconstructed here (perhaps a form of σκέπτεσθαι).","Grundsatz = principle; Behauptung/behaupten = assertion/assert; Absicht = aim; Erkenntniß = cognition; Gewißheit = certainty.","'wälen' read as wählen; 'zur Absicht zu wälen hat' kept literally ('has to choose ... as the aim'). Sachen = 'matters' (distinct from Ding = thing).","'so lang' rendered 'for that long'; the German is elliptical."]],"poelitz-s18-004":["What is a maxim? When objective rules become subjective, i.e. when we act according to them; then that is a maxim. To doubt by maxim is something other than to doubt the insight of the ground in a thing; I doubt my attentiveness, whether I will have fulfilled everything that the understanding demands of me, before I am to make a determining judgment. One has maxims of the dogmatic, of the skeptical way of thinking. The middle one is the critical way of thinking, i.e. one treats something for a time problematically, until one arrives at complete certainty in it. The first method is very harmful to the sciences because it gives free rein to all errors, if only they have some illusion. Skepticism is also not good, for there can still be something true in cognition, the critical procedure is the best, it arises from mistrust of our judgment, especially when one is concerned that subjective grounds are falsely taken for objective, and since this often happens: so this method always has ground for itself. The skeptical method is very useful to the critical, namely when one has accepted a truth: then one puts oneself in the place of the opponent, and seeks out all possible grounds to overturn his proposition and so to find the truth. Often it happens that one is brought to the opposite opinion. — Absolute and brutal skepticism says: there is no sufficient criterion of truth, in order to be able to say that it must have a standard. The standard must be the sufficient criterion of truth, so it does have truth after all; it therefore contradicts itself. In mathematics and physics skepticism is not necessary, for in the latter experience sets us right at once. In mathematics it is not so in any case, on account of evidence. But the cognition that is not mathematical or empirical, hence philosophical, has occasioned skepticism. Pure philosophy insofar as it is speculative is called metaphysics. From there one went further, and put mistrust even in experience and also in mathematics, for which one made use of the most ridiculous grounds. The skeptics had the proposition: Everything is so uncertain that it is uncertain whether everything is uncertain. This annihilates skepticism itself. They called it καθαρτικόν (means of purification). The first skeptics must have been men of insight, for to discover the conflict of reason, to spy out the grounds that can be adduced against a proposition, is difficult.",["Maxime zu zweifeln: rendered literally as \"to doubt by maxim\"; the construction is elliptical in the German and is left so.","Denkart / Denkungsart = \"way of thinking\" (Art = way); Saz = proposition; Grund = ground; Gewisheit = certainty; Schein = illusion; Erkenntniß = cognition; Meinung = opinion.","OCR \"sein- schädlich\" read as \"sehr schädlich\"; \"dochwas\" as \"doch was\"; \"drin\" taken as \"darin\" (\"in it\"); \"Irtümern\" = errors.","The Greek word is garbled in the OCR (\"KO0apKTiKÖv\"); read as καθαρτικόν, with the gloss \"Reinigungsmittel\" as given in the text. Reading uncertain.","Wiederstreit = conflict; Weltweisheit does not occur here, \"Philosophie\" = philosophy; Kriterium is rendered \"criterion\" per the glossary."]],"poelitz-s19-001":["On Hypotheses.",[]],"poelitz-s19-002":["The author defines it through an opinion that is philosophical, i.e. that is used to explain appearances in the world. Opinion does not explain like hypothesis. Actually it is a taking to be true of the judgment of the truth of a ground, for the sake of the sufficiency of the sequels, or if one assumes something as true because from given sequels a sufficient ground can be drawn from it, or the taking to be true of a presupposition as ground. If something is mediately certain, then it is true as sequel; if something is taken to be true as a ground, then that is hypothesis. I actually assume a thing without proof, if it is a sufficient ground to derive sequels from it, then it is assumed as true on account of the sequels and not on that account, because it itself is the sequel of a true cognition. If the sequel is true, then the ground is not always true, for for the same sequel there can be several grounds. All taking to be true of a hypothesis grounds itself on this, that it is sufficient as ground to explain other cognitions from it as sequels. We infer therefore here from the truth of the sequel to the truth of the ground. Above it was remarked that this is indeed a criterion of truth, but yet not sufficient. Only then is this inference completely correct, if all possible sequels are true, for if there were something false in the ground, then a false sequel would also have to be able to be drawn from it. But because we can never determine all possible sequels, this also gives no secure inference. We see therefore that hypotheses always remain hypotheses, for I can have forgotten a small circumstance, which experience showed and which perhaps would not let itself be explained from the hypothesis, and we actually can never arrive at complete certainty of the same although there is a ground of probability. If, however, all sequels that have come before me let themselves be explained from the hypothesis: then the probability of the hypothesis grows and there is no ground why I should not assume that all possible sequels will let themselves be explained; — I infer therefore all possible sequels are true, they are thus assumed as true not apodictically, but *per inductionem*. Something in a hypothesis must still be apodictic, namely: a.) The possibility of the presupposition, e.g. If we, for the explanation",["Folge is rendered \"sequel\" throughout per the glossary (Folge = sequel); Grund = ground; Fürwahrhalten = taking to be true; Zulänglichkeit = \"sufficiency\" (zureichend = \"sufficient\" is a different word); Schluß = inference.","\"Opinion does not explain like hypothesis\" and \"if it is a sufficient ground to derive sequels from it\" are kept in the German's loose construction; \"Actually\" renders eigentlich, \"actually\" renders würklich (wirklich).","OCR \"liier\" read as \"hier\"; \"grün dt\" as \"gründet\"; \"Schlus\" = Schluß; the passage breaks off mid-sentence (continued in the next passage).","The sign \"criterion\" renders Criterium."]],"poelitz-s19-003":["of earthquakes and fire-spewing mountains a subterranean fire",["Fragment of a line; continues the sentence of the preceding passage."]],"poelitz-s19-004":["assume, then it must still be possible that it such a fire",["The syntax is broken: \"daß es ein solches Feuer\" lacks its verb here and the next fragment begins with \"könne\" (\"could\"); a word such as \"geben\" appears to have dropped out. Nothing has been supplied."]],"poelitz-s19-005":["could, or it must at least be a hot body.",["See previous note on the gap before \"könne\"."]],"poelitz-s19-006":["but another the earth is an animal, where the inner juices",["The German has no verb of saying after \"ein anderer\"; none is supplied. The transcript is probably defective here."]],"poelitz-s19-007":["bring about warmth, then that is not possible. Actualities can",["Würklichkeiten (Wirklichkeiten) = \"actualities\" (glossary: reality / actuality)."]],"poelitz-s19-008":["invent but not possibilities, these must be certain, the",["The subject of \"erdichten\" is not given (the line begins \"Würklichkeiten kann\"); left as it stands."]],"poelitz-s19-009":["touchstone of possibility is experience.",[]],"poelitz-s19-010":["Consequence. From the assumed ground the sequel must flow correctly, otherwise it is a chimera, unity is required, i.e. there must be only one hypothesis. If I must already take many, then it loses much of its probability. For I infer: the more sequels flow from a hypothesis the more probable it is, the fewer, the more improbable, e.g. Tycho de Brahe's hypothesis: that the sun, planets and fixed stars moved around the earth did not suffice for many appearances, he therefore always had to assume several. Then it is already to be guessed that this is not the genuine ground. The Copernican system is on the contrary a hypothesis from which everything that has occurred up to now has let itself be explained. One does not need here *hypotheses subsidiarias*, those are such as",["\"Consequenz\" is kept as \"Consequence\" (a Latinate heading, distinct from Folge = sequel and Folgerung = consequence).","\"Denn ists schon zu errathen\" is rendered \"Then it is already to be guessed\"; \"Denn\" may also be read as \"for\" (denn) or as \"then\" (dann); the ambiguity is not resolved.","*hypotheses subsidiarias* is Latin in the original; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s19-011":["are assumed, in order to come to the aid of one already assumed.",[]],"poelitz-s19-012":["In metaphysics hypotheses are something absurd.",["ungereimt = \"absurd\" (literally \"unrhymed\", incongruous)."]],"poelitz-s19-013":["Nowhere, however, are there more hypotheses than in the doctrine of nature: they",["OCR \"H3q:>othesen\" read as \"Hypothesen\". Naturlehre = \"doctrine of nature\" (Lehre = doctrine)."]],"poelitz-s19-014":["also hold among the commentators of the classical authors.",["\"gelten\" rendered \"hold\"; \"are valid\" would also be possible (gültig = valid)."]],"poelitz-s19-015":["The author now speaks of persuasion. We have already mentioned it above.",["Ueberredung = persuasion."]],"poelitz-s19-016":["It is the taking to be true from insufficient grounds, of which one does not know whether they are objective or subjective. We believe something certain, and yet hold it only for persuasion, because we cannot give an account of the grounds. Many cognitions begin in that we are conscious of them only as persuasion. Upon persuasion follows reflection, i.e. we see to which cognitive power the cognition belongs, and upon that follows investigation, i.e. we see whether the grounds with regard to the object are sufficient or insufficient; with many it remains at persuasion, with some it comes to reflection, with few to investigation. Persuasions often go before conviction, but with those they are rare who know what belongs to certainty, they will",["Ueberlegung (OCR \"Ueber legung\") = reflection; Untersuchung = investigation; Ueberzeugung = conviction; Erkenntnißkraft = \"cognitive power\" (Kraft = power).","The final clause \"bei denen sind sie selten die wißen ...\" is awkward in the German and is kept so; the sentence continues in the next passage.","\"Object\" is rendered \"object\"."]],"poelitz-s19-017":["not easily confuse conviction and reflection. He is",["Ueberlegung = reflection (here as in the preceding passage)."]],"poelitz-s19-018":["least persuaded who has often been deceived. To let oneself and be able to be persuaded so easily",["\"Das sich so leicht überreden laßen und können\" is kept literally; the verb follows in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s19-019":["indicates a shallow way of thinking. One must therefore not blame everyone who does not give assent to a thing as quickly as another.",["Denkungs Art = \"way of thinking\" (Art = way); Beifall geben = \"give assent\"."]],"poelitz-s19-020":["Sciences have also already been spoken of above. A sum total of cognitions as aggregate is common cognition, and a sum total of cognitions as system is science. Every science must be able to be divided *a priori* in accordance with the principle; I must know how many parts belong to the science even before it is treated. The system rests on an idea of the whole. With cognitions as aggregate the parts come first, but with the system the idea of the whole, which makes the greatest difficulty, and one can rightly say that metaphysics is a rhapsody.",["Inbegriff = sum total; Erkenntnis = cognition; Wissenschaft = science; gemein = common; Idee = idea; Prinzip rendered 'principle' (not Grundsatz).","'dem Prinzip gemäs' could attach to 'eingeteilt' or to 'a priori eingeteilt werden können'; left as in the German.","'die die gröste Schwierigkeit macht': the relative pronoun's antecedent is ambiguous (the idea of the whole, or the whole clause); preserved."]],"poelitz-s19-021":["The author still speaks of art. Knowing and being able are different perfections. I can do something right away as soon as I know it, but some things I nevertheless cannot, even though I know it. Practice belongs to this, and that is art. The author is not right in understanding by science only cognition of reason, historical cognitions too can be scientific.",["Wissen/Wißen rendered 'knowing' in the infinitive pair, 'know' in the finite verb; the noun Wissenschaft = science.","Vollkommenheit = perfection; Vernunft Erkenntniß = 'cognition of reason' (compound; the genitive is supplied by the English form).","'ob ichs gleich weiß': 'it' is as in the German; no antecedent is stated."]],"poelitz-s20-001":["On certainty.",["Gewisheit = certainty (not in the glossary)."]],"poelitz-s20-002":["It is empirical or apodictic. Experience is either our own or that of others, which we have through testimonies. Empirical certainty is therefore certainty through one's own experience or through the testimony of others. This is historical certainty, and one can often believe the testimony of others more than one's own experience. The author speaks of mathematical certainty of the first and second rank, but he is not right, all mathematical certainty is of one and the same kind. Certainty of reason is at all times apodictic, or absolute necessity takes place there. Intuitive certainty, or that through the construction of concepts, is the mathematical, the discursive the philosophical. The author now speaks of arbitrary truths, which is a peculiar expression, we would rather say, arbitrary propositions or *positiones* are arbitrary rules of use. But it is nothing further than an imperative, it is a command of my reason, not a cognition of the thing itself. The author defines them as such truths as are true through our arbitrariness; to this he reckons the hypotheses, which are assumed arbitrarily but not as truths, rather this is first determined through involuntary sequels.",["OCR corrected silently: 'Noth wendigkeit', 'Heber' (lieber), 'willkührliehe', 'wilUüihrliche', 'willkührhch'.","'Erkenntniß Sache selbst': 'der' appears to have dropped out in the OCR; translated 'of the thing itself'. Sache rendered 'thing' (Ding is also 'thing').","Art = 'kind' in 'von einerley Art'; besonder = 'peculiar' here (glossary: particular; special); Ausdruck = expression; Gebrauch = use; Folge = sequel; bestimmen = determine.","'Willkühr' rendered 'arbitrariness', 'willkührlich' 'arbitrary'; 'unwillkührlich' 'involuntary'. 'Sie' (the author defines 'sie') refers back to 'positiones/Wahrheiten' as in the German.","'die diskursive, die philosophische' is elliptical (the discursive [is] the philosophical); preserved."]],"poelitz-s21-001":["On proofs, and indeed on the apodictic ones.",["Beweis = proof."]],"poelitz-s21-002":["They are divided into acroamatic ones, which are discursive and let themselves be expressed through words, and into mathematical ones, which are intuitive, from the construction of concepts. In a proof there is therefore:",["'acroamatische' kept as 'acroamatic'; intuitiv = intuitive; Einteilung = division."]],"poelitz-s21-003":["1.) the thing that is to be proved.",["Sache = 'thing' (see note above)."]],"poelitz-s21-004":["2.) the ground of proof and",["Beweisgrund = ground of proof (Grund = ground)."]],"poelitz-s21-005":["3.) the consequence, how the proposition follows from the ground of proof.",["Consequenz rendered 'consequence' (the German word here is the Latinate Consequenz, not Folgerung); Saz = proposition."]],"poelitz-s21-006":["One can know the ground of proof without knowing the form of the proof or the consequence. If one presents merely the ground of proof or *materiam probationis*, in disputing, then this is called disputing *per terminos medios*. Cognitions are, according to the author's definition, unprovable, which are also completely certain even without proof. In German, however, this expression signifies a thing that indeed requires a proof, but has none. An immediately certain proposition is therefore indemonstrable. All our cognitions must begin from *immediate* certain propositions. Even if ever so much is mediately, i.e. through a proof, certain: yet something must also be indemonstrable or immediately certain. The proof is either *directe*, which is at the same time positive, which proves that the proposition is true, or an apagogic one, which is negative, which refutes the opposite, or shows that the opposite of the proposition is false. Apagogic proofs are evident in mathematics, in philosophy one is gladly relieved of them; for the positive ones show me not only the thing but also the sources of the truth. The apagogic proof shows only the opposite, if I infer from the falsity of the same to the truth of my proposition. If the apagogic proof is to be valid: then the propositions must be opposed *contradictorie diametraliter*, not *contrarie*, otherwise they can both be false. A proof that is the ground of mathematical certainty is called demonstration, and a proof that is the ground of philosophical certainty is called acroamatic proof. The author now speaks of the sources of cognition and reckons among them cognition of reason and belief. Belief, however, belongs to experience, for I believe on account of the testimony of others, and that I must yet experience,",["Passage ends with a comma in the source; it breaks off and is not completed.","'unerweißlich' rendered 'unprovable'; the German word is then glossed by the text itself as 'a thing that requires a proof but has none'. 'indemonstrabel' rendered 'indemonstrable'.","'Dieser Ausdruk' and 'der Autor's Definition': 'Erkenntniße ... die auch ohne Beweis' retains the German's loose relative construction.","'sich die Säze ... opponirt seyn' is as printed (grammatically irregular); rendered literally as 'must be opposed'. 'immediate' (Latin/German adverbial form) italicized; 'directe' italicized.","Beweis/Beweiß both 'proof'; schließen = infer (Schluß = inference); gelten = be valid; Quelle = source; Glaube = belief; Erfahrung = experience; 'Erkenntniß Quellen' = 'sources of cognition'.","'Beweisgrund oder materiam probationis' and 'per terminos medios' are Latin in the German; italicized."]],"poelitz-s21-007":["On historical belief.",["Glaube = belief."]],"poelitz-s21-008":["The author relates belief merely to testimony, but that is false. We distinguish the two expressions to believe something and to believe someone; in the first, belief is distinguished only from knowledge, the second presupposes that another has said it. To believe something relates to cognition and to the ground of taking to be true, it may also be from reason, if the grounds of taking to be true are insufficient for the logical use, i.e. for the pure understanding, but are sufficient for the practical use, i.e. for application, then that is belief. There is no theoretical belief of reason, for in speculation reason gives us either knowing or nothing, also opining but not conviction. But there is practical belief of reason — logical grounds need not be sufficient, but reason can, with regard to our interest, have grounds for accepting something as true. Propositions of reason are in themselves, to be sure, no object of belief, but in mathematics it is surely all right, because errors in application do not hide so easily. If we believe something because we believe another, then that is historical belief, or when one takes something to be true merely for the sake of testimony. There is a wholly grounded belief of reason which, however, can never ascend to knowledge, and it can in practical aim be as strong a taking to be true as the greatest certainty, only the logical *requisita* are lacking to it. Historical belief can be an actual knowledge and need not be distinguished from the same, but belief of reason must be distinguished. If we *contradistinguish* belief from knowledge, then we must take it for a logically insufficient but practically sufficient taking to be true. For historical belief a witness is required, whom one can believe historically, if he is of infallible reason or recounts his own experience. In philosophical cognition we can take nothing to be true except the testimony of God. All *citationes* have a historical belief and the invocation of witnesses is entirely necessary with regard to the empirical, because we cannot experience everything ourselves. For the credibility of a witness there is required competence, that he has been able to say the truth, or *authenticitas*, and sincerity, that he has been willing to say it, or *integritas*. He must be competent, in that he has been able to conduct the experience, and he must also be able to declare it so that one understands him. It is not as easy to conduct experiences as one believes. As for the sincerity of the witness, with the common man one is not to rely on it, he is too crude to place a value in truth. The old times were thus attuned, and almost all old writers have not been very punctilious in truth. E.g. Herodotus, Livy. In this the newer times have much ahead of the old. We are more upright in our way of thinking and compelled to confess the truth, because otherwise we lose our honorable name. This change took place at the end of the previous century — and the exactness of testimony finds place especially in experimental physics, which also extended to history, to which printing and the newspapers contributed much, which are means to refute lies soon. With sincerity one must investigate whether the witness has had an interest in departing from the truth, but this is so various that one can hardly judge it, that which we do not hold at all for an interest is one with another. Witnesses are divided into ear- and eyewitnesses, the latter attests his own experience of a thing, the former his experience of the testimonies of others. — With Greek legend and tradition there is no proper witness. With the former one believes not on account of the validity of the witness, but on account of the multitude of the same, with the latter there is a multitude of subordinate witnesses. If unbelief is a blame then belief must be moral. In Latin one is not capable of translating it. — Belief is either theoretical, or practical, i.e. moral, him one calls unbelieving who has no moral belief, i.e. who has no moral interest, and this one can demand of everyone, and this unbelief is a blame, namely when a human being does not believe in virtue, and this unbelief is harmful because he thereby lets go all resolve toward virtue, in that he believes that there were none.",["Glaube/glauben = belief/believe throughout; Wißen/Wissen = knowledge (*scientia* not printed here); Fürwahrhalten = taking to be true; Zeuge = witness, Zeugnis = testimony; Erfahrung = experience, erfahren = experience (v.); anstellen = conduct.","'puren Verstand': pur (not rein) rendered 'pure'; 'Vernunftglaube' rendered 'belief of reason' (Vernunftsäze: 'propositions of reason') to keep the genitive compound.","'dürfen nicht' (twice) rendered 'need not'; English 'may not' would be the other reading ('must not'); the German is itself open to both.","'auch Meinen aber nicht Überzeugung': 'opining' (infinitive of meinen, cf. Meinung = opinion); 'auch' could mean 'also' or 'even'; I have kept 'also'.","'gehts wol an' rendered 'it is surely all right'; 'wol' could also be 'perhaps/well enough'.","'Tüchtigkeit' = competence; 'avthenticitaet' and 'integritaet' (OCR/Kant's spelling) given as *authenticitas*, *integritas*; 'declariren' = 'declare' (cf. Erklärung = declaration).","'beim andern eines' kept as 'is one with another' (the other's case it is one); 'und den kann man von jedem fodern': 'den' could be 'moral belief' or the person, so 'this' keeps it open; 'dieser/jener' rendered 'the latter/the former'.","'Tadel' = 'blame'; 'welches Mittel sind': neuter 'welches' with plural verb kept as 'which are means'; 'f indt' read as 'findet', 'betritt' as 'betrifft' (OCR/orthography)."]],"poelitz-s22-001":["On the reconciliation of reason and experience.",["'Vereinbarung/vereinbaren' = reconciliation/reconcile; the sense 'unite, bring into agreement' is also possible in the German."]],"poelitz-s22-002":["If something is concluded from experience by means of reason: then one cannot say that they are reconciled, but experience is utilized by reason. — In order that experience-ground cognition become, reason belongs to it. Experience is then the sufficient ground and reason avails itself of the same for cognitions. Reason is the faculty through which we can use experience as ground for cognitions, but then reason and experience are not reconciled, but when one faculty of cognition gives the other *complementum ad sufficientiam*. If a cognition from experience and reason is insufficient and one supplements the other, then reason and experience are reconciled. So it is with hypotheses.",["'DamitErfahrungsgrund Erkenntniß werde' is garbled in the OCR (words run together); translated as it stands, without emendation. A reading 'Damit Erfahrung Grund [der] Erkenntniß werde' is conceivable but not certain.","'denn werden aber' translated 'but then': 'denn' appears to stand for 'dann'; doubtful.","gefolgert = concluded (folgern); genüzt = utilized; zureichend = sufficient; Erkenntnißvermögen = faculty of cognition."]],"poelitz-s23-001":["On practical cognition.",["Erkenntniß = cognition."]],"poelitz-s23-002":["A cognition is called practical in counter-relation to the theoretical but also to the speculative. If a proposition is an imperative: then it is practical. The ought signifies here a free action that is good in a certain aim, and a proposition that expresses such an action is an imperative. This imperative is practical in opposition to the theoretical, for the latter says not what ought to happen, but what has happened. Practical cognition is also opposed to the speculative, and here it can also be theoretical, if only imperatives can be derived, for then the cognition is called objectively practical, namely when it contains the ground for possible imperatives. Remark. What is speculative is always theoretical, but not conversely, for the theoretical can be theoretical in another aim. — A speculative cognition would thus be one from which no rules of the relation can be derived, or that is no ground for possible imperatives. E.g., in theology there are such merely speculative cognitions that have no influence on the conduct of life, e.g., whether God is present in space, or whether omnipresence is only an influence on all creatures, without his being determined through space. One can also define thus: A cognition is theoretical if it does not indicate what ought to happen, but contains the cognition of an object, as it is. The use of cognition is either theoretical or practical. E.g., morals is a practical cognition, yet the use of it can nevertheless be speculative, and only in expectation of the practical use do our cognitions get a worth, and everything speculative looks out in the distance to the practical. (Speculation is also good in the aim, because the understanding is practiced for practical investigations.) This worth can be merely mediate, or also immediate, then it is dignity. We actually know as yet no thing that would have dignity, other than the human being, for he does not belong to the means to other ends, like all other things. All knowledge and what the human being acquires has yet only a mediate worth, and all this must relate to an immediate worth. If the will makes good use of the acquired knowledge, then it is worth more than all cognition. The dignity of the human being rests therefore on morality, and this we call *kat' exochēn* practical, and the part of philosophy that treats morals is called practical philosophy, although in the other parts too there is something practical.",["Gegenverhältnis rendered literally as 'counter-relation'; Saz = proposition (Satz); Sollen = 'the ought'.","Absicht = aim in both 'in gewißer Absicht' and 'in einer andern Absicht'; the phrase is left as bare as the German.","'denn' (in 'denn heist...' and 'denn ist er Würde') is rendered 'for' in the first case and 'then' in the second; the German could be read either way in both places.","'Regeln des Verhältnißes': Verhältniß = relation; the referent of 'the relation' is left open as in the German.","Kenntniße = 'knowledge' (plural sense), distinct from Erkenntniß = 'cognition'; kennen = know; Mittel = means; Zwecke = ends; mittelbar/unmittelbar = mediate/immediate; Gebrauch = use; Werth = worth.","'Wüle' read as Wille (OCR slip); 'Imparativen' read as Imperativen; 'Algegenwart' = Allgegenwart (omnipresence).","'Korr 'e^ox^l v' is garbled OCR; read conjecturally as Greek κατ' ἐξοχήν (*kat' exochēn*), 'par excellence'; the reading is doubtful.","'nur' in 'in Erwartung des praktischen Gebrauchs bekommen unsere Erkenntniße nur einen Werth' could modify 'in expectation...' or 'a worth'; left ambiguous."]],"poelitz-s23-003":["The author has yet a special section, on the use of our understanding and of reason in opposition to the speculative use. This belongs not at all in logic, it considers merely the form of thinking, and can have no propositions except those that rest on principles a priori. Since, however, everything practical has our will and the various ends as its aim, which we must cognize through observations, the practical is distinguished from the speculative according to content, this we see from the treatment itself: The author speaks of gratification, desire, etc. etc. which all does not belong here. — Further there is here the division of the cognitions into dead ones that do not move our will and living ones that move it. It is good, only the bounds of logic are also overstepped. We therefore leave this section out. It belongs in psychology.",["Säze = propositions (Satz); 'principien' rendered 'principles' (Grundsatz/principium).","'Vergnügen' rendered 'gratification'; 'Begierde' = desire; 'Abhandelung' = treatment; 'Grenzen' = bounds; 'Einteilung' = division.","'zur Absicht hat' rendered 'has ... as its aim' (Absicht = aim).","'betracht' read as betrachtet (OCR/abbreviation); Verstand = understanding, Vernunft = reason."]],"poelitz-s24-001":["On concepts.",["Begriffe = concepts (conceptus)."]],"poelitz-s24-002":["Up to now we have spoken of cognition according to the various kinds, now the author divides the cognitions into their elements, which fairly should already have happened sooner. 1.) The first perfection, says the author, is when a cognition becomes representation and concept. 2.) when concepts are combined and judgments thus arise, 3.) when judgments are combined and inferences thus arise. The ancients said: *quot sunt mentis operationes? tres, apprehensio simplex, Judicium, et ratiocinium.* In order to explain a concept we note, *repraesentatio* is the first and most general and cannot be explained, 2.) a representation with consciousness is *perceptio*, perception 3.) representation insofar as it is considered as related to an object is *cognitio*, or perception insofar as it is objective. I must therefore also become conscious of the object. We have many representations that relate to no object, e.g., all inner sensations. They relate to the subject. If someone speaks to me: then I have a representation related to the object, thus cognition; if he screams so much that my ears hurt; then that is sensation and I feel my state. Cognition is either *intuitus* or *conceptus*; *intuitus*, when I have only single representations, *conceptus* when I have representations that are common to many, or *repraesentatio communis*. *Conceptus est repraesentatio communis* because the concept goes to the mark of the object and thus represents the object *mediate* through the mark, and this mark can be common to many things. *Intuitus* is *repraesentatio singularis*, for it is always a single object of the senses.",["Latin left as in the source: *quot sunt mentis operationes? tres...* = 'how many are the operations of the mind? three: simple apprehension, judgment, and ratiocination'.","'Erkennt niß' and 'beAvust' are OCR breaks, read as Erkenntniß and bewußt.","Arten rendered 'kinds' (Art = way; species; kind; manner); Vollkommenheit = perfection; Schlüße = inferences; Urteile = judgments; Merkmal = mark (nota).","Gegenstand and Object are both 'object'; Gegenstand is used in this passage in most places, Object in 'bezogen aufs Object' and 'kein Object'.","'billig' rendered 'fairly'; 'einzelne' rendered 'single'; 'Erklären' rendered 'explain' (Erklärung = explanation; definition).","'perception' appears in the German in Latin-like spelling without the Latin ending; rendered as 'perception' alongside *perceptio*."]],"poelitz-s24-003":["We always begin from intuitions. Every *conceptus* is either *empiricus*, if it has arisen from the senses and has received merely universality through the understanding, or has come about through comparison of the objects of experience, or *purus*, which does not arise through the senses. In this division what matters is the origin, so it belongs not in logic but in metaphysics. We have many empirical concepts, namely of color, sensation, etc. etc. *Purus* was one that arises independently of experience from the understanding. These are again such whose objects can be given in experience, e.g. of substances, causes, effects etc. etc., or they are such whose objects can be given in no experience, that is a concept of mere reason. *Conceptus purus est notio.* *Notio* is thus after all more than *conceptus*, but in German there is nevertheless no corresponding expression. *Notio*, whose object can be presented *in concreto*, is *notio intellectus*; where the object can be given in no experience it is idea. Idea one can call concept of reason, for through reason I think something *a priori*. Otherwise one speaks of the idea of the red color etc., but that is false. — The whole of morals rests on ideas, for pure virtue is possible in no experience. A *notion* is sensitive, whose objects can be made sensible, if not in empirical then still in pure intuition. E.g. the concept of a triangle. — How do concepts arise, or how can representations become concepts? This I can ask only in logic, *per reflectionem, comparationem, separationem seu abstractionem*. I reflect on things, i.e. I become conscious little by little of various representations, or I compare various representations with my consciousness; if that is so, then I compare them among one another, that is *comparation*; where I find identity of consciousness, that I separate or abstract from the rest; thus I have a concept, e.g. I see a spruce, willow or linden, I see that they have a trunk, branches and leaves which are different, one has more branches than the other etc.; I now attend to that which they have in common, as to the trunk, branches and leaves, from the figure I abstract, so I get the concept of tree. Remark. One does not use the word abstract rightly in logic. We must not say *abstrahere aliquid* but *ab aliquo*. In the previous example we do not abstract the trunk, the branches etc., those we separate, but we abstract from the figure of the leaves etc. etc. Abstraction thus adds nothing to our cognition, but rather limits it. Attention to the similar is called wit. In order to make concepts one must thus be able to compare, attend and abstract, e.g. if with scarlet I think only the red color: so I abstract from the cloth, I abstract from that, and think it to myself only as a being, so I abstract still from more, and such a concept is called abstract.",["OCR corrected silently: meineni → meinem; Hegt → liegt (in 005); solchenBegrif, abstahir; Algemeinheit rendered 'universality' (allgemein).","Begriff = concept throughout; Vorstellung = representation; Anschauung = intuition; Erkenntniß = cognition; Gegenstand = object; Wesen = being; Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason.","'conceptus', 'notio', 'notio intellectus', etc. kept in Latin in italics; 'a priori' and 'in concreto' likewise italicized; 'notion' (German 'notion' in lower case, 'Eine notion ist sensitiv') left as the Latinate word.","'Purus war ein solcher der…': the past tense 'was' is in the text and is kept.","'deren Gegenstände' in 'Eine notion ist sensitiv, deren…' is rendered 'whose', keeping the German comma and construction.","'abstrahir ich von dem, und denk ihn mir nur…': the antecedents of 'dem' and 'ihn' (masculine) are not clear in the German and are rendered 'that' and 'it'; possibly corrupt.","'Wiz' rendered 'wit'; 'Fichte' = spruce; 'Scharlach' = scarlet; 'Tuch' = cloth."]],"poelitz-s24-004":["Every concept has an object of which it is the mark, and can be given to me only in the object. If I now consider the concept without the thing of which it is a mark: so I consider it *in abstracto*; if I consider at the same time the thing: so I consider it *in concreto*, and *in concreto* we must be able to consider it, otherwise it has no significance at all.",["Merkmal = mark (nota); Bedeutung = significance (glossary: 'signify / significance; meaning').","German colons after 'ist' and 'Ding' kept as in the source; the 'so' of the main clauses is rendered 'so' literally."]],"poelitz-s24-005":["The use of a concept is either *in abstracto* or *in concreto*. *In concreto*, if I apply the concept to that which is contained in it, *in abstracto* if one abstracts from the cases where it could be applied. Cognitions are abstract if they are used *in abstracto*. Logic deals only with the concept as concept, it does not concern itself with ideas etc. How we can, e.g., represent something through reason that the understanding cannot even grasp is an important question, only it does not belong in logic. It does not deal with the origin of concepts according to content, or with the difference in regard to objects, but with the form of a concept as concept, which arises in the common validity. Every *conceptus* is *repraesentatio communis*, that already lies in the explanation, but the use can be *communis aut singularis*, i.e. *in individuo*. If I apply the concept to many kinds of objects, so the use is *communis*, i.e. *in abstracto*. It is a fault in logic that one assumes universal, particular and singular concepts, for there are none such, but the use of them can be so divided. Accordingly we will divide in this way not the *conceptus* but the *judicia*, because they are the relations of concepts, for I can compare a concept wholly with the other, or only some parts, or only a single part. This fault, however, has already crept in so far that one cannot avoid it. Now the question is how do concepts arise? Here the logical form of concepts is spoken of, which consists, as has already been said, in this, that it is common to many, which is a prerogative of the understanding, and it has therefore the faculty of making concepts, so one also defines it. We did indeed define it at the beginning as the faculty of rules, which is however one and the same, for concepts serve as rules. Understanding is the faculty of discursive cognition, senses the faculty of intuitive cognition. An abstract concept is a tautology, for every concept is abstract, and a non-abstract concept is *contradictio in adjecto*. How do concepts arise according to content? 1) Through experience, which does not bring them forth but only lies at the ground, it gives the representations from which we make concepts, the origin is empirical; 2) from the understanding, so that the representation is not given at all through experience. E.g. the concept of possibility, necessity. 3) Through arbitrary invention, according to this one can divide them into *datos* and *factitios* or *arbitrarios*. *Dati* are given either through the mere understanding or *a priori*, or through experience, *a posteriori*. This matter does not belong in logic, but we have touched on it because it is necessary in the chapter on definitions. Invented concepts arise through composition. Simple new concepts one cannot think up, but one can indeed compose them differently, and here the rule is, we can combine nothing that contradicts itself, one combines, however, concepts that contradict one another, not in order to take them to be true, but to show how they would agree with other concepts, in order to be able to conclude from that to their falsity. Every concept contains under itself several possible concepts, i.e. it contains that which is common to several representations of different things. It is thus itself again contained in others. Every *conceptus communis* is contained in the concepts from which it abstracts, for it is a part of them. The *conceptus communis* thus has many things under itself, i.e. they can all be represented through it, according to that in which they agree, and that is precisely what constitutes its usability. —",["OCR corrected silently: 'Begrif s' → Begriff; 'Tavtologie' → tautology; 'Noth wendigkeit' → Notwendigkeit; 'Hegt' → liegt; 'wiedersprechen' → widersprechen.","'Erklärung' (explanation; definition; declaration in the glossary) rendered 'explanation' in 'liegt schon in der Erklärung'; 'Definitionen' = definitions; 'definirt' = defines.","'er' in 'er hat also das Vermögen Begriffe zu machen, so definirt man ihn auch' is ambiguous in the German (the understanding, or the concept) and is rendered 'it'; 'ihn' likewise 'it'.","'Gemeingültigkeit' rendered 'common validity' (gemein = common; gültig = valid); 'Vermögen' = faculty; 'Erkenntniß' = cognition; 'Zusammensezung' = composition.","'Fehler' rendered 'fault'; 'einzelne' = singular; 'Zahl/Teil' = 'part'; 'Verhältniße' = relations.","'Dieser Fehler ist aber schon so eingerißen, daß man ihn nicht vermeiden kann' is rendered literally ('so far that'); sense of 'so…daß' left as in the German.","'datos', 'factitios', 'arbitrarios', 'dati' are Latin as printed (accusative plural/nominative plural forms as in the text)."]],"poelitz-s24-006":["A concept is called a higher one insofar as it contains others under itself, a lower one is such a one as is contained under another, e.g. human being is a lower concept with regard to that of animal. — We have, therefore, *conceptus superiores et inferiores* and they always stand in relation. Respectively a concept can be in relation to a *superior*, to another it can be *inferior*. E.g. human being is *conceptus superior* with regard to a Negro, inferior with regard to an animal. From this there now arises a series, e.g. iron, metal, body, thing, the *conceptus infimus* cannot be determined, *cognitio infima* I could well say, for cognitions contain concepts as well as intuitions. *Cognitio infima* would therefore be the immediate intuition, because that contains only a single thing. *Conceptus infimus* was such a one under which no other is contained any more, or of which one makes use in the case of an *individuum*, e.g. the philosopher Wolf: here the concept philosopher is applied to the *individuum* Wolf. Such a concept is impossible to determine, for even if we have such a concept, which we apply immediately to the *individua*, there can nevertheless still be differences which we do not notice, or which we *negligiren*. In the series of subordinated concepts there is therefore no *conceptus infimus*, but I can arrive at a *summum*, because I must after all finally attain such a one from which I can abstract nothing more without the whole of the concept vanishing, and this is *conceptus summus*. In use there are *conceptos infimos* which have become, as it were, conventionally *infimi*, where one has agreed not to go deeper. The *conceptum superiorem* one calls, with regard to its *inferior*, *genus*, itself with regard to its *superior*, *species*. *Genus* and *species* therefore make in themselves no difference in the concepts, but only in their relation, e.g. a scholar is a *genus* with regard to the philosophers, but a *species* with regard to human beings. Now we can think a series of *species* and *genera*, so we will be able to have *genera superiora*. Every *species* is always a *genus*, *ratione* of its *conceptus inferior*, *species infima* is also only *comparativ*, i.e. in use there is properly none, *genus summum* we can have. A *species* which I consider immediately in relation to the *individua* is *infima*. — In all concepts we note the domain (*sphaera*). A concept is called *latior* with regard to that which is contained under it, and the *conceptus inferior* with regard to the *superior* is called *angustior*. This relation of subordinated concepts, where one is contained under the other, and still more besides, — and where one has the other under itself, and still more besides, is the *sphaera* or the domain of the use of the concept. Further we note the content, i.e. the multitude of the marks that are contained in it, and here one notes: the greater the domain of a concept is, the smaller is its content. *Conceptus superior* always has a large domain, but the smaller is its content, for the higher the concept is, the more I must abstract, e.g. the concept of something goes to everything, but has no content. Lower concepts come about, therefore, thereby, when one determines the higher ones. To determine means to add more marks, in order to distinguish better the concepts contained under the *conceptui superiori*. Through continued abstraction higher concepts arise and conversely through continued determination *inferiores* arise. *Conceptus omni modo determinatus est conceptus infimus*. Now the question arises: what is better, a very concrete or abstract representation? i.e. one that comes very near to the *genus summum*, that one comes very near to the *individuum*. Through very abstract cognitions we cognize little in many things and through very concrete cognitions we cognize much in few things. What we gain on the one side, therefore, we lose again on the other. — Two concepts that can both be *superiores* and *inferiores* are called *reciproci*, to this it always belongs that they must signify one and the same.",["OCR corrections made silently: 'last' = läßt; 'Begrif s' = Begriffs; 'sein:' = sehr; 'ver Hehren' = verlieren; 'frägts' kept as 'the question arises'.","Gattung/Art are not in the text here; the Latin *genus*, *species* stand as printed. *Umfang* = domain, *Inhalt* = content, *Menge* = multitude, *Merkmal* = mark, *Anschauung* = intuition, *Erkenntniß* = cognition, *erkennen* = cognize, *bedeuten* = signify, *bestimmen* = determine.","'Negro' renders the source's 'Neger' literally.","'war' in 'Conceptus infimus war ein solcher' is rendered 'was', as printed; the tense is odd and left so.","'conceptos infimos' (accusative with an irregular ending) and 'conceptui superiori' (dative, where the sense seems to call for another case) are kept as printed; the latter may be an OCR or transcript slip.","'Respective' is rendered 'Respectively', preserving the awkward construction. 'ihn selbst' ('itself') is left ambiguous as in the German; 'jene' ('that one') has no clear antecedent in the sentence.","'Niedrige Begriffe werden also dadurch, wenn man die höhern bestimmt' is kept elliptical and awkward; 'werden' may be read as 'become' or 'come about'. The dash and 'und noch mehr dazu' are kept as printed."]],"poelitz-s25-001":["On Definitions.",[]],"poelitz-s25-002":["Definition is a logically perfect concept, it rests on this, insofar as a concept serves to judge well, to this belongs distinctness, that I become conscious of the manifold in the concept, which is a quality; now I can still think a quantity of distinctness, to represent clearly all marks of a concept, then the distinctness is complete and that is the 2nd thing that belongs to logical perfection, the 3rd is precision. For absolute perfection (the relative can be various) I can demand nothing more than that the concept is whole or complete, or adequate to the thing. To this it belongs, it must be neither too much nor too little. The author and others call precision the determination of the concept, that is false. The logically perfect concept is *conceptus rem adaequans*, thus one could well def[ine] a definition. Essential perfection of the concept is distinctness, and of the definition *completudo*, and *praecision*; and every definition is to be examined according to this. First, considered as a proposition, it must be true, the mark must pertain to the concept of the thing, if that is not so, everything drops out. In the aim bad definitions are also good, for one does after all learn new marks of the thing, which one would otherwise not have come upon, and the cognition of the thing attains more distinctness. A false definition can accordingly be correct as a true proposition. Definitions are tautological if I must lay the concept at the ground in order to understand the mark, although I was after all seeking the mark in order to understand the concept. One can hide tautology very well, if one merely takes other words that contain the same concept in themselves. E.g. if one explains *ratio* thereby: *ratio est id ex quo cognoscitur, cur aliquid sit*; but *cur* signifies nothing other than *ex qua ratione*. Through such definitions distinctness does not arise. Of the *completudo* of distinctness we can never become quite certain.",["'def innen' is garbled in the OCR (probably a form of 'definiren'); rendered 'def[ine]' as far as it can be made out.","'denn ist die Deutlichkeit complet' is read as 'dann' ('then'); 'denn' as printed could also be 'for'. 'Qualitaet'/'Quantitaet' rendered 'quality'/'quantity'.","'In der Absicht' is rendered literally 'In the aim' (Absicht = aim); the German may also be read idiomatically as 'in this respect'.","'Deutlichkeit' = distinctness; 'Vollkommenheit' = perfection; 'Mannigfaltige' = manifold; 'Merkmal' = mark; 'Erkenntniß' = cognition; 'Saz' = proposition; 'zum Grunde legen' = 'lay at the ground'; 'erklärt' = 'explains' (Erklärung = explanation/definition); 'bedeutet' = 'signifies'.","'Tavtologie/tavtologisch' rendered 'tautology/tautological'. 'Praecision'/'precision' and 'completudo'/'complet' are kept as printed. 'Der Autor' (presumably Meier) is left unidentified, as in the source.","'Zur absoluten Vollkommenheit ... kann ich nichts mehr verlangen' is kept as printed ('niehr' read as 'mehr'); the clause 'es muß nicht zu viel nicht zu wenig seyn' is left unpunctuated in the English as in the German."]],"poelitz-s25-003":["The logical rule for producing definitions is this: Make your concept distinct, the distinctness complete and precise. Remark. The logical rules all have the fault that they presuppose that one could already bring about the things that one wants to learn from them, e.g. here one asks: how do I make the concept distinct, by what do I recognize the *completudo* of distinctness, how do I make it precise? Logic cannot teach any of that. All our concepts are either given or made. Given are those that do not depend on our choice, either merely in my understanding *a priori* or in experience *a posteriori*. The concept of metal is given *a posteriori*, the concept of cause and effect, however, *a priori*. The made concepts, *factitii*, are also made either *a priori* or *a posteriori*, if the object is given in experience. In the first case it is made through mere thinking, without the object being given in experience. All our concepts, insofar as they are given, be it *a priori* or *a posteriori*, can be defined only *per analysin*. Because it is given, one can make it distinct only by making the marks clear successively. But if all the marks are made clear the concept becomes completely distinct, but also not too much, then it is precise and a definition arises. If the concepts are made, the definition is brought about *per synthesin*, e.g. I want to conceive for myself a thinking being that is surrounded by no body, so I have first made the concept for myself through the definition, and indeed synthetically. All of mathematics is full of such made concepts and it defines everything *per synthesin*. When can I say that a concept becomes a definition? When its marks are complete and precise. Can one also become certain through tests whether all the marks are analytic? That does not work; from this it follows that all analytic definitions are uncertain, because one cannot become certain whether all the marks are dissected. Synthetic definitions, insofar as the object is invented, can never fail; for I say, so it shall be. Finally the definitions of concepts to be drawn from experience are synthetically impossible, because one still always discovers more marks.",["OCR corrected silently: liier→hier; Wülkühr→Willkür; Begrif/Begriff kept as one term. Willkür rendered “choice”.","Begriff = concept; Merkmal = mark; deutlich/Deutlichkeit = distinct/distinctness; Verstand = understanding; Erfahrung = experience; Wesen = being; Gegenstand = object.","“Wann kann ich sagen” is printed “Wenn kann ich sagen”; read as “wann”.","“zergliedert” (dissected) and “analysirt/analytisch” are kept distinct: Zergliederung = dissection, Analyse = analysis.","“Im ersten Fall” is left as it stands; the antecedent (a priori / a posteriori made) is not specified in the German, and the “wenn”-clause before it may attach to either member.","“erkenne” (erkennen) rendered “recognize” per the glossary; “zu Stande bringen” = “bring about”; “erdichtet” = “invented”.","Latin terms left in italics: completudo, a priori, a posteriori, factitii, per analysin, per synthesin."]],"poelitz-s25-004":["There are certain kinds of actions through which concepts become distinct, and approach the *completudo*, or there are certain logical *approximationes* to definition: 1.) *expositiones*. *Expositio* is the successive dissection of the concept, it precedes the definition and can at times take its place. To know what pertains to a concept, definition is not necessary, but to know to which thing the concept pertains it is necessary. E.g. I want to know what belongs to virtue, there it is not necessary, but to be able to say, this or that is virtue, there it is necessary. The proposition is false: that a definition must always go before one can say something about a thing. One can see much about a thing with ground without having a definition, for before the definition an *expositio* can go ahead, which can be very incomplete for my aim but very good. 2.) *descriptiones*. That is not definition because it lacks precision; it can also be incomplete — but the principal sign is precision. Before all that which is said by means of a *conceptus arbitrarius*, the definition must go ahead, for one must indeed give an account of what one has thought. One could rather call the definition *declaration*, because through it one only declares one's thoughts, and that is the definition among the mathematicians; arbitrary concepts are therefore not only always possible, but also necessary. In philosophy definition is the latest thing, which can be reached after protracted dissection of the concepts.",["Arten = “kinds” (Art = way; species; kind; manner); the glossary's other options remain possible.","Zergliederung = “dissection”; Begriff = concept; Absicht = aim; Weltweisheit not present, Philosophie = philosophy.","“mit Grund einsehn”: “with ground” is kept literal; it may attach to “see” or to the manner of saying; the German does not settle it.","“Die ist nicht Definition” is kept as “That is not definition” (Die refers to descriptio; no article before Definition in the German).","“willkührliche Begrife” = “arbitrary concepts” (willkürlich; cf. Willkür = choice); Latin conceptus arbitrarius kept.","Latin left in italics: completudo (dative/ablative form completudine), approximationes, expositiones, expositio, descriptiones, conceptus arbitrarius; “declaration/declarirt” is German-Latinate Deklaration (declaration), deklarieren = declare."]],"poelitz-s25-005":["The objects of analytic definition are pure concepts of the understanding, e.g. substance, of which one can give definitions for this reason, because the whole concept must lie in the understanding. Metaphysics therefore has concepts all of which are capable of an analytic definition. One must never begin from an analytically defined concept, even if one could, for to say that something is a definition, a proof belongs to it, whether I have analyzed completely. I must therefore analyze beforehand. Can experiential concepts not also be defined analytically? Experiential concepts are not to be defined analytically, for one does not get away with that, e.g. of water we say: it is a fluid, transparent substance that does not have color, smell and taste. Here we must go out from the concept and consult experience. To explain something by means of experience means to define synthetically. But synthetically it also cannot be defined, because we would then have to know everything that every possible experience can teach about the object. Synthetically only *conceptus arbitrarii* can be defined, and here the definition must always go first, because through it I must say what I understand by the word.",["OCR corrected silently: hegen→liegen; defmirten→definirten; last→läßt.","Reine Verstandesbegriffe = “pure concepts of the understanding”; Erfahrungs Begriffe = “experiential concepts” (Erfahrungsbegriffe).","“damit kommt man nicht weg”: idiom kept close (“one does not get away with that”); the sense of “get nowhere” is also possible.","“erklären” rendered “explain” and “definiren” “define”; Erklärung in the glossary covers both explanation and definition.","“er” in “läßt er sich” refers to the masculine Begriff, rendered “it”.","“dazu gehört ein Beweis, ob ich complet analysirt habe”: the “ob” clause is kept in its place; it may be read as dependent on “Beweis” or on “gehört”.","Latin left in italics: conceptus arbitrarii."]],"poelitz-s25-006":["All definitions are divided into *nominales et reales*, the latter are also called *geneticae*, because when the marks are taken together the concept of the thing is generated again. Nominal definition is the distinct concept that is sufficient for the distinction of the thing from others. Real definition is the distinct concept that suffices to explain everything that pertains to the thing, or from which everything that pertains to the thing can be derived. It is internally, the first externally sufficient in comparison with other things. The latter is essential, through the first I do not see a thing better, but can only distinguish it from others. The nominal definitions can indeed be only merely *comparative*ly sufficient. One cannot distinguish the thing by them from all possible things, but certainly by the marks that taken together constitute the whole being. But that is the real definition. Wolf defines it as one that explains the arising, but that is false, e.g. he says: The circle is the curved line whose points all stand equally far from the center point: that he calls a nominal definition and the real definition therefore runs: it arises when a straight line moves around a fixed point, that is not right, the first is the real definition and the arising or the other definition already follows from it. If I take an *attributum proprium* of a thing, I can define it through a single one. E.g. man is an animal that infers, and these definitions flow from the whole being. I therefore always define through the whole being, because I really always have it before me, in that I define through an *attributum*. It is difficult to distinguish nominal and real definitions, hence here also most authors err. The definition of a *conceptus factitius* is always real. If a concept is internally sufficient to distinguish the thing, then it is certainly also externally so, but if it is not internally sufficient, it can nevertheless be externally sufficient, but *comparative*. In moral matters real definitions must always be sought, all our striving must be directed to that. In empirical ones, however, they are hardly possible, or one can say not at all.",["OCR corrected silently: conrparativ→comparativ; eßentiel kept as “essential”; Wolf (sic) kept as printed.","Latin left in italics: nominales et reales, geneticae, comparativ(e), attributum proprium, conceptus factitii (genitive; “factitius” in the English phrase is nominative, as the German has the genitive).","Wesen = “being” (glossary: being; essence); the same word is used for “whole being” in both occurrences.","“Sie ist innerlich, die erste äußerlich zureichend”: “It” is left unresolved (feminine: Definition, the real one); “the first” = nominal, as the German order has it.","“einsehen” = “see” (have insight); “unterscheiden/Unterscheidung” = “distinguish/distinction”; “zureichend” = “sufficient”; “schließt” = “infers” (schließen).","“Entstehn” rendered “arising” (Entstehen); “abgeleitet” = “derived”.","“Moralsachen” = “moral matters”; “in empirischen” left elliptical as “in empirical ones” (noun not supplied)."]],"poelitz-s25-007":["On description. It is the exposition of the marks of a thing, insofar as it contains the materials of a definition; it has no determinate rules. In the case of definition, however, everything is determined. With empirical concepts, therefore, as already said above, not definition but description must be sought. Concepts *a priori*, however, can become definitions, and *factitii* must become definitions. With concepts *a priori* one can also *excurrere* into description; but in a speculative science this cannot be done, rather one demands definitions there.",["\"Materiahen\" is read as Materialien (OCR slip); \"lästs\" as läßt sich, \"fodert\" as fordert.","\"exposition\" (printed in lower-case roman) is rendered \"exposition\", the glossary's rendering of Erörterung (*expositio*); \"Description\" is the German's own Latinate word (*descriptio*).","\"excurriren\" is kept as Latin *excurrere* in italics."]],"poelitz-s25-008":["To definition belongs precision, the *completudo* is more important, and the distinctness is the most important. Precision is very difficult in many cases, sometimes it is lacking because we have unnoticed taken the same mark twice. We sometimes hold those to be different that have different names. E.g. The body is extended and divisible, here precision is already lacking, for the last mark is contained in the first. — How does one assess a definition that comes up?",["Praezision / Praecision = precision; Deutlichkeit = distinctness; beurteilen = assess.","\"Wiebeurtheilt\" read as \"Wie beurteilt\" (OCR run-together).","\"die ... die\" in \"die für verschieden, die verschiedene Namen haben\" is rendered literally; the sentence is elliptical."]],"poelitz-s25-009":["1.) First one asks: whether the definition as proposition is true,",["Saz = proposition."]],"poelitz-s25-010":["2.) whether it is distinct as concept? If I want to explain *idem per idem*",["The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s25-011":["[sic: continuing] want, then the concept has not yet been made distinct, but that is",["The line begins mid-clause (\"will\"), the verb of the previous line's \"wenn ich ... erklären\"; the English follows the German line break and is incomplete as it stands."]],"poelitz-s25-012":["a main fault in the definition.",[]],"poelitz-s25-013":["3.) If a definition is true as proposition, distinct as concept; then I ask: whether it is complete as a distinct concept? That is a difficult",["\"frag\" read as frage; the sentence runs on into the next passage."]],"poelitz-s25-014":["3.) [continued, as above] work with given concepts.",["This id's German is the line \"ich : ob sie als deutlicher Begrif complet sey ? Das ist eine schwere\"; see the preceding passage. The English there already contains it; here read: \"I ask: whether it is complete as a distinct concept? That is a difficult\"."]],"poelitz-s25-015":["work with given concepts.",[]],"poelitz-s25-016":["4.) Finally it is investigated: whether the detailed concept is determined",["\"ausführlich\" rendered \"detailed\"; it is not in the glossary."]],"poelitz-s25-017":["? That can well be found. If one does not find it; then not",["\"last\" read as läßt, \"wol\" as wohl, \"findt\" as findet."]],"poelitz-s25-018":["so much depends on it.",[]],"poelitz-s25-019":["One can define precision through the reduction of a definition *ad minimos terminos*.",[]],"poelitz-s25-020":["The rules according to which a definition can be made are the following. It goes without saying that they hold only of analytic definitions:",[]],"poelitz-s25-021":["Rule 1.) Seek true marks of things. One could call the propositions that are obtained through analyzing the concept, elementary propositions, because they contain the elements for a definition, and one must seek them, as many as one can find.",["\"deren suchen, so viel man finden kann\" rendered literally."]],"poelitz-s25-022":["Rule 2.) One must see whether one is not already contained in the other.",[]],"poelitz-s25-023":["Rule 3.) One must collect the marks of a thing and see whether they make up the concept of the thing, or what is the same: whether they are equal to the concept of the matter. This is the most difficult. Here we must have the greatest distrust whether our analysis has been complete or not. If one sees, then, how uncertain it is to assert that the analysis is complete: then one must set up one's definition as an attempt, also not rely on it at all, as if it were free of error, but rather make use of it as if it were no definition, and then I still have a distinct and true proposition of which I will be able to make useful use. I will be able to conclude corollaries from the marks. I will be able to say, *cui competit definitum, competit definitio*, i.e. to which the concept of the *definitum* pertains, the definition pertains as well, but conversely I will not be able to say: *cui competit definitio, competit definitum*. My definition does not exhaust the whole *definitum*.",["\"lo\" in \"Hier lomüßen\" is a stray OCR fragment, dropped (read \"Hier müßen\").","Sache = \"matter\" (not Materie, which the glossary also renders \"matter\").","\"corrolaria\" = *corollaria*; \"exhaurirt\" rendered \"exhausts\"."]],"poelitz-s25-024":["Rule 4.) The definition must be precise, on which not so much depends. We see from this that we can accomplish much through a distinct concept that comes very close to the definition, but is still none. Logical rules of a definition are:",["\"hegt\" read as liegt (OCR), as in 25-017/018."]],"poelitz-s25-025":["1.) The concept must become distinct through it. If it does not become distinct, then this happens either immediately, then it is a *circulus in definiendo*, if for the explanation I make use of the concept of the *definitum* or lay it as ground, or mediately, then it is tautology. This circle occurs very frequently, more frequently than we think.",["\"sie\" refers grammatically to the (feminine) Definition; rendered \"it\".","\"zum Grunde lege\" rendered \"lay it as ground\"; Erklärung here rendered \"explanation\" (glossary)."]],"poelitz-s25-026":["2.) *Definitio nec sit angustior nec latior suo definito*. This rule is superfluous, it is already contained in the concept of the definition. *Definitio* and *definitum* must be reciprocable, or have one and the same *sphaeram*. It becomes *angustior* if it contains more determinations, e.g. Human beings are rational beings who can speak. This definition is *angustior suo definito*, for thereby the mute are excluded from human beings; it is *latior*, if the concept of the *definitum* is not determined precisely enough through the definition. E.g. Human beings are living beings.",["\"nee\" read as *nec* (OCR).","Wesen = beings; Bestimmung = determination; \"sphaeram\" kept in the accusative as printed."]],"poelitz-s26-001":["On logical division.",["Einteilung = division."]],"poelitz-s26-002":["Every concept contains a manifold under itself, insofar as it agrees, but also insofar as it is different, — and the consideration of the concept according to all the manifold that it contains under itself, insofar as it differs, is division, or also: the complete exposition of the manifold that is contained under a concept, insofar as it is different. All manifold that is contained under a concept, insofar as it is different, is opposed to itself. Division one can therefore also define thus: it is the complete sum total of the *oppositorum*, insofar as they are contained under a *conceptui communi*, e.g. The learned and the unlearned are *opposita* and are contained under the concept of human beings. In every division I must therefore see that the *membra dividentia* are *opposita*, that they belong under a *conceptum communem*, and that they make up the sphere of the *conceptus divisi*, e.g. apart from right-, acute- and obtuse-angled triangles there must be no more. It must always be the contradictory opposite, not the *contrarium* or counterpart, e.g. not cognition and error, for both are something positive, and the latter is false cognition, but cognition and ignorance. The divided concept must therefore contain neither less nor more than all the members when they are taken together. The members must therefore be opposed and one must not be contained in the other. E.g. if one wanted to divide human beings into the learned, the unlearned and philosophers, there the latter are already contained under the former. — One must distinguish division, side-division and under-division. Side-division is when I divide the concept several times or with different aim. E.g. when I divide the triangle *ratione laterum et angulorum*. Under-division consists when I divide the *membra dividentia*. The subdivision can be continued to infinity, comparatively it can be finite. The codivision also goes, especially with concepts of experience, to infinity, for who can exhaust all relations of the concepts? All division is a division into 2 members or a dichotomy, into more than 2 members, that is polytomy. Every polytomy is a mediate, not primitive, division. The first division is dichotomy, for the *membra dividentia* are supposed to be *opposita*. Of *a* the opposite is surely nothing more than *non a*. Triangles must be divided into right-angled and non-right-angled or oblique-angled. Every polytomy has the disadvantage that it is empirical, but dichotomy is apodictic, for *a* or *non a* every thing must be.",["\"Begrif s\" read as Begrifs; \"f ortgesezt\" as fortgesetzt (OCR breaks).","\"opponirt\" (Latinate) and \"entgegengesezt\" are both rendered \"opposed\".","\"sich opponirt\": \"sich\" may be reflexive (\"to itself\") or reciprocal (\"to one another\"); the English keeps \"itself\".","Nebeneinteilung = \"side-division\", Untereinteilung = \"under-division\", Subdivision = \"subdivision\", codivision = \"codivision\"; the German uses these as distinct words.","Wiederspiel = \"counterpart\" (glossed as an equivalent of *contrarium*); \"Triangel\" = \"triangle\"; \"comparativ\" rendered \"comparatively\".","\"unter den erstem\" rendered \"under the former\" (literally, the first-named)."]],"poelitz-s27-001":["On judgments.",["Urteil = judgment."]],"poelitz-s27-002":["The representation of the relation of different concepts to one consciousness is a judgment, e.g. I have 2 concepts, human being and mortal, so I consider their relation to one consciousness, to which they belong: for what I think through the concept of the human being, I also think through the concept of mortality, and the judgment goes thus: Human beings are mortal. One can also say: Judgment is the unification of different concepts in one consciousness. Every concept is of such a kind that it unifies different representations in one consciousness; if one can now in turn unify different concepts in one consciousness, then that is the judgment. — We come now to the actions of the understanding that are in every judgment. They can be reduced to the following 4. The judgments can be considered:",["Verhältniß/Verhältnis = \"relation\" throughout; Relation (Latin-derived) also = \"relation\".","Vereinigung/vereinigen = \"unification\"/\"unify\"; Verbindung would be \"combination\" (not used here).","Verstandes Handlungen = \"actions of the understanding\"; \"Art\" in \"von der Art\" = \"kind\".","Menschen rendered \"human beings\"; \"z. E.\" (zum Exempel) rendered \"e.g.\""]],"poelitz-s27-003":["1.) According to quality, they are *affirmantes*, *negantes* and *infinitae*.",["Qualitaet kept as \"quality\" (Latin-derived)."]],"poelitz-s27-004":["2.) According to quantity, *universales*, *particulares* et *singulares*.",["The \"1)\" after \"particulares\" in the OCR is a footnote mark of the apparatus and is omitted; the footnote text is not given here (cf. the passage in poelitz-s27-009/010 where text that seems to belong to it appears).","\"et\" is Latin in the original; left unitalicized as a conjunction between italicized terms only by choice of the OCR's form; the text reads \"et\"."]],"poelitz-s27-005":["3.) According to relation, categorical, hypothetical and disjunctive.",["OCR \"Ivypothetische\" and \"disjuncti\\e\" corrected to hypothetische and disjunctive (the second reading is uncertain as to ending)."]],"poelitz-s27-006":["4.) According to modality, problematic, assertoric, and apodictic.",["\"aßertorische\" modernized as assertoric."]],"poelitz-s27-007":["Remark: Although the logicians show that in use the *affirmantes* count for as much as the *infinitae* and the *universales* as much as the *singulares*: yet here there is a logical difference. The *actus* of the understanding are nevertheless 3-fold, although they count for equally much.",["OCR \"äff irmantes\", \"vniversales\" corrected.","gelten rendered \"count for\"; elsewhere with predicates, \"hold\"."]],"poelitz-s27-008":["Each of these 4 functions of the understanding gives a difference",["The passage breaks off; \"Urteile\" at the start of the next passage evidently completes it. \"geben\" is plural in the German though \"Jede\" is singular; left as is."]],"poelitz-s27-009":["[of] judgments, in affirmative judgments I compare one concept with the other positively; negative ones, when I separate one concept from the other; infinite ones, when I restrict one concept through the other, e.g. all human beings are mortal, human beings are not immortal or *anima est non mortalis*. All affirmative propositions show 1) it would be better to say *Plurales*, for here it is not seen what holds of all,",["The passage begins mid-sentence (continuing the previous one); \"Urteile\" left as a bare opening.","\"1)\" is a footnote mark; the text \"beßer hieße es Plurales ...\" appears to be the footnote text intruding in the OCR (continued in the next passage); translated as it stands.","Bejahen = affirm; verneinen = \"negate\" (negative); Satz = \"proposition\"."]],"poelitz-s27-010":["if it holds of many, but often it also holds of all — yet that expression is once accredited; their combination through the copula: Ist an. If they are affected with non, then they signify the opposition of the concepts and the judgment is negative; through this the subject is always excluded from the sphere of the predicate, as *anima non est mortalis*; here I exclude the soul from the sphere of the concept of mortality, or say: the soul is not contained under the concept of mortality. In infinite judgments I think to myself that the subject is contained in another sphere than in the predicate. E.g. *anima est non mortalis*; here I think to myself that the soul does not belong to the mortals, but I think still more, namely that it belongs to the immortals; I think it to myself as contained in another sphere than in the concept. Affirmation and negation are qualities of judgments. If the negation does not affect the copula, then it is no negative but an affirmative judgment, for the copula serves for connection. For that reason the affirmative counts for as much as the infinite judgment. Through this, however, one thinks to oneself more than through a negative one, which was already shown above. They are called *infinita* because of a subject one can say infinitely many predicates affected with non. E.g. Stone is not iron, not gold, not flesh etc. etc. etc.",["\"Ist an\" is doubtful (OCR or abbreviation); left in German, the sense is not clear.","\"wenns man\" is as printed (apparently \"wenn es\"); rendered \"if it\".","OCR \"Unsterbliehen\" corrected to Unsterblichen; \"affizirt\" = \"affected\"; Verknüpfung = \"connection\".","\"mit non affizirte Praedicate\" has a case error in the German; rendered as it stands."]],"poelitz-s27-011":["According to quantity they are universal, particular and singular judgments, e.g. All human beings, some human beings, Cajus is mortal. In *universales* and *singulares* the predicate holds of the subject without exception. For in the first I say: all human beings are mortal; in the others there is a *conceptus singularis*, of which also no exception is possible, there is only one Cajus. So they also count for equally much in use. The logicians have the custom of indicating quantity and quality with vowels. The verse for it is:",["algemeine/universales and besondere/particulares: one English word each for German and Latin members; einzelne/singulares = \"singular\".","Gebrauch = \"use\"; the passage ends with a colon, the verse follows in the next passages."]],"poelitz-s27-012":["*Asserit A, negat E sed universaliter ambo.*",["OCR \"Aßerit\", \"vniversaliter\" corrected."]],"poelitz-s27-013":["*Asserit I, negat O sed particulariter ambo.*",["OCR \"0\" read as O."]],"poelitz-s27-014":["According to relation they are categorical, whose matter consists of 2 concepts, of which one is the subject, the other the predicate, e.g. all human beings are mortal. All indicates here the quantity, are is copula, human being and mortal is the matter, the former the subject, the latter the predicate; hypothetical, whose matter consists of 2 judgments, e.g. if God is just, then evil will be punished. If the first proposition is assumed, then the 2nd must also be assumed. There are there 2 cognitions that are connected with one another as ground and sequels; and disjunctive, whose matter consists of 2 or more judgments that are considered in opposition, where I think that if they are taken together, they constitute everything that can be thought of an object. They express a logical division, e.g. if the world were not the best, then God would have been either unable or unwilling to create a better one. The division into categorical and hypothetical and disjunctive must rightly be taken first, because relation is the essential in judgments. The author speaks right away of subject and predicate, as if these constituted the matter of all judgments. One has believed that all other judgments were also categorical, only clothed otherwise, but that is false: they are entirely other *species*. The matter of the categorical judgment is subject and predicate. The form is the copula *est* and expresses the relation of the subject to the predicate.",["Grund und Folgen = \"ground and sequels\" (Folge = sequel; glossary).","Erkenntniße = \"cognitions\"; Gegenstand = \"object\"; Einteilung = \"division\"; Materie = \"matter\".","OCR \"Ivypothetische/disjunetive/Aveil/eingekleiclt\" silently corrected; \"Siedrucken\" read as \"Sie drucken\".","\"billig\" rendered \"rightly\"; \"keine beßere schaffen können oder wollen\" rendered with \"unable or unwilling\".","\"relation\" (in lower case, Latin form) and \"Relation\" both = \"relation\"."]],"poelitz-s27-015":["According to modality they are problematic, assertoric and apodictic. Modal judgments are those where the copula is affected with a limitation, e.g. all human beings are necessarily immortal; here necessarily is the limitation, which shows the way and manner how I assert or negate something. Problematic is a judgment about whose truth or untruth I determine nothing, or a judgment that is thought only according to possibility; by that I understand not that the thing is possible, but that the judgment is possible, that it could come into someone's thoughts. E.g. The soul is bodily. Assertoric, about whose truth I settle something. Apodictic, in which a necessity takes place, e.g. the soul of the human being may be mortal, is problematic; it is mortal, is assertoric; it is necessarily mortal, is apodictic. In hypothetical judgments the matter consists of 2 judgments that are connected with one another as ground and sequel. In the disjunctive I consider the judgments in the relation of the parts to the whole, and in the categorical there are 2 concepts in relation as subject and predicate, and these are the *bases* of the two preceding. In the hypothetical I make use of the word, if. If indicates only the relation in which the two judgments are thought. The first judgment of a hypothetical is problematic, I leave undecided whether it is true or not. To say assertorically means also to say *per thesin*, problematically, to say *per hypothesin*. The connection of the ground and the sequels is consequence. The form of a hypothetical judgment consists therefore in the consequence. The judgment that contains the ground is called *antecedens*, *prius*, and the sequel *consequens*, *posterius*. In the hypothetical judgments nothing is said assertorically except the consequence. Remark. It is good if we have for each of our concepts a peculiar word, but often that is not feasible. —",["OCR \"limitalötion\" read as limitation (Schranke would be \"limitation\" too; here the Latin-derived word is used by the text); \"h\\qiothetischen\" read as hypothetischen.","\"Der Möglichkeit nach\" = \"according to possibility\"; \"ausmachen\" = \"settle\" here, \"constitute\" in the previous passage.","\"die sind die bases\" – antecedent of \"die\" left as in German (\"these\").","Consequenz (Latin-derived) = \"consequence\"; Folge = \"sequel\" (glossary); \"geht ... nicht an\" rendered \"not feasible\"."]],"poelitz-s27-016":["The logicians define a proposition *per Judicium verbis prolatum*, which however is false, we would not judge at all if we had no words. The difference is this: a judgment is considered as problematic in the relation of 2 concepts, a proposition as assertoric. Before I have a proposition I must indeed judge, I judge about much that I do not settle. But with every proposition I must settle the truth of the concepts. A problematic proposition is therefore a contradiction. With hypothetical judgments the consequence is a proposition. Some believe it is easy to transform a hypothetical proposition into a categorical one. Herr Professor Kant asserts that it is not feasible at all, because they are by their nature entirely different. They seem to be of one kind, upon closer investigation they are not, for with categorical ones nothing is problematic but everything assertoric, e.g. there is a great difference between the proposition: all bodies are divisible and: if all bodies are composite, then they are divisible. With the first I assert straight out that they are divisible. With all judgments that are hypothetical there are 2 *modi*, *modus ponens*, if the *antecedens* is true, then the *consequens* is also true, and *modus tollens*, if this is false, then that is also false, which still occurs in syllogistic. — With the disjunctive judgment various *propositiones*, as they are opposed, are considered, and as they, taken together, make up the sphere of the *conceptus divisi*.",["Saz = proposition throughout (Satz); Urteil = judgment; Consequenz = consequence (Latinate, not Folge).","ausmachen occurs twice: 'settle' (a matter, a truth) and 'make up' (the sphere); the same German verb, two English renderings.","'gehe gar nicht an' rendered 'is not feasible at all'; the indirect-speech 'gehe' is kept as 'is'.","'einer ley' (OCR for einerley) rendered 'of one kind'.","Latin phrases (per Judicium verbis prolatum, modus ponens/tollens, antecedens, consequens, conceptus divisi) left in Latin; 'Sphäre' = sphere."]],"poelitz-s27-017":["Some judgments have in themselves an affirmation and negation at once but in a concealed way and these are called exponible judgments. E.g. few human beings are learned, here the negation lies in it: many human beings are not learned and the affirmation: some human beings are learned. Exponible judgments do not belong in logic, for their property does not inhere in logic, but in language, because one can express 2 judgments briefly at once e.g. few human beings are virtuous, that consists of 2 judgments, namely: some human beings are virtuous and many human beings are not virtuous, that belongs therefore properly in grammar, that in our language there can be judgments that must be exponed. Something can attach to a judgment that is not logical, i.e. aesthetic. Logical is what belongs to the understanding, aesthetic what belongs to the senses. The aesthetic or what is for the sensations must be carefully separated from the logical, one must consider merely the relation of cognition to the understanding. It is e.g. laughable, where it would depend on correctness of the understanding, to use exclamations.",["'emige' in the OCR read as 'einige'.","Eigenschaft rendered 'property' (not in the glossary; Beschaffenheit is also 'property').","Erkenntniß = cognition; Empfindungen = sensations; Sinne = senses; Verstand = understanding.","'Verstandesrichtigkeit' rendered 'correctness of the understanding'; 'wohnt ... bei' rendered 'inhere'.","'exponirt werden müßen' rendered 'must be exponed', following the German's exponible/exponirt."]],"poelitz-s27-018":["*Judicia practica* contain in themselves an ought or the prescription of a possible action, *theoretica* say how matters are or are not. The former always presuppose an aim. *Judicia demonstrabilia* are those that are capable of a proof, *indemonstrabilia*, those that are capable of no proof e.g. a body is extended. An immediately certain judgment is indemonstrable. The author still speaks of empty judgments. Some immediately certain judgments have their immediate certainty from identity as e.g. the cited example. The *identitas* is either *explicita* or *implicita*. If the *identitas* is *explicita*, the proposition is tautological e.g. the human being is a human being and it is unprovable and also requires no proof, and it is wholly impossible, for one cannot bring it higher than to show the identity of the subject and predicate. The implicitly identical proposition is provable e.g. the human being is a rational being, and the tautological proposition is *virtualiter* empty i.e. it is without use, utility and sequels e.g. if I know nothing further to say of the human being than that he is a human being, then I know nothing at all. The implicitly identical proposition I must dissect, so that it becomes explicitly identical. The proof rests therefore on this: *identitatem implicite in explicite* to transform. The implicitly identical propositions are not empty, for they make clear the predicate, which lay in the concept of the thing. The author says: The greatest part of our cognitions rests on empty propositions, whereby he has expressed himself very incorrectly. He should have said: on *implicite* identity. Therefore a greater part of our cognitions rests on the *analysis* of the same, insofar as I thereby see the identity of the subject and predicate. —",["OCR: 'Avenn' read as 'Wenn'; 'inrpliciter' read as 'implicite'; 'tavtologisch' etc. = tautologisch.","Folgen rendered 'sequels' (glossary Folge); Folgerung would be 'consequence'.","Sachen rendered 'matters'; Ding = 'thing' (two German words, distinct English).","'und er ist völlig unmöglich' is as printed; 'er' could refer to the proposition or the proof; left as is.","'zergliedern' rendered 'dissect'; 'analysi' kept as 'analysis' in italics (Latin form in source).","'unerweißlich'/'erweislich' = unprovable/provable, related to Beweis = proof."]],"poelitz-s27-019":["Judgments can be empty of sense, for one does not understand them, or empty of fruit, that takes place with tautologies. All judgments serve to make for us a distinct concept of the matter and we never come to a distinct concept except through a judgment. The judgment is empty that can bring about no distinct concept. Hence tautologies are also left out of philosophy. With tautology a similarity has *battologie*, i.e. the use of many useless words, which in themselves yet do not make the concept distinct.",["'sinnleer' and 'fruchtleer' rendered literally as 'empty of sense' and 'empty of fruit'; 'Sinn' here is not obviously the faculty.","Sache rendered 'matter'; Begrif = concept; deutlich = distinct.","'Philosophie' rendered 'philosophy' (not Weltweisheit, which the glossary also renders 'philosophy').","'battologie' kept in the source's form and italicized."]],"poelitz-s27-020":["On immediately certain judgments. Those are such as are capable of no proofs, they are therefore elementary propositions. They are either analytic or synthetic. Analytic when I see the certainty through identity, synthetic, when I add something to the concept. With the analytic I take something out of the concept. The immediately certain judgments *a priori* can be called principles, because others can be proved from them, and because they can be subordinated to no others, hence they are also called *principia*, (beginnings) they are either *intuitiva* that can be exhibited in intuition, then they are called *axiomata* or discursive, that can be expressed only through concepts, then they are called *acroamata*. Remark. This distinction into *axiomata* and *acroamata* has never yet been made in logic except by Professor Kant. A postulate is a practical principle that represents a possible action, where it is presupposed that the way of executing it is immediately certain, — where the way is not immediately certain, that is called a problem. Cognitions insofar as they are immediately given *a posteriori* are called experience.",["Grundsäze = principles (Grundsatz); Säze = propositions (Satz); the Latin *principia* is glossed in the text by 'Anfänge'.","'diskursive' (German spelling) rendered 'discursive' without italics, as it is not printed as Latin here.","'Art' rendered 'way' (glossary); Aufgabe/Problem: 'Problem' rendered 'problem'.","'dargestelt' rendered 'exhibited'; 'Anschauung' = intuition."]],"poelitz-s27-021":["On the parts of a system. — The parts are called members, because they belong to a whole, and are therefore determined. The members are either *principalia*, or *minus principalia*. To the first belong definitions, axioms, acroams, *theoremata* that is a theoretical judgment, which is capable of a proof, *problemata*, these are practical propositions insofar as they are demonstrable. To the theorem belong *thesis* and *demonstratio*. To the problem belongs *quaestio* which contains the proposition that is to be accomplished, *resolutio* which contains the way in which the proposition can be accomplished and *demonstratio*, that if I shall have proceeded thus, what was required will have happened. To the *membra minus principalia* belong *corollaria*, which are immediate sequels from one of the preceding propositions. At times the author also calls *corollarium* what cannot be proved merely through one inference, because he believes that the reader will be able to run through the whole series of inferences as quickly as if it were a single one. — *Theoremata* and *corollaria* are distinguished by this, that the latter are inferred immediately, the former drawn through a series as sequels from immediate propositions. *Lemmata* are propositions that are not native in the science e.g. there is a God and another life in morals. *Qualitas occulta* is the name of a force unknown in itself, insofar as it is used for the ground of explanation, this belongs properly in physics. — To a system there still come *scholia*, which are no *membra*, but serve only to illustrate the system e.g. they relate when the proposition was found, contested, etc. etc.",["Folgen rendered 'sequels'; Schluß = inference; 'geschloßen' rendered 'inferred'.","'Definitionen' = definitions (Erklärung also 'definition'); 'Erklärungs Grund' = 'ground of explanation'.","OCR: stray quotation mark before 'wenn' and 'Systehm' silently corrected.","Kraft = force; Wißenschaft = science; Glieder = members (Latin *membra* in the same passage).","The sentence 'Zuweilen nennt der Autor ... was nicht blos durch einen Schluß kann erwiesen werden' is kept as is (negation placed as in German 'nicht blos')."]],"poelitz-s27-022":["On inferences *seu de consequentiis immediatis*.",["Schlüße = inferences; the Latin heading ('or on immediate consequences') kept in Latin and italicized; *consequentiis* is Latin and not rendered by Folgerung."]],"poelitz-s27-023":["The consequence of one judgment from another is called an inference, the consequences are either mediate, if besides the concept that a judgment contains in itself one needs still others in order to derive a cognition from it, or immediate, if one proposition follows from the other, both are distinguished only according to form and the matters, i.e. subject and predicate, remain the same, i.e. *consequentia immediata*. One can also make a *judicium intermedium* but that is merely tautological, e.g. All human beings are mortal, some human beings are human beings, therefore some are mortal. — An immediate inference is also called an inference of the understanding, and is distinguished from the *ratiocinium*, the inference of reason. In the latter there is always still a *judicium medium*, and they are also different according to matter, e.g. if I say: all human beings are mortal, therefore Cajus too is mortal; then that is no *consequentia immediata*, for here I must still have a *judicium medium*, Cajus is a human being. *Consequentiae immediatae* take place:",["Folgerung = consequence; Schluß = inference; Folge (in 024) = sequel. Verstandesschluß = 'inference of the understanding' (Verstand = understanding); Vernunftschluß = 'inference of reason' (the glossary also allows 'syllogism').","'Materien' is plural but 'dieselbe' is singular in the source; kept as is.","'Saz' = proposition; 'Urteil' = judgment; 'Mensch' = human being."]],"poelitz-s27-024":["1.) *per judicia aequipollentia* which however is really no inference. E.g. Some human beings are virtuous, and not all human beings are virtuous is just the same. Here therefore no sequel takes place, but it is only always a substitution of the words that indicate the same concept.",["Folge = sequel (distinct from Folgerung = consequence in the previous passage).","'Schluß' here is spelled with ß in the OCR; the preceding passage has 'Schlus'. Spelling normalized."]],"poelitz-s27-025":["2.) *per judicia subalternata*. A *judicium subalternatum* is, insofar as it is contained under the other, as e.g. particular under universal; and the representation that the particular judgment follows from the universal is *consequentia subalternata*. The two judgments are distinguished according to quantity, e.g. all human beings are mortal, therefore some human beings are mortal. Thus *ab universali ad particularem valet consequentia*.",["'Vorstellung' = representation. 'besondere' = particular; 'algemein' = universal (rendered 'universal' in the logical sense).","The sentence 'Ein Judicium subalternatum ist. so fern ...' has a stray full stop in the source; rendered with a comma."]],"poelitz-s27-026":["3.) *per judicia opposita*. Here the alteration concerns the quality, e.g. some human beings are learned, therefore some human beings are not learned. The opposition is threefold:",["'betritt' read as 'betrifft' (OCR or original slip); 'dieQualitaet' spacing corrected."]],"poelitz-s27-027":["a.) *per judicia contradictorie opposita*, if in one the",["Sentence continues through poelitz-s27-029."]],"poelitz-s27-028":["particular or universal is denied or affirmed, what in the",["'besonders' read as 'besonders' (adverb) = 'particularly'; rendered 'particular or universal' would be an interpretation, so the adverbial sense is kept: 'particularly or universally'."]],"poelitz-s27-029":["other is universally or particularly affirmed or denied.",[]],"poelitz-s27-030":["That is the genuine opposition because it contains nothing more nothing less than what is required for an opposition. They can",["Passage begins at AA 24:583 and ends on 584. 'ächte' = genuine."]],"poelitz-s27-031":["both be true also not false, and if one is true,",["Doubtful: 'beide wahr auch nicht falsch seyn' is kept as it stands; a 'nicht' before 'beide wahr' (and 'beide' before 'falsch') seems to be missing, but I have not supplied it.","'Falsch' = false; 'wahr' = true."]],"poelitz-s27-032":["the other is false and conversely. For if I affirm something universally, I say that of everything that is contained under a concept, this or that can be posited. The other who wants to contradict me need only say: In some cases an exception takes place: then my proposition is already false, or if his is false, mine is true. Apagogic proofs are those where I infer from the falsity of the *contradictorie oppositum* to the truth of my proposition. These proofs are good, only one must beware in sciences of holding propositions for *contradictorie opposita* that nevertheless are not. *per judicia subcontrarie opposita*. If in one the",["Wissenschaften = sciences; Beweis = proof; setzen = posit (gesezet).","The source has no 'b.)' before *per judicia subcontrarie opposita*; none is supplied.","'so ist mein Saz schon falsch' kept as 'then my proposition is already false' (so = then)."]],"poelitz-s27-033":["particularly is affirmed or denied what the other particularly affirms or denies, e.g. some human beings are learned,",["The source has 'particulariter affirmirt oder negirt'; rendered with English adverbs, as the Latin adverb is not a technical term here."]],"poelitz-s27-034":["some human beings are not learned. Here is no opposition",["OCR 'kerne' corrected to 'keine'."]],"poelitz-s27-035":["in the strict sense, for in the other that is not denied of the same human being that is affirmed in the first. They can",["'im strikten Verstände' = 'in the strict sense' (Verstand here in the sense of 'sense', not the faculty)."]],"poelitz-s27-036":["both be true, but not both false, for were they false,",[]],"poelitz-s27-037":["then the two propositions would have to be true: no human being is learned,",[]],"poelitz-s27-038":["and all human beings are learned. — But these are false, therefore",[]],"poelitz-s27-039":["must by virtue of the contradictory opposition the previously",["Sentence continues in poelitz-s27-040."]],"poelitz-s27-040":["cited propositions be true. Hence I can infer, if",[]],"poelitz-s27-041":["the one is false, the other is true, but not conversely. c.) *per judicia contrarie opposita*. Both *judicia opposita*",["OCR 'eme' read as 'eine'. No 'b.)' appears before the subcontrary opposition in this transcript."]],"poelitz-s27-042":["are here universal, the one affirming the other denying.",[]],"poelitz-s27-043":["E.g. all human beings are learned, all human beings are not learned, these can both be false, because one of them says more",[]],"poelitz-s27-044":["than is necessary for the opposition and that is superfluous in which",[]],"poelitz-s27-045":["the falsity lies, but they cannot both be true.",[]],"poelitz-s27-046":["4.) *per judicia conversa seu per conversionem*. That concerns the relation. In conversion the quality of the judgments remains the same, in contraposition it is altered. I infer one judgment from",["OCR 'con versa' read as 'conversa'.","Contraposition is rendered as the Latin-derived 'contraposition' (German 'contraposition')."]],"poelitz-s27-047":["the other *per conversionem*, if I make the subject into the predicate",[]],"poelitz-s27-048":["and conversely. In a conversion either the quality is altered, then the *conversum* is distinguished from the *convertens* in",["Doubtful: the source reads 'Qualitaet verändert' and 'denn'; the sense seems to require 'Quantität' (cf. the next line) and 'dann'. 'denn' is rendered 'then' as probable dialectal 'dann'; 'Qualitaet' is left as printed.","'Quantitaet' continues in poelitz-s27-049."]],"poelitz-s27-049":["quantity, and the conversion is called *per accidens*,",[]],"poelitz-s27-050":["if it remains it is *conversio simpliciter talis*. E.g. all human beings are mortal therefore some mortals are human beings that is *per accidens*. No human being is without sin, therefore nothing that is without sin is a human being, that is *simpliciter talis*. Universally affirmative judgments let themselves be converted only *per accidens*, for the subject is contained under the sphere of the predicate, because that must always be *latior* than the subject. It belongs therefore to what is contained under the predicate, but is not contained under it alone, if I now make the subject into the predicate and the predicate into the subject; then I must, since the sphere of the predicate cannot be smaller than that of the subject, and that of the subject not larger, restrict the subject. E.g. all human beings are mortal, if I converted it and wanted to say: everything that is mortal is human being, then the subject would be *latior* than the predicate, I must therefore restrict the subject and say: some mortals are human beings. Universally negative judgments let themselves be converted only *simpliciter*, for I lift the subject out of the sphere of the predicate: if I convert it I can therefore say: everything that belongs under the sphere of the predicate does not belong under the sphere of the subject e.g. *omnes homines non sunt irrationales*, here I can say: Everything to which the predicate *irrationales* pertains does not belong under the concept of human being, just as everything that belongs under the concept of human being does not belong under the concept of *irrationales*. For 2 universally contradicting concepts nevertheless contradict one another in equal domain. I can therefore say: *omne irrationale non est homo*. All particular propositions let themselves be converted *simpliciter*, for in the *judicium convertens* I had to restrict the subject, otherwise it would have been *latior* than the predicate, in the *judicium conversum* the predicate becomes subject and the subject predicate, the present subject was therefore *latior* than the present predicate therefore it must be restricted e.g. some human beings are learned; therefore some learned are human beings.",["Passage runs from AA 24:584 into 585. OCR 'm gleichem' read as 'in gleichem'; 'großer' as 'größer'; 'Sünde' = sin.","Umfang = domain; Sphäre = sphere; Begriff = concept; Satz/Säze = proposition(s).","Ambiguity kept: 'Es gehört also zu dem, was unter dem Praedicat enthalten ist, ist aber nicht allein unter ihm enthalten' leaves the subject of 'es' unstated, as in the German; the following 'mache ich nun ...' is joined by a comma, as printed, and the semicolon after 'predicate' is from the source.","'omnes honiines' in the OCR read as 'omnes homines'. 'Sterbliche' = 'mortals', 'Gelehrte' = 'learned' (substantivized adjectives)."]],"poelitz-s27-051":["5.) *per judicia contraposita*. Here there takes place such a *metathesis*, i.e., transposition of the *terminorum*, where the quality is also changed, namely a negative proposition The quantity however remains, because only a universal proposition can be contraposed. E.g., All human beings are mortal. What is not mortal is no human being. All universally affirmative judgments allow themselves to be contraposed. They say, where the subject is, there the predicate also is, and where the predicate accordingly is not, there the subject also is not (but that is the contraposition), for if the subject were, then the predicate would also have to be. One can also define them thus: *propositio A contraponitur, si praedicatum ejus mutetur in conceptum negativum, hic fiat subiectum novae propositionis, de quo subjectum prioris A universaliter in hac negatur* [a proposition A is contraposed if its predicate is changed into a negative concept, this becomes the subject of the new proposition, of which the subject of the prior A is universally denied in this one]. In the contraposition the alteration concerns merely the modality. It signifies the necessity of an affirmative concept. — All *consequentiae immediatae* have the constitution that no subsumption can take place except the one that is tautological, that one however is no true subsumption. Remark. Many universally affirmative judgments allow themselves also to be converted *simpliciter*, but that flows not from the form of the same, but from the particular constitution of their matter. E.g., Everything unalterable is necessary, and everything necessary is unalterable.",["OCR corrected silently: findt/Versezung (as in source), eontraposition, ModaHtaet, tavtologiseh, subiectum/subjeetum, vniversaliter, hie→hic, Noth wendigkeit.","'es wird nehmlich ein negativer Saz Die Quantitaet aber bleibt': the text appears to lack something after 'Saz' (or a stop); translated as it stands, nothing supplied.","'die ist aber keine wahre Subsumtion': the construction (die … die) is awkward in the German; kept as it stands.","Bracketed English after the Latin definition is my rendering of the Latin, which is in the original. 'allgemein' (algemein) = 'universal'; Mensch = 'human being'; Qualitaet/Quantitaet/Modalitaet = quality/quantity/modality; bedeuten = 'signify'; Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'; Materie = 'matter'.","Latin kept in italics: per judicia contraposita, metathesis, terminorum, propositio, consequentiae immediatae, simpliciter."]],"poelitz-s28-001":["On Syllogisms.",["Vernunftschluß = 'syllogism' (glossary); plain Schluß is 'inference' or, where paired with *conclusio*, 'conclusion'."]],"poelitz-s28-002":["A syllogism is the truth of a judgment insofar as it is cognized that the subsumption of the same stands under the condition of a universal rule, i.e., I say: that takes place with the cognition, which is a condition under which a universal rule affirms or denies something, now if the condition pertains to the cognition, so also does what the rule says. Caius is not the same as the concept of human being. The rule is, I must show that the concept of human being takes place in Caius, and then I can say he is mortal. First, then, in every inference there is a universal rule, *major propositio*. The proposition that subsumes a cognition under the condition of a rule is *minor*. To subsume means to give to be cognized that this or that is contained under the condition of this rule. The proposition that affirms or denies the predicate of the rule of the subsumed cognition is called conclusion, *conclusio*. We have, then, 2 antecedent propositions, which are called premises. In the syllogism the *conclusio* is given at once, as soon as the premises and the consequence are given. Logic therefore considers 2 premises and of these their truth and the correctness of the consequence from the same. In rejecting a syllogism one must never reject the conclusion first, but rather either the premises or the consequence. The premises constitute the matter, the consequence the form, of the syllogism.",["Schluß appears twice: 'bei jedem Schluß' = 'inference'; 'heist Schluß, conclusio' = 'conclusion'. One English word does not cover both; the glossary lists both.","OCR corrected silently: Mensehen→Menschen, •conclusio (stray mark dropped); 'consequenz' rendered 'consequence' (Folgerung/Consequenz).","'zu erkennen geben' rendered 'to give to be cognized' (idiom: 'to make known'); 'Vorsäze' = 'antecedent propositions', glossed by 'Praemißen' = 'premises'.","'deren ihre Wahrheit' is a colloquial doubled genitive; rendered 'of these their truth'.","'ist gegeben' is singular after a plural subject ('die Praemißen und die consequenz'); kept as 'are given' in English only because English requires it.","'Cajus' kept as 'Caius'; Mensch = 'human being'."]],"poelitz-s28-003":["The inferences cannot be divided *ratione qualitatis*, because every *major* is a rule hence universal, nor *quantitatis* because it is the same whether the conclusion be affirming or denying, nor *modalitatis*, because every conclusion contains a necessity, hence all are apodictic. According to relation they are divided into categorical, in which the *major* is a categorical proposition, into hypothetical, in which the *major* is a hypothetical proposition, or a problematic one, the *minor* is assertoric, into disjunctive, in which the *major* is a disjunctive proposition. The author calls the categorical ones ordinary, the others extraordinary, this however is false, they are all three entirely correct acts of the understanding different from one another. This error of the logicians comes from this, because they believe they would always have to do with categorical inferences. They therefore also err in the definition of the propositions. — A syllogism arises when a *nota remota* pertains to the subject on account of a *nota intermedia*, or is removed, because it contradicts the *nota intermedia*. —",["Schlüße = 'inferences'; conclusion (for conclusion) = 'conclusion'; Vernunftschluß = 'syllogism'.","'quantitatis weil es einerlei ist ob die conclusion affirmirend oder negirend sey': translated as it stands (the reasoning given concerns what is elsewhere quality); not emended.","'oder ein problematischer, der minor ist aßertorisch': the clause is run on in the German and is left so; 'der' could be read as relative ('the minor of which is assertoric'), but is left as it stands.","'Handlungen' = 'acts'; 'Definition' = 'definition'; 'wiederspricht' (Widerspruch) = 'contradicts'.","Latin kept in italics: ratione qualitatis, quantitatis, modalitatis, major, minor, nota remota, nota intermedia."]],"poelitz-s28-004":["The predicate in the conclusion is called *terminus major* because it has a greater sphere than the subject. The subject is therefore called *terminus minor*, and the *nota intermedia* is called *terminus medius*. The logicians say: the first proposition is called major because the predicate of the conclusion is *terminus major* and is contained in it. The 2nd premise is called minor because the subject in the conclusion is *terminus minor* and is contained in it. That the *termini* must be distributed thus is easy to see. I connect the more remote mark (major) with a nearer one (*medius*), major and *medius* therefore come together *in majori*, because I want to predicate the major of the subject in the conclusion, and say that the subject is contained under it. *In minori* I connect the *nota intermedia* (*medius*) with the subject (minor), here therefore *terminus medius* and minor come together, and in the conclusion I say, therefore the remote mark (major) belongs to the thing (minor), here major and minor come together. These terms must apply to all syllogisms, categorical as well as hypothetical and disjunctive. The hypothetical proposition, where the major consists of 2 judgments, would have according to this definition no major and no minor, therefore these definitions fall away and ours are the right ones. For if it did not have *minorem* and *majorem*, it would not be an inference either. In the categorical there are 2 concepts in the major, so there 2 *termini* also take place, major and *medius*, in the hypothetical there are 2 judgments, e.g. if God is just he punishes the persistently evil, here there are 4 *termini*. In the categorical inference there are always 3 concepts. The conclusion belongs to the inference not as a part, but as the sequel, for when the premises are given, the conclusion is also given. The correct consequence of the conclusion or the correctness of the *consequentia* constitutes the form of the inference. Everything depends on the conclusion, and all inferences run out into it. Sometimes the conclusion is true, the premises also, but the inference, namely the consequence, is nevertheless false, and such inferences one must always contest. — An inference of reason is divided into *verum et falsum*. An inference can be false *ratione materiae et formae*. Every *ratiocinium* is *vel purum vel hybridum*, when besides the 3 propositions of the inference of reason a *consequentia immediata* is also mixed in, e.g. no thinking being is composite — (nothing composite is a thinking being) all bodies are composite, therefore no bodies are thinking beings. It is correct as far as content and consequence are concerned, but nevertheless not a proper inference of reason. For a *ratiocinium* it is therefore also required that it be *purum*. We will show below that all inferences of reason in the 3 last figures are *hybrida*, because a *consequentia immediata* lies hidden in them *tacite*. *Ratiocinium formale est Syllogismus*; one otherwise says *verbis expressum*, that is false, without the words one cannot think at all. In common life we always make inferences of reason, only not formally, if they are formal *ratiocinia*, they are called syllogisms. The author speaks further of the formal principles of all inferences, for the matter is the rule and gives for it the proposition of contradiction, which is indeed the *principium* of judgments but not of inferences. There are properly as many formal principles as genera of inferences. The categorical one is, according to the author, the *dictum de omni et nullo*. We assert, however, that it applies to all inferences, for we always infer from the universal to the particular. If one assumes the *dictum de omni, et nullo* thus, namely *quid quid valet de genere et specie, valet quoque de omnibus sub isto genere et specie contentis* — *quidquid non valet de genere et specie* etc. etc. *non valet* etc. etc. Then there is a *principium* of categorical inferences of reason, namely here there is a relation of the subject and predicate. A proposition that is to become the *principium* of the possibility of inferences of reason cannot be proved through any inference of reason. The *dictum de omni et nullo* can, however, be proved, and indeed through the principle that we will adduce — That of the affirming inferences reads thus: *nota notae est nota rei ipsius*, What belongs to the marks belongs to the things themselves. That of the negating: *repugnans notae repugnat rei ipsi*, what does not belong to the mark does not belong to the thing either. E.g.",["Latin technical terms and phrases kept in italics as in the source; 'Praemiße/Praemißen' = premise(s); 'Sphäre' = sphere; 'Merkmal' = mark (*nota*).","'Vernunftschluß' = 'inference of reason' and 'Schluß' = 'inference'; 'Syllogismen' = 'syllogisms'. 'Folge' = 'sequel', 'Folgerung' and 'Consequenz' = 'consequence'.","'in ihr' (after 'terminus major und') is left as ambiguous as the German; the antecedent could be the proposition or the conclusion. Likewise 'unter ihm' and 'in ihr' in the next sentences.","'Sache' and 'Ding' both rendered 'thing'. 'Gattung' = 'genus' ('genera'); 'das material' = 'the matter' (Materie); 'Satz' = 'proposition'; 'Principium/principien' = 'principle'.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'Termhii' → *termini*, 'Schkißes' → 'Schlußes', 'last sich' → 'läßt sich'; 'z. E.' (zum Exempel) = 'e.g.'","The passage breaks off at 'Z. E.'; the examples follow in the next passages."]],"poelitz-s28-005":["Nothing composite is a thinking being",["OCR 'zusaminengeseztes' corrected to 'zusammengeseztes'."]],"poelitz-s28-006":["All bodies are composite",[]],"poelitz-s28-007":["Therefore no body is a thinking being. Thinking being conflicts with the composite, the composite is a mark of the body. Thinking *repugnat notae et rei ipsi*, i.e. the body.",["'wiederstreitet' rendered 'conflicts with'; 'd. h.' = 'i.e.'"]],"poelitz-s28-008":["Everything composite is divisible",[]],"poelitz-s28-009":["All bodies are composite,",[]],"poelitz-s28-010":["Therefore all bodies are divisible.",[]],"poelitz-s28-011":["The divisible does not flow from the body, it is a *nota remota*. It must be attributed to it through the *intermedia*. Divisible is a mark of the composite, composite is a mark of the body, therefore here *nota notae est nota rei ipsius*, i.e. of the body. Now the *dictum de omni et nullo* is derived from this. The *genus* and the *species* are indeed a *nota* of the thing, it is the universal mark, *quicquid competit generi seu speciei est nota notae, competit illis sub eo contentis* i.e. *nota rei ipsius*. These are the *principia* of all categorical inferences and indeed the very highest. In every hypothetical proposition there is indeed an *antecedens* and *consequens* and the *principium* of hypothetical inferences is this: *a positione antecedentis ad positionem consequentis valet consequentia*, this is the *principium* of all affirming inferences and *a remotione consequentis ad remotionem antecedentis*, that is the *principium* of all negating inferences. This flows from the first, for if the *antecedens* were, then the *consequens* would also have to be. In every disjunctive inference the *principium* is *a veritate unius membri divisionis ad remotionem ceterorum* (because they are all opposed) *et a remotione ceterorum ad positionem unius quod manet*. The *positus* of the *termini* of a categorical inference in the premises is a figure. In the 1st premise the *terminus* major is, in the 2nd the minor, but since every judgment must have 2 *termini*, *terminus medius* is added in both. Everything in the figures will therefore depend on the position of the *terminus medius*. It can be in one *loco subjecti*, in the other proposition *loco praedicati*, or in both *loco subjecti* or in both *loco praedicati*:",["OCR slips corrected: 'speis cies' → *species*, 'Ulis' → *illis*, 'vnius' → *unius*, 'terrninus' → *terminus*, 'lsten' → '1st'.","'Merkmal' = 'mark'; 'Saz' = 'proposition'; 'Urteil' = 'judgment'; 'Schluß' = 'inference'; 'Figur' = 'figure'.","'Ulis sub eo contentis' read as *illis sub eo contentis*; 'Positus' left in Latin as printed."]],"poelitz-s28-013":["E.g. No body is a thinking substance. *Medius* composite the inference in the 1st figure is: Nothing composite is a thinking substance All bodies are composite; No body is a thinking substance. E.g. No body is a thinking being *medius* composite. The inference in the 2nd figure is: No thinking being is composite All bodies are composite No body is a thinking being. E.g. Some virtuous are imperfect *medius* they are human beings. The inference in the 3rd figure is:",["OCR slips corrected: 'Kern' → 'Kein', 'zusamniengesezt' and 'ziasammengesezt' → 'zusammengesezt', 'lsten' → '1st'. The line breaks of the syllogisms are lost in the source as given.","'Substanz' = 'substance'; 'Wesen' = 'being'; 'Mensch' = 'human being'; 'unvolkommen' = 'imperfect' (cf. Vollkommenheit = perfection)."]],"poelitz-s28-014":["All human beings are imperfect",[]],"poelitz-s28-015":["Some human beings are virtuous",[]],"poelitz-s28-016":["Some virtuous are imperfect.",[]],"poelitz-s28-017":["E.g. Some of what has a force to act is a spirit *medius*:",["'Einiges was' rendered 'Some of what', as ambiguous as the German. 'Kraft' = 'force'; 'Geist' = 'spirit' (the glossary also allows 'mind')."]],"poelitz-s28-018":["it is a substance. The inference in the 4th figure is this:",["'er eine Substanz ist' is verb-final (subordinate) with no conjunction in the passage as given; the connection to the preceding 'medius:' is not supplied. 'er' rendered 'it', referring to the preceding 'Geist' (masculine)."]],"poelitz-s28-019":["Every spirit is a substance",[]],"poelitz-s28-020":["Every substance has the force to act",[]],"poelitz-s28-021":["Some of what has a force to act is a spirit.",[]],"poelitz-s28-022":["These figures have as their ground that one arrives *per notam intermediam* at the constitution of the thing, under the rule: *nota notae est nota rei ipsius, et nota repugnans repugnat rei ipsi*. The *major* says that this mark belongs to this or that subject, and the *conclusio* says that *nota notae* belongs to the thing itself, which was already shown above.",["Beschaffenheit = constitution; Merkmal = mark (*nota*); Subject, *major*, *conclusio* are Latinate words in the German, kept as such."]],"poelitz-s28-023":["If the *terminus medius* is to have the place of the *notae intermediae*, then it must be the subject in the *propositio major*, the predicate in the *minor*. For in the *major* I see whether the *nota remota* belongs to the *intermediae*, the *intermedia* must therefore be the subject. In the *minor* I compare the *intermedia* with the thing itself, it must therefore be the predicate in the *minor*. In the 2nd figure I did not get *a nota intermedia ad remotam*, but rather the inference had to be so: I say according to the 2nd figure:",["Schluß = inference.","The German breaks off with a colon; the examples follow in the next passages."]],"poelitz-s28-024":["No thinking being is composed, *simpliciter* converted: Nothing composed is a thinking being,",["Wesen = being."]],"poelitz-s28-025":["All bodies are composed,",[]],"poelitz-s28-026":["No body is a thinking being. and so in the remaining figures. This difference of the figures rests on the intermixing of a *consequentiae immediatae*.",["OCR 'WTesen' read as 'Wesen'.","*consequentiae immediatae* is printed in the genitive in the German; kept as it stands."]],"poelitz-s28-027":["All 4 figures are entirely correct, only not logically perfect, they are not *ratiocinia pura* but *hybrida*. —",[]],"poelitz-s28-028":["*Modus* consists of the quantity and quality of the propositions of an inference, which are expressed with the vowel. We want to set down a few: aaa, aae, aai, aao, aea, aee, eaa etc. etc. Of such *modorum* there are 4 classes, because there are 4 vowels, and in this way one gets 64 *modi*, in each figure 16, that can be thought, but only 19 of them are such as agree with the general rules of the inferences of reason, and among these there are still only 10 *utiles*.",["OCR 'vtiles' read as 'utiles'.","Preserved ambiguity: 'die sich denken laßen' can attach to 'modi' or to 'figure' (or the whole count).","Vernunftschlüße = inferences of reason; Schluß = inference; Saz = proposition.","In the German 'die' before 'mit den algemeinen Regeln' is relative ('of which are those that agree'); rendered closely, the construction is somewhat loose."]],"poelitz-s29-001":["Rules of the categorical inferences.",[]],"poelitz-s29-002":["1.) The author says: in every regular syllogism there must be no more than 3 *termini*, for I am to combine 2 concepts through a *nota intermedia*. That lies in the definition of syllogisms. By regular syllogisms the author and all others understand the categorical syllogism, to which they want to reduce everything, so that they can carry on their play of figures, which does not work with the hypothetical and disjunctive.",["OCR 'interlsmediam' read as 'intermediam'.","'Definition' in the German (not Erklärung); rendered 'definition'.","verbinden = combine; Begriff = concept."]],"poelitz-s29-003":["2.) The *terminus medius* must not be in the conclusion. This oversight does not so easily take place, because it is too obvious.",["'conclusion' is the German's own (Latinate) word; the Latin *conclusio* appears elsewhere."]],"poelitz-s30-001":["Rules of the inferences of reason.",["Vernunftschlüße = inferences of reason."]],"poelitz-s30-002":["1.) *ex puris negativis nihil sequitur*. For if everything is negating, then the subsumption must nevertheless be affirming, for it says indeed that something stands under the condition of the rule — or if something was negating, then there was no subsumption.",["verneinend = negating; affirmirend = affirming; Bedingung = condition.","The past tense 'war' is kept as in the German."]],"poelitz-s30-003":["2.) *ex puris particularibus nihil sequitur*. Then again there was no rule, for every rule must be universal.",["Past tense 'war' kept."]],"poelitz-s30-004":["3.) *conclusio sequitur partem debiliorem*. *pars debilior* is negative and particular. If a proposition is negative or particular, then the conclusion is also negative or particular. For if a proposition is particular, then the subsumption is also particular, and if I say: some is contained only under the rule, then I can also say in the conclusion that the predicate of the rule belongs to some. If the rule is negative, then the conclusion must also be negative, for the rule says: of everything that stands under the condition of the rule, this or that must be denied, so the conclusion must also deny the predicate, because the subsumption says, this stands under the condition of the rule.",["The German lacks a full stop after 'zukomme'; the sentence division here follows the capital 'Wenn'.","'Saz' = proposition; verneinen = deny / negative (verneinend = negating)."]],"poelitz-s30-005":["The rule of the 1st figure is: *major sit universalis*, because it is a rule, *minor affirmans* because it is the subsumption. The first figure can have conclusions of every quantity and quality, but in all the remaining figures there are only conclusions of a certain kind. The rule of the 2nd figure is: *major sit universalis, conclusio negativa*. The rule of the 3rd figure is *major sit universalis, conclusio particularis*. Of the 4th figure, *major sit universalis, conclusio* either *negativa* or *particularis*, never universally affirming. These limitations already indicate that in the inferences there are certain conditions under which they can be brought into the first figure. —",["OCR 'vniversalis' read as 'universalis'; 'Emschrenkungen' read as 'Einschränkungen' (limitations; Schranke = limitation).","bejahend = affirming (bejahen = affirm)."]],"poelitz-s30-006":["All logicians admit that the inference in the 4th figure is very unnatural because 2 *consequentiae immediatae* must be intermixed. The 3 figures are therefore only artifices to entangle something, but they nevertheless infer correctly and are *ratiocinia hybrida*. —",["'Die 3 Figuren' kept literally; it is unclear whether the 3 refers to the three non-first figures or to a misreading of the number (possibly 4)."]],"poelitz-s30-007":["The logicians, in order to make possible the reduction of the others to the first figure, have devised barbarous words, where the vowels indicate the quantity and quality, but the consonants serve partly for euphony, but at the same time they also indicate, in all figures except the first, through which *metathesis* the reduction is possible. *S. vult simpliciter verti*: the proposition where S. occurs must be converted *simpliciter*, *P. vero per accidens*. *M. transponi*, i.e. the *major* must be made into the *minor*, C. must be contraposed. The whole verse of it is called thus:",["'zeigen ... an' and the grammatical subject of the second 'aber zugleich' clause are kept as loose as in the German.","Saz = proposition; Quantitaet = quantity; Qualitaet = quality."]],"poelitz-s30-008":["*S vult simpliciter verti*",[]],"poelitz-s30-009":["*P vero per accid.*",[]],"poelitz-s30-010":["*M vult transponi*",[]],"poelitz-s30-011":["*C per impossibile duci.*",["OCR/orthography 'impoßibile' given as 'impossibile'."]],"poelitz-s30-012":["The verse in which the vowels indicate the quantity and quality is called thus:",[]],"poelitz-s30-013":["*Asserit A negat E, sed universaliter ambo*",["OCR/orthography 'Aßerit' given as 'Asserit'; 'vniversaliter' as 'universaliter'."]],"poelitz-s30-014":["*Asserit I, negat O, sed particulariter ambo*. In the first figure there are: bArbArA, cELArEnt, dArII, fErIO. In the second figure: cEsArE, cAmEstrEs, fEstInO, bArOccO. In the third figure: dArAptI, fElAptOn, disAmis, dAtIsI, bOcArdO, fErIsOn. In the fourth figure: cAlEmEs, bAmAlIp, dImAtIs, fEsApO, frEsIsOn.",["The mnemonic words are heavily garbled by OCR (e.g. 'dArll', 'fErlO', 'festlnO', 'bAmAllp', 'dlmAtls', 'frEsIsOm', '0' for 'O'); I have restored the standard forms with the vowels capitalized, as the passage's sense requires."]],"poelitz-s30-015":["There are 19 of these, and that many are only possible in the 4 figures. In reading one must always draw the consonants to the preceding vowel, e.g. cAm-Estr-Es. — C wants to be contraposed, M transposed, the *minor* shall be converted *simpliciter*, and the conclusion also. A hypothetical inference is one whose *major* is a hypothetical proposition, which has 2 members, an *antecedens* which is the ground and a *consequens* which is the sequel, and here one infers *a positione antecedentis ad positionem consequentis*, which is *modus ponens*, and *a remotione consequentis ad remotionem antecedentis*, which is *modus tollens*, e.g. if God is just, then he punishes the evil, *atqui, verum est prius ergo posterius*, or the soul is composed, therefore it is corporeal, *atqui falsum est posterius ergo prius*. In disjunctive inferences the *major* is a disjunctive proposition, it must therefore also have *membra disjunctionis*. *Per modum ponentem* I infer, *unum membrum est verum igitur falsa cetera*; and *per modum tollentem*, *omnia membra sunt falsa igitur verum est unum quod manet*. E.g. The soul is simple or composed, *atqui* it is simple, therefore not composed — or it is not composed, therefore it is simple. —",["Grund = ground; Folge = sequel; Glied = member.","OCR 'vnum' read as 'unum'; 'tolle ntem' as 'tollentem'.","einfach = simple; zusammengesezt = composed; körperlich = corporeal."]],"poelitz-s30-016":["The dilemma consists of a disjunctive hypothetical syllogism. In every hypothetical syllogism the *major* is a hypothetical proposition, but the *consequens* a dilemma. It is a disjunctive judgment, e.g. if this world is not the best, then God has been unable or unwilling to create a better one, now I refute the *consequens* *per omnia membra*: God has been able to create a better world, for he is almighty, and also willing, for he is infinitely good, therefore the world is not the best. One infers here *a remotione consequentis ad negationem antecedentis*. The ancients made very much of it, and called it *cornutum*. They knew how to drive their opponent into a corner thereby, that they recited everything to which he could turn, and also refuted everything for him. They showed him many difficulties, with every opinion that he adopted. But it is a sophistical trick not to refute propositions straightaway, but only to show difficulties. That works with many, indeed with most things. If I want to call everything false where difficulties are found, then it is an easy game to reject everything. It is indeed good to show the impossibility of the opposite, but here there is nevertheless something blinding, because one holds the incomprehensibility of the opposite for the impossibility of the same, and it is always the trick of the sophist to prove *apagogice*. These dilemmas have much that is captious about them, although they infer correctly. They can be used to defend true propositions, but also to attack true propositions through difficulties that one raises against them. —",["Vernunftschluß = syllogism; schließen = infer; Saz = proposition; Urteil = judgment; Meinung = opinion; Unbegreiflichkeit = incomprehensibility (begreifen = comprehend).","The sentence 'In jedem hypothetischen Vernunftschluß ist major ... das consequens aber ein Dilemma' is kept as it stands; it may be garbled in the transcript.","'hat keine beßere schaffen können oder wollen' is rendered 'has been unable or unwilling to create a better one'; the German has the infinitives können/wollen without a negative attached to them.","'verblendendes' = 'blinding'; 'Verfängliches' = 'captious' (literally, something that catches or ensnares).","'Kunstgrif' (also spelled Kunstgriff) = 'trick'; 'wiederlegen' = 'refute' (modern widerlegen)."]],"poelitz-s30-017":["One divides syllogisms further into *cryptica* and *formalia*. A hidden inference is one that contains everything as to matter, where the form, however, is not properly expressed, although it is thought. One also calls this inferring *per medios terminos*, e.g. all bodies are divisible, because they are composed. No human being infers in secret otherwise than formally, for without that we could not infer at all, because otherwise there would be no interconnection. An *enthymema* is such a hidden inference, where one of the premises is missing, which one retains in thought. —",["Teilen = divide (Teilung/Einteilung = division); Materie = matter; Zusammenhang = interconnection.","'vorsäzen' (Vorsätze) is rendered 'premises'; the glossary's Obersatz/Untersatz does not appear here.","'der Materie nach allesenthält' is read as 'alles enthält' (OCR/spelling run-together)."]],"poelitz-s30-018":["We now have still 2 kinds of inferences that properly do not belong in logic, but cannot conveniently be placed elsewhere, namely the inference *per inductionem*, where we infer: what pertains to many things that belong to a genus pertains to all the rest that belong to this genus. This is indeed not quite correctly inferred, for we cannot infer from many to all, but from all to many; but it must be done so, because otherwise we have no universal judgments from experience. Thus we must properly say: of that which pertains to many things that belong to a genus, we can presume that it pertains to all other things that belong to the same genus. But one must well note that it is only an empirical certainty, which is very different from the rational. The 2nd kind of inferences is that *per analogiam*, when 2 or more things of one genus agree in so many marks as I have been able to discover: so I infer that they will also agree in all other marks, which I do not know. Without these two inferences we could get no extended cognition of experience, for without them we could have no universal propositions, hence make no inferences. We could often deceive ourselves here, but we still cannot avoid them. But we must use them with great caution. — Inferences from examples are nothing at all, e.g. humans have sinned, therefore the angels too may well have sinned, because both belong under the concept of rational beings. — There can be a combination among judgments that have the *speciem* of a syllogism, and the form can nevertheless be false, then it is called *fallacia*. Insofar as one thereby deceives oneself it is *paralogismus*, insofar as one deceives others with intention it is *sophisma*. The ancients and the Stoics occupied themselves greatly with the art of making such sophisms. Hence various ones have arisen, e.g. *sophisma figurae dictionis*, where the *terminus medius* is taken in different meanings, e.g. a philosopher is a genus of scholars, Leibniz is a philosopher, therefore he is a genus of scholars. *Fallacia a dicto secundum quid ad dictum simpliciter.* Whoever says you are an animal speaks the truth, *atqui* he who says you are an ass says you are an animal, *Ergo* he who says you are an ass speaks the truth. In part he speaks the truth because the ass has in common with the human being that it is an animal. Here one can answer: *concedo totum argumentum*. Only one must make some restrictions. *Sophisma heterozeteseos*, when one does not answer the question that one is to answer, but another. *Sophisma ignorationis Elenchi*, when one does not refute what one is to refute, namely what the other means, but what one believes that the other means. Thus one refutes one's own delusion about the other's propositions. It is properly called a missing question of dispute, for *Elenchus* is the distinct representation of the content of a matter. The Megarian sect made the greatest weight of it. Its originator was Euclides of Megara. One they called the liar, from the *terminus medius*. If you say you lie, then you speak the truth in that you lie, *atqui* you say you lie, therefore you speak the truth in that you lie.1) Its incorrectness is easy to see, namely in the *major* something is omitted, etc. in that (you say) you lie. — *Obullatus*, it was called thus: do you know your father? Yes — Now I present him to you covered; do you still know him? No — So you know your father, and also do not know him. *Acervus*, *Calvus*, *Cornutus*. What you have not lost, you still have, *atqui* the horns you have not lost, so you still have them. — *Sorites* is called a chain of syllogisms that are subordinated. A chain of syllogisms where the *conclusio* of the one becomes the *praemisse* of the other: so it is *ratiocinatio polysyllogistica*. Here I can doubly\n\n1) It reads thus: If you say you lie and in saying this speak the truth",["The passage breaks off in mid-sentence ('Hier kann ich doppelt') and ends with the opening words of the editors' footnote 1), which continues in the next passage; both are translated as they stand.","Stray OCR marks ('•', '■') were dropped. Corrected silently: Vernnnftschhißes, figurac, seeundum, Emschrenkungen (Einschränkungen), beantAvortet; 'Elenchi', 'heterozeteseos', 'ignorationis' kept as printed.","'Obullatus' is kept as printed; the reading is doubtful (the name of the fallacy, probably the 'veiled' one, is garbled in the OCR/transcript).","'Gattung' = genus; 'Art' = kind; Merkmal = mark; kennen = know; Erkenntniß = cognition; 'Erfahrungs Erkenntniß' is rendered 'cognition of experience' (compound split in the source).","'betriegen' (sich betriegen) and 'hintergehen' are both rendered 'deceive'; 'Absicht' = intention; 'Bedeutung' = meaning; 'Weltweiser' = 'philosopher' (cf. Weltweisheit = philosophy); 'Sache' = 'matter' (not Materie); 'Einschränkungen' = 'restrictions'; 'praesumiren' = 'presume'.","'denn heist es fallacia': 'denn' is rendered 'then', as it stands; 'speciem' is kept in Latin (accusative form as printed).","'Wahn' = 'delusion'; 'Praemiße' (Praemisse) retained in Latin form where Kant writes the Latin term; 'verfehlende Streitfrage' = 'missing question of dispute' (literal)."]],"poelitz-s30-019":["speak, then you speak the truth in that you lie — it should read: in that you",["This is the continuation of footnote 1) begun at the end of the preceding passage; it breaks off in mid-sentence and is continued in the next passage."]],"poelitz-s30-020":["say you lie, and then it would be correct to infer either down to the sequels, then they are *episyllogismi*, or up to the grounds, then they are *prosyllogismi*, when namely the premise of the one becomes the *conclusio* of the other, e.g. everything simple is indivisible, the soul is simple, therefore it is indivisible — Everything that thinks is simple, the soul thinks, therefore it is simple. *Per episyllogismos* I infer: Everything simple is indivisible, the soul is simple *ergo* etc. etc. Everything indivisible is unchangeable *atqui* etc. etc. *ergo* etc. In disputations the opponent must infer *per prosyllogismos*. — If from a series of *episyllogismen* the *major* is omitted, then it is *sorites*. One leaves out here one of the premises for the sake of brevity. If it consists of categorical propositions, then it is *sorites categoricus*. There are in the very same way also hypothetical",["The opening words ('sagst du lügst und dann wäre es richtig') may belong to the end of footnote 1) and 'schließen' to the main sentence 'Hier kann ich doppelt ... schließen'; the line division is unclear in the OCR, so the text is translated as it stands.","'Folgen' = 'sequels' (Folge); 'Gründe' = 'grounds'; 'Praemiße' = 'premise'; 'unteilbar' = 'indivisible'; 'unveränderlich' = 'unchangeable'; 'episyllogismen' kept as printed.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('hypothetische') and is left uncompleted."]],"poelitz-s30-021":["*Saltus in probando* is the combination of a premise with the conclusion where the other is omitted: *legitimus*, if everyone can at once insert the missing one, *illegitimus* if the subsumption, for example, is not clear. Here I connect *notam remotam* with a thing without *intermedia*. — *Petitio principii* is the assumption of a proposition as ground of proof as immediately certain, which nevertheless still needs a proof. —",["Praemiße = premise; Verbindung = combination, verknüpfe = connect (Verknüpfung); Sache and Ding would both be 'thing' (here Sache).","Beweisgrund rendered 'ground of proof'; Annehmung = assumption; 'z. E.' (zum Exempel) = 'for example'."]],"poelitz-s30-022":["*Circulus in probando*, when I lay the proposition that I wanted to prove at the ground of its own proof. The *circulus* is often hard to discover, and where the proofs are difficult it is most frequent. A proof can prove too much, also too little. It proves too much if it also goes to what is false; if it proves too little, then it proves a part of the truth, or a part of what is to be proved, it is therefore not to be rejected.",["'zum Grunde legen' kept literal: 'lay at the ground of'; Beweis = proof, Satz = proposition, Teil = part."]],"poelitz-s30-023":["We now want to say something more about the topics that Aristotle set going. If someone wants to think about a thing, he must know to which science it belongs. In this there are principal parts into which this or that belongs as into its place. The principal parts are called *loci*, and the regular division of all themata of the science under which every cognition gets its position is called topics. Such a true topics, to which however the mere enumeration of the themata does not belong, would be an incomparable guidance for reflection in sciences. For example: to virtue I can give a political place, as a means to attain happiness, but also a moral place. One can also define place through every determination of a cognition in a possible system, then it is called *locus scientificus*, and the art of indicating the place of the manifold cognitions in a possible system is the *topica*. For example, if I want to speak of philosophy, I first think of the etymology of the word, that is *locus grammaticus*, of the definition, that is *locus logicus*. *Locus metaphysicus* is, for example, the title of substance, *nexu effectivo*; *locus topicus* is therefore a tautology. —",["Topik = topics; Thematen = 'themata' (Greek plural kept as in the German); Ort and Plaz both 'place'; Stelle = 'position'.","Nachdenken rendered 'think about' / 'reflection' (nachdenken); Anleitung = 'guidance'; Wißenschaft = science, Erkenntniß = cognition, Bestimmung = determination, Mittel = means.","'Definition' (definiren/Definition) is distinct from Erklärung in the glossary; here the German has definiren, Definition, rendered 'define', 'definition'."]],"poelitz-s30-024":["We come now to the practical part of logic, which is supposed to contain the rules for giving to every cognition the form of a science. It cannot indicate the sources of cognition, for it abstracts from all content, it has to do merely with the scholastic form of science. Science is a system of cognitions where always the idea of the whole lies at the ground, which determines the division. We will speak of the scholastic and popular form of science, because there can be science without scholastic form of the system. The way of the combination of thoughts is either manner, i.e. *modus aestheticus*, or method, i.e. *modus logicus*. The former is also called popularity and is a great art. But one will never attain to it except if one has method in command, without which it is merely rhapsody. Method concerns the scientific in thinking-through, manner concerns the presentation. To think without any method is called tumultuary. If one has thought according to a method but does not present it so, then it is rhapsodistic. The former betrays little order in the head of the one thinking. If the propositions hang together in order, yet so that one cannot easily understand the transition of one proposition to another, then that is aphoristic, but if it is thought according to a method, expressed in the presentation, and the transition from one proposition to another is distinct, then that cognition is systematic. — Popularity requires everything methodical like scholastic perfection, only it demands that the scholastic arrangements and means to the perfection be concealed, and that one calls natural. The author speaks further of the mathematical and philosophical way of teaching because previously it was the fashion to bring the mathematical way of teaching into the philosophical and conversely. — To observe a method is something other than to present methodically. *Methodus syllogistica* is a presentation through sheer syllogisms, i.e. *ratio formalis* — Way of cognition is the way one arrives at the combination of the manifold. — Style is the way to communicate a manifold of cognitions to others and to make it graspable. We need method for thinking and style for presentation. The way of presentation is important, but the way of thinking still more important. Method belongs to logical, style to aesthetic perfection. Well-spokenness concerns merely the language with regard to the grammatical rules: eloquence is the way to produce persuasion instead of conviction. Style *in genere* is the way to express something, appropriate to the method of thinking, and insofar logical perfection is promoted. All method could be divided into the popular and scientific. If something is composed according to the latter, then it is called methodical κατ᾽ ἐξοχήν, and that serves the sciences to great advantage. The popular method does not have the intention of promoting the sciences but interest. The popular presentation is quite different from the popular method. The presentation can be popular, the method scholastic. One need only, for example, begin from principles, definitions, etc. etc., just as Gottsched, since he is yet so easy that even ladies can read and understand him. The French, who have come furthest in the popular method, begin right away with the interesting. In the scholastic method I must begin from the first elements. The popular and scholastic method differ therefore according to kind, not the presentation, that is a gift of nature.",["OCR 'Korr 'e^oyjiv' is garbled; read as κατ᾽ ἐξοχήν, a conjecture.","Art rendered 'way' (Art der Verbindung, Art des Vortrags, Erkenntniß Art), but 'kind' in 'der Art nach'; Lehrart = 'way of teaching'.","'Erkenntniß Art' (as printed, two words) rendered 'Way of cognition'; 'so ist das Erkenntniß systematisch' could also read 'then that is systematic cognition'.","Wolredenheit = 'well-spokenness' and Beredsamkeit = 'eloquence'; faßlich = 'graspable'; Ueberredung = persuasion, Ueberzeugung = conviction; Satz = proposition, Grundsatz = principle, deutlich = distinct, Vollkommenheit = perfection.","'nicht dem Vortrag' kept elliptical; it may be read 'not [in] the presentation' or 'not [according to] the presentation'.","OCR 'Intereße', 'Thematen' etc. kept as in the text; 'etc. etc.' and 'Gottsched' as printed (no verb in the German after 'so wie Gottsched')."]],"poelitz-s30-025":["Of the way of teaching of reason- and historical cognition. Since the first rests on concepts, the latter on *facta*, the method must also be different in each. Meanwhile history, if it is to be a science, must nevertheless be a system, and as a system it needs a method that rests on rules of reason. Every method must indeed be in accordance with reason, but *ratione* of the content they are nevertheless different. The method of cognition of reason is either scientific or scholastic, where the form of science shines forth distinctly, or popular, where it does not shine forth but is covered, as it were, with a garment. — One no longer notices the scientific form now in philosophy because it is too contrary to the present taste. But the metaphysical of the science must be presented scientifically. The method is further divided into the analytic and synthetic. The first begins from the *principiata* and goes to the *principia*. The synthetic goes from the *principia* to the sequels, or from the simple to the composite. The analytic method one could also call the regressive and the synthetic the progressive. With the popular method the analytic procedure is better, but the synthetic is the most perfect and a system can be possible only through it. In the analytic method, one says, we ascend. It is also otherwise called the method of inventing. — The author distinguishes art and science. Art comes from being able and science from knowing. I can know to make something without being able to it, and conversely. There are many arts that are far removed from science, and it is also not feasible to make them into it, e.g. painting and music. Art can again be divided into bread-art and free art. It is bread-art insofar as it is esteemed not on account of its inner worth, but as a means to earn bread. Free arts are those that are esteemed on account of their inner worth, and if one uses them for bread-arts, they are degraded (*pretium internum est dignitas*), e.g. poetry and eloquence. The use of an in itself free art as bread-art is indeed permitted. —",["Lehrart = 'way of teaching' (Art = way); Methode = 'method'; Manier is kept distinct elsewhere as 'manner'.","Vernunft- und historischen Erkenntnißes: 'reason- and historical cognition' keeps the German hyphenated compound; the genitive 'des ... Erkenntnißes' is left as it stands.","'begriffen' (lowercase, as OCR) is read as 'Begriffen' = concepts. 'Wißenschaf t', 'Systehm', 'seyjj' (= seyn) silently corrected.","Folgen = 'sequels' (glossary Folge), not 'consequences'; Philosophie = 'philosophy' (the glossary's Weltweisheit is also 'philosophy').","'etwas wißen zu machen ohne es zu können' is syntactically odd; kept literally ('know to make ... without being able to it'). 'sie dazu zu machen' = 'to make them into it' (antecedent of 'dazu' left open).","'es geht nicht an' rendered 'is not feasible'; 'geschäzt' = 'esteemed'; 'an sich' = 'in itself'; 'z. E.' = 'e.g.'.","'— -' before 'Der Autor' is an OCR/dash artifact; rendered as a single dash."]],"poelitz-s30-026":["Art and science",["Heading; Kunst = 'art', Wißenschaft = 'science'."]],"poelitz-s30-027":["Knowing and being able are different perfections. Practice belongs to art. A science is a system of cognitions. Science is either cognition of reason or erudition. Erudition is itself science of the cognitions of reason although it is not cognition of reason. Both, however, are distinguished from art. The beautiful mind, orator, poet are not philosophers, not scholars, not mathematicians but artists, *vid.* pag. 28.",["'S}rstem' silently corrected to 'System'.","Vernunfterkenntniß = 'cognition of reason'; Gelehrsamkeit = 'erudition'; Gelehrte = 'scholars'.","'Der schöne Geist': Geist = 'mind' (glossary 'mind; spirit').","'Wißen und können' rendered as infinitives 'knowing and being able'; Uebung = 'practice'; 'vid. pag. 28' left as in the source."]],"poelitz-s31-001":["Of the way of teaching",["Heading; Lehrart = 'way of teaching'."]],"poelitz-s31-002":["Furthermore there is yet a division of the way of teaching into artificial and natural. Every way of teaching is, however, artificial, if by artificial is understood that which happens according to principles reflected on beforehand. An artificial way of teaching is therefore a tautology. The natural can be distinguished from the contrived. Contrived is that in which an art is shown that is contrary to the constitution and the end of the cognition, natural is that which is purposive. — Syllogistic method is one where one presents the science in a chain of inferences. — Tabular method is that where one shows an already finished edifice of doctrine in its whole interconnection. The way of teaching of a cognition in a system is either acroamatic if I alone teach or erotematic if I ask. This can again be divided into the dialogical or Socratic and into the catechetical. With the Socratic or dialogical the one teaching and the one learning ask, and also answer each other alternately. Of him who asks it is presupposed that he does not know the thing, but wants to learn it from him whom he asks. With the catechetical method the one answering is the learner, thus just the reverse. If it is regarded merely with respect to the historical, then this way of teaching is quite good, for then it is called *examen* and is merely an investigation whether the apprentice has grasped the thing of memory well. *Modus catecheticus* is more manner than method, for it is only the way in which things of memory can be interrogated, not how cognitions of reason can be imparted, there the dialogical method is the best. — The one teaching is there also teacher as in the catechetical but it also seems that the pupil himself is teacher. The end is otherwise not attained, that the apprentice's thoughts are developed, but the teacher has put them in. Also in religious instruction, where cognitions of reason are the most important, the catechetical method is wholly unusable, for by the apprentice's reciting after the questions and answers, he does not get more light in religion, but it thereby becomes memory-work, which he forgets in a short time. Here the proper dialogical or Socratic method must be observed. Socrates asks his pupils, and it seems as if he knew nothing at all. He calls himself the midwife of the thoughts of his pupils, and says he learns much from the fact that he asks others, and this is also actually so, through asking one is brought to much that one knows, and yet previously did not believe to have in one's cognition. This asking is very artificial. The pupil learns from the source of his reason and such cognitions are indelible. —",["künstlich = 'artificial' (cf. Kunst = 'art'); gekünstelt = 'contrived'; zweckmäßig = 'purposive'; Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'; Zweck = 'end'.","A period is supplied after 'geschieht' (missing in the OCR text).","Lehrling = 'apprentice' and Schüler = 'pupil'; Sache = 'thing' (Ding is also 'thing'); Gedächtnißsache = 'thing of memory'.","'denn heist es examen': 'denn' here read as 'then' (dann); 'for' is also possible.","'Die kann wieder eingeteilt werden': 'Die' left as 'This' (antecedent not specified in German). 'der lehrende und lernende' rendered 'the one teaching and the one learning'.","'zuwieder' = zuwider; 'gerad' = 'just'; 'vorausgesezt' = 'presupposed'; 'würklich' = 'actually'; 'Zusammenhang' = 'interconnection'.","Latin kept in italics: *examen*, *Modus catecheticus*; 'acroamatisch', 'erotematisch' rendered as cognates."]],"poelitz-s31-003":["The author also speaks of the geographical and chronological way of teaching. The former presents the things of the world according to their combination in space, the latter according to their combination in time. The former must lie at the ground of the latter. One must distinguish doctrine and discipline. Every institution can be negative, i.e. preserve from errors, then it is discipline, or positive, i.e. extend cognitions, then it is doctrine. — Critique is that which precedes before I present cognitions as doctrine or discipline. It is the researching of the sources from which the cognition springs, but that is for the teacher. Science, says the author, is demonstrated discipline, that is false, it can be doctrine, and can be science without demonstration, as e.g. history. —",["'hegen' read as 'liegen' (OCR); 'seynd.h.' read as 'seyn d.h.'.","Verbindung = 'combination'; Grund = 'ground'; Raum = 'space'; Zeit = 'time'; Welt = 'world'.","'doctrin' / 'disciplin' rendered 'doctrine' / 'discipline' (Lehre = doctrine); 'denn' = 'then' (dann), as in the preceding passage.","Wißenschaft = 'science'; Historie = 'history'; Kritik = 'Critique' (here 'critique'); Erkenntniß = 'cognition'; 'Irtümer' = 'errors'."]],"poelitz-s31-004":["To meditate does not mean to think something over, for that happens tumultuously, but to think after, that is methodical. In meditating one must make preliminary investigations, write them down for oneself and then bring that into order, which constitutes the meditating. It must accompany all reading and learning. —",["'Es muß alles lesen und lernen begleiten': read as 'alles Lesen und Lernen' (objects of 'begleiten'); the grammar of the transcript is otherwise ambiguous.","'denn' in 'und denn das in Ordnung bringen' read as 'then'; 'nachdenken' rendered 'to think after' (literal)."]],"poelitz-s31-005":["*Termini familiares* or current expressions that one believes to understand on account of frequent use, where it nevertheless becomes difficult to declare oneself about them, e.g. fate, luck, etc. *Logodaedalus* is one who contrives new words in order to dispatch the other with them instead of the things. He has the name from Daedalus, a renowned builder in Crete. One calls artificial works: *opera daedala*. — *Terminus vagus* is one that has no determinate meaning, *inanis* one that has no meaning. —",["'Termini familiäres' in the text; read as Latin *termini familiares* (probable OCR/transcript slip).","'sich drüber zu erklären' = 'to declare oneself about them' (erklären also 'explain; define').","Ausdruck = 'expression'; Gebrauch = 'use'; Bedeutung = 'meaning'; bestimmt = 'determinate'; Sachen = 'things'.","Schiksal = 'fate'; Glük = 'luck' (could also be 'fortune')."]],"poelitz-s31-006":["Of words. That does not belong in logic at all. If it comes down to how words are to be used in conformity with thoughts: then a general characteristic of languages would have to be made. It can only show the difference of the *terminorum technicorum* and *vulgarium*. *Synonyma* are 2 words, if they have one meaning. In a language, however, one does not find wholly synonymous words. *Homonyma* when a word has different meanings. — We may not dare to use antiquated words again, or to introduce new ones, or else there would have to be a proposal for a new idea for which there is yet no word. A tropical expression is a likeness, of which one makes use to express the thing, e.g. enlightenment. — *Propositio indefinita* is that where the sign of quantity is not added, and one always considers it as general. —",["Wörter and Worte both 'words'.","Gleichnis = 'likeness' (could be 'simile'); 'Erleuchtung' = 'enlightenment' (could be 'illumination').","'Gedanken gemäß' = 'in conformity with thoughts'; 'müste' = 'would have to'; Idee = 'idea'; Zeichen = 'sign'; algemein = 'general'.","'ankömmt', 'findt', 'betracht' are older spellings (= ankommt, findet, betrachtet); 'Quantitaet' = quantity."]],"poelitz-s31-007":["As for style of writing, it too does not belong in logic. In philosophy one must not hunt for *lepores*, but the essential property is simplicity and punctual appropriateness of expression. Barbarisms and everything that conflicts with correctness of language can be avoided. — Cognition of reason has nothing to do with style except as regards purity. — Speech is aesthetic art applied to cognitions that are at the same time supposed to have logical perfection. Here it is to be noted: eloquence [Beredheit], i.e., multitude of expressions, fluency [Wolredenheit] concerns style, speaking in a way appropriate to the object, and oratory [Beredsamkeit], the art of persuading someone, which actually ought not to be. We ought to convince, but not persuade. In the didactic method *condescendence* and *coascendence* are to be observed. The teacher must consider himself as in the middle, and to those who have more capacities and greater ends, raise himself, to others, who do not have this, lower himself. It is a great art to keep to the middle road, and very few possess this. Those who cannot do it must apply themselves more to *coascendence* than to *condescendence*. In disputes there is always a *status controversiae*, and there no *ignoratio elenchi* may take place. The best would be to abolish all disputes and, when two were at odds, to take pains in common to love all misunderstanding, and to investigate the truth. —",["*lepores*: Latin kept as in the German (\"charms, wit\" not rendered in text). *condescendenz*, *coascendenz*, *status controversiae*, *ignoratio Elenchi* are Latinate terms left in Latin.","Erkenntniß = cognition; Vernunft Erkenntniß = \"cognition of reason\" (compound kept literal). Vollkommenheit = perfection; Fähigkeiten = capacities; Endzwecke = ends; Ausdruck = expression; Gegenstand = object.","Beredheit, Wolredenheit, Beredsamkeit: three distinct German words; the English equivalents (eloquence, fluency, oratory) are given with the German in brackets, the bracketed German being my insertion. Überreden/überzeugen = persuade/convince (Überredung/Überzeugung in the glossary).","\"zu lieben\" in \"alles Mißverständnis zu lieben\" is translated as it stands; it is probably an error (perhaps for \"zu heben\" or similar) in the transcript or OCR, but it is not emended.","\"dem Gegenstande angemeßen zu reden\" is kept in its position; whether it modifies Wolredenheit or Stil is left as ambiguous as in the German.","\"sich erheben\" / \"sich herablaßen\" are the infinitives governed by \"muß\"; the construction \"zu solchen ... sich erheben\" is kept literal."]],"poelitz-s31-008":["Logomachy is a dispute about words, where one disputes, and in the end is in agreement in the matter, only with regard to the expressions it has not been so. Professor Kant accepts no logomachies, there is always a difference in the things, only imperceptible. One can argue against persons and things, the first are only in personal dispute and do not hold for all human beings. — *Argumenta κατ' ἄνθρωπον* are: when I accept the opinion of the opponent, but not as true, in order to prove my opinion from it. Strictly, all arguments should be *argumenta κατ' ἀλήθειαν*, where I lay at the ground something that I actually hold to be true. *Ad verecundiam*: when the opponent says: You will surely not be wiser than Leibniz, Wolff, etc. etc. — One can retort someone's attacks through evil consequences that one draws from his opinions, that is called *argumentum ab odio*. —",["The Greek in the source is badly garbled by OCR (\"koct 'dvOpcoiTOV\", \"Kcrr'aAr)6sioa;\"); I have read these as κατ' ἄνθρωπον and κατ' ἀλήθειαν, the standard forms; the readings are conjectural.","\"kerne\" read as \"keine\"; \"wieder\" (= wider) rendered \"against\"; \"würklich\" = actually.","Ansehung = regard; Meinung = opinion; Grund = ground (\"zum Grunde lege\" = \"lay at the ground\", kept literal); Folgen = consequences (glossary: Folge = sequel; here plural of Folge in the sense of consequences, rendered \"consequences\").","\"die ersten\" (the first) is left without resolving whether it refers to the arguments against persons; \"in the matter\" renders \"in der Sache\"; \"Sachen\" = things (Ding is a different word)."]],"poelitz-s31-009":["On the composing of writings, this does not belong in logic. — He who makes one out of many books is called a *compilator* and he is useful, because often in many books there is little information. He who makes a small one out of a book is called an *epitomator*. It is best when the author himself epitomizes.",["Kenntniß = information (glossary: Kenntnis = acquaintance; information; knowledge); \"nüzlieh\" read as \"nützlich\" (OCR slip).","*compilator*, *epitomator* kept in Latin; *epitomirt* rendered \"epitomizes\"."]]};
